(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["prasna"]={"slug":"prasna","name":"Praśna","deva":"प्रश्न","note":"","intro":["Sanskrit text courtesy of sanskritdocuments.org (Pippalāda recension, as preserved in Śaṅkara's bhāṣya). English glosses cross-checked against Śaṅkara's Praśna-bhāṣya (Gambhirananda tr., Advaita Ashrama, 1957), Sharvananda (Ramakrishna Math, 1923), Sivananda (Divine Life Society, 1937), and Deussen's Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda (1897).","The Praśna Upaniṣad — Praśnopaniṣad, 'the Upaniṣad of Questions' — belongs to the Pippalāda recension of the Atharva-Veda and forms, together with the Muṇḍaka and the Māṇḍūkya, the Atharvan triad of the ten Principal Upaniṣads commented upon by Śaṅkara. Of these three Atharvan Upaniṣads, the Praśna is the most dialogical: where the Muṇḍaka is gnomic-mantric and the Māṇḍūkya is aphoristic-architectonic, the Praśna is conversational, narrative, and pedagogical."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"1. About the Praśna Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Praśna Upaniṣad — Praśnopaniṣad, 'the Upaniṣad of Questions' — belongs to the Pippalāda recension of the Atharva-Veda and forms, together with the Muṇḍaka and the Māṇḍūkya, the Atharvan triad of the ten Principal Upaniṣads commented upon by Śaṅkara. Of these three Atharvan Upaniṣads, the Praśna is the most dialogical: where the Muṇḍaka is gnomic-mantric and the Māṇḍūkya is aphoristic-architectonic, the Praśna is conversational, narrative, and pedagogical.","Six brāhmaṇa-students — Sukeśā Bhāradvāja, Satyakāma Śaibya, Sauryāyaṇī Gārgya, Kausalya Āśvalāyana, Bhārgava Vaidarbhi and Kabandhī Kātyāyana — come, fuel-sticks in hand (samit-pāṇayaḥ), to Ṛṣi Pippalāda with the same need: 'devote yourselves to discipline (tapas), to faith (śraddhā) and to celibacy (brahmacarya) for one further year, after which ask whatever questions you wish; whatever we know we shall declare to you.' At the end of that year the six approach him in turn, each with a single question. The Upaniṣad is structured as six praśnas (questions), each forming a chapter; hence the name.","The questions move from cosmogony to anthropology to soteriology — a deliberate ascent. The first asks whence creatures come; the second, which divinity sustains the body; the third, the genesis and operation of prāṇa; the fourth, what wakes and what sleeps in this person, and who is the witness behind both; the fifth, what is won by meditating on the syllable Oṃ at the end of life; the sixth, where the Puruṣa of sixteen kalās is to be found. The order, as Śaṅkara remarks at the head of his bhāṣya, is no accident: it is the order of progressive interiority — from the outer cosmic pair (rayi-prāṇa) down through the body, then into prāṇa-physiology, then into the layered states of consciousness, then into the meditative instrument that traverses them, and finally into the trans-personal Puruṣa in whom the inquiry began.","The Praśna's most enduring contributions to Upaniṣadic thought are three. First, its presentation of Prāṇa not merely as breath but as the cosmic principle of life — Praśna 2 declares prāṇa to be greater than all the other devas of the body, and Praśna 3 traces prāṇa's birth from the ātman, its five-fold division (prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna), its operation in sleep and in dying, and its post-mortem exit. Second, its three-tier state-analysis in Praśna 4, which is the immediate Upaniṣadic prototype for the Māṇḍūkya's four-state schema. Third, its sixteen-kalā doctrine in Praśna 6, which became the principal Upaniṣadic source for the kalā-vidyā elaborated in later Tantric Śaivism and in the Pāñcarātra."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. The six questioners and their questions","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The order in which the six students appear in the text is the reverse of the order in which their questions are answered. The Upaniṣad opens by listing them in genealogical-honorific order: Sukeśā Bhāradvāja, Satyakāma Śaibya, Sauryāyaṇī Gārgya, Kausalya son of Aśvala, Bhārgava of Vidarbha, Kabandhī Kātyāyana. But the questions are answered in reverse: Kabandhī first (cosmogony), Sukeśā last (the Puruṣa with sixteen kalās). The reversal is intentional: the most senior student asks the most esoteric question and waits longest for his answer.","<table><tr><td>Praśna</td><td>Questioner</td><td>Question</td></tr><tr><td>1 (16 mantras)</td><td>Kabandhī Kātyāyana</td><td>Whence, sir, are these creatures born?</td></tr><tr><td>2 (13 mantras)</td><td>Bhārgava Vaidarbhi</td><td>Which deities support the body? Which is greatest among them?</td></tr><tr><td>3 (12 mantras)</td><td>Kausalya Āśvalāyana</td><td>Whence is prāṇa born? How does it enter the body, divide itself, depart, and sustain the outer and the inner?</td></tr><tr><td>4 (11 mantras)</td><td>Sauryāyaṇī Gārgya</td><td>What sleeps in this person, what wakes, who is it that dreams, in whom rest happiness and consciousness?</td></tr><tr><td>5 (7 mantras)</td><td>Satyakāma Śaibya</td><td>He who meditates on Oṃ until the end of life, what world does he conquer thereby?</td></tr><tr><td>6 (8 mantras)</td><td>Sukeśā Bhāradvāja</td><td>Where is the Puruṣa of sixteen kalās to be found?</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. Manuscript variations and numbering","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Praśna has an exceptionally stable text. Unlike the Īśā (Mādhyandina / Kāṇva) or even the Kena (numbering of prose units), there are no significant recensional divergences across the major editions. What varies is only the mantra-counting convention within each praśna — Śaṅkara's bhāṣya numbers each praśna's mantras independently (1.1, 1.2, … 1.16; 2.1, 2.2, … 2.13; etc.), which is the convention followed in this document. A few editions (Hume; some early translations) number continuously from 1 to 67 across the whole work; this document marks the continuous number in parentheses where useful.","The Sanskrit text used here is the Śaṅkara-bhāṣya recension as preserved in Gambhirananda (1957) and as encoded at sanskritdocuments.org (file: praznopanishhad.itx). Variant readings noted in footnotes by Śaṅkara himself are flagged when they affect translation.","Total mantras: 16 + 13 + 12 + 11 + 7 + 8 = 67. (Some editions give 66 by treating the closing salutation of Praśna 6 as a colophon rather than a mantra.)"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The five commentaries summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The same scholarly stack used in the Īśāvāsya and Kena volumes of this series, with one adjustment for the Praśna: where the Kena's fifth commentator was Aurobindo, the Praśna's fifth slot goes to Swami Sharvananda (Ramakrishna Math, 1923), whose Praśna translation-and-commentary is the most widely cited Ramakrishna-Mission rendering and whose treatment of the prāṇa-physiology of praśnas 2–3 is unusually thorough. Aurobindo did not write a complete commentary on the Praśna; his scattered remarks on prāṇa and on the sixteen kalās (in The Life Divine and in CWSA vol. 18) are cited inline where relevant but not used as a fifth running voice.","Swami Gambhirananda (Advaita Ashrama, Eight Upaniṣads with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, vol. 2, 1958) — Carrier of Śaṅkara's Praśna-bhāṣya in English, with Anandagiri's ṭīkā notes. The classical Advaitin lens: every praśna is read as moving the seeker from the saguṇa Brahman of the questions to the nirguṇa Brahman of the answers. Śaṅkara is especially careful in Praśna 4 (sleep / dream / deep sleep) and Praśna 6 (the sixteen kalās) to dismantle any reification of either the dreamer or the kalās.","Swami Sharvananda (Ramakrishna Math, Praśnopaniṣad, 1923; repr. many editions) — Standard Ramakrishna-Mission translation. Conservative-literal in the manner of Gambhirananda but with much fuller treatment of the physiology of prāṇas (Praśna 2.13, 3.5–3.10) and a careful Yoga-aligned reading of Praśna 5's Oṃ-meditation. Sharvananda is the source most cited in modern English Praśna-studies after Gambhirananda himself.","Swami Sivananda (Divine Life Society, The Principal Upanishads, 1937; Praśna lectures, Rishikesh) — Practical-sādhanā Advaita. Treats Praśna 5 as the canonical scriptural authority for the daily Oṃ-meditation he prescribed across his ashram; reads Praśna 4 as a meditation manual for the witness; uses Praśna 6's sixteen kalās as the foundation for his prāṇāyāma instruction.","Swami Krishnananda (Divine Life Society, The Praśna Upaniṣad, 1981 lecture series; Mandukya Karika commentary) — Philosophical-Advaita. Krishnananda is unusually sharp on the structural logic of the six questions as a single ascent, and on the question, common to praśnas 4 and 6, of whether the soul departs from the body or whether departure is itself a category-error. His treatment of Praśna 3.9–10 (the dying breath's trajectory through the nāḍī-system) is the clearest in the literature.","Paul Deussen (Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda, 1897; Eng. tr. Bedekar/Palsule, Motilal 1980) — Indological-historical reading. Treats the Praśna as a relatively late Atharvan composition (later than the verse Upaniṣads but earlier than the Māṇḍūkya), reconstructs the prāṇa-physiology in Praśna 3 against parallels in the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka and the Chāndogya, and provides the first European philological apparatus for the text. His running comparison of the Praśna's state-analysis (Praśna 4) with the Māṇḍūkya is the single most useful textual-historical contribution in the European literature.","Order of commentary digests below: Gambhirananda (Śaṅkara) — Sharvananda — Sivananda — Krishnananda — Deussen."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Peace Invocation (Atharva-Veda Śānti-pāṭha)","title":"","sa":["ॐ भद्रं कर्णेभिः शृणुयाम देवाः । भद्रं पश्येमाक्षभिर्यजत्राः ।","स्थिरैरङ्गैस्तुष्टुवाꣳसस्तनूभिः । व्यशेम देवहितं यदायुः ॥","स्वस्ति न इन्द्रो वृद्धश्रवाः । स्वस्ति नः पूषा विश्ववेदाः ।","स्वस्ति नस्तार्क्ष्यो अरिष्टनेमिः । स्वस्ति नो बृहस्पतिर्दधातु ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ śṛṇuyāma devāḥ | bhadraṃ paśyemākṣabhir yajatrāḥ |","sthirair aṅgais tuṣṭuvāṃsas tanūbhiḥ | vyaśema devahitaṃ yad āyuḥ ||","svasti na indro vṛddha-śravāḥ | svasti naḥ pūṣā viśva-vedāḥ |","svasti nas tārkṣyo ariṣṭa-nemiḥ | svasti no bṛhaspatir dadhātu ||","oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Oṃ. May we, O gods, hear with our ears what is auspicious; may we, O worshipful ones, see with our eyes what is auspicious. With steady limbs and bodies, praising you, may we attain the full term of life set for us by the gods. May Indra of great renown grant us well-being; may Pūṣā, knower of all, grant us well-being; may Tārkṣya of unobstructed motion grant us well-being; may Bṛhaspati grant us well-being. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This is the standard Atharva-Veda śānti-mantra, shared with the Muṇḍaka and the Māṇḍūkya. Note its outward-facing structure: unlike the Bṛhadāraṇyaka's 'asato mā sad gamaya' (an inward petition) or the Sāma-Veda's 'āpyāyantu mamāṅgāni' (a petition for sensory nourishment), the Atharvan śānti is a petition for the cooperation of the cosmic powers — Indra, Pūṣan, Tārkṣya (Garuḍa), Bṛhaspati — that the lifespan allotted by the gods may be lived without obstruction. The Praśna's six-questioner frame is itself a kind of communal śravaṇa, and the choice of this opening mantra accordingly stresses 'may we hear what is auspicious' (bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ śṛṇuyāma)."]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"The Six Praśnas","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each mantra below is given as: Devanāgarī → IAST → pada-by-pada gloss → literal → paraphrase → five-commentary digest. Where a praśna opens with prose narrative (the questioner's approach, the ṛṣi's answer-framing), the prose is rendered as IAST + literal + paraphrase + commentary, without the formal pada-table; pada-tables are reserved for the doctrinal mantras themselves.","Grammar abbreviations: m./f./n.; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc.; sg./du./pl.; pres./impf./pf./pot./impv./ger./inf./caus.; pp. = past passive participle; pap. = present active participle; kc. = karmadhāraya cpd.; tp. = tatpuruṣa cpd.; bv. = bahuvrīhi cpd.; dv. = dvandva cpd."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Opening frame","title":"The six approach Pippalāda","sa":["सुकेशा च भारद्वाजः शैब्यश्च सत्यकामः सौर्यायणी च गार्ग्यः कौसल्यश्चाश्वलायनो भार्गवो वैदर्भिः कबन्धी कात्यायनस्ते हैते ब्रह्मपरा ब्रह्मनिष्ठाः परं ब्रह्मान्वेषमाणा एष ह वै तत्सर्वं वक्ष्यतीति ते ह समित्पाणयो भगवन्तं पिप्पलादमुपसन्नाः ॥","तान् ह स ऋषिरुवाच भूय एव तपसा ब्रह्मचर्येण श्रद्धया संवत्सरं संवत्स्यथ यथाकामं प्रश्नान् पृच्छत यदि विज्ञास्यामः सर्वं ह वो वक्ष्याम इति ॥"],"iast":["sukeśā ca bhāradvājaḥ śaibyaś ca satyakāmaḥ sauryāyaṇī ca gārgyaḥ kausalyaś cāśvalāyano bhārgavo vaidarbhiḥ kabandhī kātyāyanas te haite brahma-parā brahma-niṣṭhāḥ paraṃ brahmānveṣamāṇā eṣa ha vai tat sarvaṃ vakṣyatīti te ha samit-pāṇayo bhagavantaṃ pippalādam upasannāḥ ||","tān ha sa ṛṣir uvāca bhūya eva tapasā brahmacaryeṇa śraddhayā saṃvatsaraṃ saṃvatsyatha yathākāmaṃ praśnān pṛcchata yadi vijñāsyāmaḥ sarvaṃ ha vo vakṣyāma iti ||"],"words":[],"lit":"'Sukeśā Bhāradvāja, and Śaibya Satyakāma, and Sauryāyaṇī Gārgya, and Kausalya Āśvalāyana, (and) Bhārgava Vaidarbhi, (and) Kabandhī Kātyāyana — these, devoted to Brahman, established in Brahman, seeking the supreme Brahman, (thinking) “This one indeed will declare all that” — they, fuel-sticks in hand, approached the blessed Pippalāda.'<br>‘To them the ṛṣi said: “Dwell for a further year in tapas, in brahmacarya, in faith; then ask questions as you wish. If we know, we shall declare all to you.”’","para":"Six brāhmaṇa-students, all serious seekers of Brahman, came together to Ṛṣi Pippalāda — carrying samit (fuel-sticks for the homa-fire), the canonical gesture of formal discipleship — believing that he was the one teacher who could answer all their questions.<br>Pippalāda did not answer at once. He set a probationary year of additional discipline, celibacy, and faith — only after which would he take their questions, and only on what he himself knew.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The compound 'brahma-parā brahma-niṣṭhāḥ paraṃ brahmānveṣamāṇāḥ' — devoted to Brahman, established in Brahman, and (still) seeking the supreme Brahman — is read by Śaṅkara as the three stages of an authentic seeker: doctrinal commitment (parā), practice (niṣṭhā), and the unsatisfied ongoing inquiry (anveṣaṇa) that distinguishes the genuine adhikārin from the merely doctrinally satisfied."],["Sharvananda","Notes that 'samit-pāṇayaḥ' is technical: the student carries fuel-sticks not as a gift to the teacher but as offerings to the teacher's sacred fire, signifying their entry into the teacher's household as resident disciples. The full traditional period is twelve years; Pippalāda will ask for one more on top of whatever they have already done."],["Sivananda","The opening verse establishes the model of all spiritual seeking — go to one teacher, carry fuel-sticks, surrender, and ask. He repeatedly cites this verse in his ashram-formation writings as the Upaniṣadic warrant for the gurukula structure of the Divine Life Society."],["Krishnananda","The six are not random students but representatives of the six dimensions of inquiry the Upaniṣad will trace — cosmological, anthropological, energetic, psychological, soteriological, and metaphysical. Their listing in this opening prose-frame already prefigures the six praśnas."],["Deussen","Names — Bhāradvāja, Śaibya, Gārgya, Āśvalāyana, Vaidarbhi, Kātyāyana — span the geographical range of late-Vedic India (Vidarbha = central India; Kātya-vamsa = north; Gārgya = a Bhāratī gotra). The Praśna therefore depicts a pan-Bharatavarṣa convocation at one teacher's hermitage — a literary picture of the eclectic Brahmavidyā gathering at the very end of the Upaniṣadic period."],["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> 'bhūya eva' — even further — implies these were already accomplished students. The triad tapas-brahmacarya-śraddhā recurs across the Upaniṣads (cf. Muṇḍaka 1.2.11) as the non-negotiable prerequisite for receiving the higher vidyās. The conditional 'yadi vijñāsyāmaḥ' — 'if we know' — is Pippalāda's modesty, not his ignorance; Śaṅkara reads it as the warrant of an authentic ācārya, who promises only what he himself has realised."],["Sharvananda","The 'further year' is the Upaniṣadic equivalent of the modern probationary novitiate. The student-cohort had presumably already completed a normal course of brahmacarya elsewhere; Pippalāda is asking for a Brahmavidyā-specific addendum."],["Sivananda","'yadi vijñāsyāmaḥ' — 'if we know' — is, for Sivananda, the most important phrase in the whole frame. A teacher may answer only what he has realised, not what he has merely read."],["Krishnananda","Tapas-brahmacarya-śraddhā corresponds to the three classical paths — karma, jñāna, bhakti — without yet being them. They are the preconditions of any of the three."],["Deussen","Parallels Chāndogya 4.10 (Upakosala served Satyakāma for twelve years without instruction; cf. the Praśna's 'further year' as a milder form of the same convention)."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Praśna 1 — Kabandhī Kātyāyana: Whence creatures? (16 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Praśna 1","title":"Kabandhī Kātyāyana: Whence creatures? (16 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Mantras 1.1 and 1.2 are the opening frame narrative given above (the six students approach Pippalāda; he requests a further year of tapas, brahmacarya, and śraddhā). The doctrinal exchange begins with mantra 1.3 — Kabandhī, the most junior of the six, asking first.","Praśna 1's answer-architecture: Prajāpati, desiring offspring, produces a primal pair — rayi (matter, the formed) and prāṇa (life, the formless). Sun is prāṇa, moon is rayi; year is Prajāpati, with two ayanas (southern → pitṛ-yāṇa, return; northern → deva-yāṇa, no return); month is Prajāpati (dark = rayi, bright = prāṇa); day and night are Prajāpati (day = prāṇa, night = rayi); food itself is Prajāpati, from which proceeds semen and from semen creatures. The chain works downward from cosmos to body, concluding with the brahma-loka attained by those rooted in tapas, brahmacarya and satya — and withheld from those in whom is crookedness, falsehood, or māyā."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1.3","title":"Kabandhī's question: whence creatures?","sa":["अथ कबन्धी कात्यायन उपेत्य पप्रच्छ । भगवन्कुतो ह वा इमाः प्रजाः प्रजायन्त इति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["atha kabandhī kātyāyana upetya papraccha | bhagavan kuto ha vā imāḥ prajāḥ prajāyanta iti || 3 ||"],"words":[["atha","indecl.","then / now"],["kabandhī kātyāyanaḥ","m. nom. sg. (patronymic)","Kabandhī of the Kātya lineage"],["upetya","ger. of upa-i","having approached"],["papraccha","3 sg. pf. of pracch","asked"],["bhagavan","m. voc. sg.","O Blessed One"],["kutaḥ","indecl. interrog.","whence / from where"],["ha vai","indecl. emphatic","indeed / verily"],["imāḥ","f. nom. pl. dem.","these"],["prajāḥ","f. nom. pl.","creatures / progeny"],["prajāyante","3 pl. pres. mid. of pra-jan","are born / come forth"],["iti","indecl.","(thus, ending the question)"]],"lit":"“Then Kabandhī Kātyāyana, having approached (the teacher), asked: ‘Bhagavan, from where indeed are these creatures born?’”","para":"Kabandhī, the most junior of the six and therefore answered first, asks the most elementary and the most encompassing question: what is the origin of creatures?","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The interrogative kutaḥ (‘whence’) admits two readings — pratyakṣa-kāraṇa (proximate cause: from food, from semen) and parama-kāraṇa (ultimate cause: from Brahman). Pippalāda will answer the first all the way down through the mantras of Praśna 1, and let the second emerge by implication. Cf. Taittirīya 3.1 ‘yato vā imāni bhūtāni jāyante’."],["Sharvananda","Note that Kabandhī does not ask ‘why’ but ‘whence’ — kutaḥ is locational/causal, not teleological. The Upaniṣad accordingly answers in the language of origination, not of purpose; the purpose-language (brahma-loka, amṛta) emerges only at the end of the praśna."],["Sivananda","‘kuto ha vai imāḥ prajāḥ’ — the daily contemplation: pause before you encounter any being and ask, ‘whence?’ The Upaniṣad teaches that the answer must travel down to rayi-prāṇa and finally to Prajāpati himself."],["Krishnananda","Kabandhī is described as kātyāyana — of the Kātya gotra, a lineage particularly associated with the karma-kāṇḍa. His question is accordingly framed in cosmological-creational terms, which the karma-kāṇḍa naturally privileges; he asks first because his question is the most exterior of the six."],["Deussen","Reads kutaḥ as the philosophical parallel to the Greek ‘pothen?’ — the same archetypal question with which Anaximander and Heraclitus opened their inquiries. The Praśna's first praśna is therefore the canonical Indian cosmogonic question."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.4","title":"Prajāpati's tapas: the primal pair rayi & prāṇa","sa":["तस्मै स होवाच प्रजाकामो वै प्रजापतिः स तपोऽतप्यत स तपस्तप्त्वा स मिथुनमुत्पादयते रयिं च प्राणं चेत्येतौ मे बहुधा प्रजाः करिष्यत इति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["tasmai sa hovāca prajākāmo vai prajāpatiḥ sa tapo 'tapyata sa tapas taptvā sa mithunam utpādayate rayiṃ ca prāṇaṃ cety etau me bahudhā prajāḥ kariṣyata iti || 4 ||"],"words":[["tasmai","m. dat. sg.","to him"],["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he (Pippalāda)"],["ha uvāca","indecl. + 3 sg. pf. of vac","indeed said"],["prajā-kāmaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","desirous of progeny"],["vai","indecl.","verily"],["prajāpatiḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Lord-of-creatures"],["saḥ tapaḥ atapyata","3 sg. impf. mid. of tap","he performed tapas"],["tapas taptvā","ger.","having performed tapas"],["mithunam","n. acc. sg.","a pair / couple"],["utpādayate","3 sg. pres. caus. mid. of ut-pad","produces / brings forth"],["rayim","m. acc. sg.","matter / food / the formed"],["prāṇam","m. acc. sg.","life-breath / the formless"],["etau","m. nom. du. dem.","these two"],["me","1 sg. gen.","of mine / for me"],["bahudhā","indecl. adv.","manifoldly"],["kariṣyataḥ","3 du. fut. of kṛ","they two will make"],["iti","indecl.","(thus, end of inner thought)"]],"lit":"“To him he said: ‘Prajāpati, desiring progeny, performed tapas. Having performed tapas, he produced a pair — rayi and prāṇa — (thinking) ‘these two of mine will make creatures manifoldly’.’”","para":"Pippalāda's answer begins at the origin: Prajāpati, the Lord-of-creatures, having no progeny, willed offspring; he warmed himself in tapas; and from that tapas issued the primal pair, rayi (matter, the formed) and prāṇa (life, the formless). All multiplicity descends from these two.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> rayi and prāṇa are not equivalent to puruṣa and prakṛti of Sāṅkhya, though the parallel is close. Rayi is mūrta — all that has form (annam, the moon, matter, the southern path); prāṇa is amūrta — all that lacks form (the sun, life, the northern path). The pair is the Upaniṣad's pre-Sāṅkhya formulation of the same metaphysical division. The tapas of Prajāpati is read by Śaṅkara as ālocana — primordial deliberation, not heat-yoga."],["Sharvananda","utpādayate (causative middle) emphasises Prajāpati's role as efficient cause: he does not give birth but causes the pair to come forth. The middle voice signals that the pair is for his own purposes (me bahudhā prajāḥ kariṣyataḥ — ‘they will make creatures for me’)."],["Sivananda","The verse is the canonical scriptural authority for tapas as a creative principle. Daily tapas is not punitive — it is generative; Prajāpati's example is the warrant."],["Krishnananda","The mithuna of rayi-prāṇa is the first appearance of polarity in the cosmos. All Upaniṣadic dvandvas — sun/moon, day/night, north-path/south-path, prāṇa/apāna — are echoes of this primordial dvandva. The unity behind them is Prajāpati, which is itself the unity behind the question."],["Deussen","The mithuna-cosmogony is the Atharvan signature. Compare Atharva-Veda 11.4 (the prāṇa-sūkta) and Praśna 3.3 below, where prāṇa is described as ātma-jāta (born from ātman). The Praśna therefore stands at the head of a continuous Atharvan tradition of life-as-cosmogonic-principle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.5","title":"Sun is prāṇa, moon is rayi; rayi is all that is formed","sa":["आदित्यो ह वै प्राणो रयिरेव चन्द्रमा रयिर्वा एतत्सर्वं यन्मूर्तं चामूर्तं च तस्मान्मूर्तिरेव रयिः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["ādityo ha vai prāṇo rayir eva candramā rayir vā etat sarvaṃ yan mūrtaṃ cāmūrtaṃ ca tasmān mūrtir eva rayiḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["ādityaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the sun"],["ha vai","indecl.","indeed"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) prāṇa"],["rayiḥ","m. nom. sg.","rayi"],["eva","indecl.","alone / itself"],["candramāḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the moon"],["rayiḥ vā etat sarvam","m. nom. sg. + n. nom. sg.","rayi indeed (is) all this"],["yat mūrtam ca amūrtam ca","n. nom. sg.","what is formed and (what is) formless"],["tasmāt","indecl.","therefore"],["mūrtiḥ eva rayiḥ","f. nom. sg. + m. nom. sg.","form itself (is) rayi"]],"lit":"“The sun, indeed, is prāṇa; the moon, indeed, is rayi. Rayi is verily all this — that which is formed and that which is formless; therefore form itself is rayi.”","para":"Of the two, the sun is prāṇa (the formless life-principle) and the moon is rayi (the formed). But rayi is then expanded: it is all that exists, whether formed or formless. Mūrti — embodiment, form — is itself rayi.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The apparent contradiction (rayi = mūrta only, vs. rayi = mūrta + amūrta) is resolved by reading the second as the inclusive sense in which rayi is the material-cause of everything. Just as gold (the substance) is both ‘formed’ ornaments and ‘unformed’ ingot, rayi covers both poles when read as material-cause; when read as polar opposite to prāṇa it covers only the mūrta side."],["Sharvananda","Renders mūrta as ‘gross’ and amūrta as ‘subtle’, after Śaṅkara — the same pair that recurs in the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka (2.3) as mūrtam ca amūrtam ca, the famous twofold division of Brahman."],["Sivananda","The sun-moon correspondence is the warrant for sūrya-namaskāra and candra-namaskāra as daily disciplines: solar practice for prāṇa, lunar practice for rayi (the bodily ground)."],["Krishnananda","Notes the inversion: in Vedic ritual, agni and soma form a pair where soma is offered into agni; here sūrya and candra form a pair where the formed (moon) is the food of the formless (sun). The Praśna therefore re-reads the agni-soma sacrifice as a cosmic alimentation of prāṇa by rayi."],["Deussen","Parallels Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.5.13 (annam ha vai mūrtam) and the broader Upaniṣadic identification of moon = food = rayi. The Praśna is unique in adding the inclusive expansion — rayi as the substance of both formed and formless."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.6","title":"Sun's rising — directional fivefold / sixfold prāṇa","sa":["अथादित्य उदयन् यत्प्राचीं दिशं प्रविशति तेन प्राच्यान्प्राणान्रश्मिषु सन्निधत्ते । यद्दक्षिणां यत्प्रतीचीं यदुदीचीं यदधो यदूर्ध्वं यदन्तरा दिशो यत्सर्वं प्रकाशयति तेन सर्वान्प्राणान्रश्मिषु सन्निधत्ते ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["athādityaḥ udayan yat prācīṃ diśaṃ praviśati tena prācyān prāṇān raśmiṣu sannidhatte | yad dakṣiṇāṃ yat pratīcīṃ yad udīcīṃ yad adho yad ūrdhvaṃ yad antarā diśo yat sarvaṃ prakāśayati tena sarvān prāṇān raśmiṣu sannidhatte || 6 ||"],"words":[["atha","indecl.","now / then"],["ādityaḥ udayan","m. nom. sg. + pap.","the sun, while rising"],["yat … praviśati","rel. + 3 sg. pres. of pra-viś","(in that it) enters"],["prācīṃ diśam","f. acc. sg.","the eastern direction"],["tena","n. instr. sg.","by that"],["prācyān prāṇān","m. acc. pl.","the eastern prāṇas (i.e. lives of beings in the east)"],["raśmiṣu","m. loc. pl.","in (its) rays"],["sannidhatte","3 sg. pres. mid. of sam-ni-dhā","gathers / deposits"],["dakṣiṇām … pratīcīm … udīcīm","f. acc. sg.","southern, western, northern"],["adhaḥ … ūrdhvam … antarā diśaḥ","indecl./f. acc. pl.","downward, upward, intermediate directions"],["yat sarvaṃ prakāśayati","rel. + 3 sg. pres. of pra-kāś (caus.)","(in that) it illuminates all"],["sarvān prāṇān","m. acc. pl.","all the prāṇas (= all living beings)"]],"lit":"“Now the sun, while rising, in that it enters the eastern direction, by that act gathers the eastern lives into its rays. And in that it (illuminates) the southern, the western, the northern, the downward, the upward, and the intermediate directions — in that it illuminates all — by that it gathers all lives into its rays.”","para":"The rising sun is not a static body — it is an active gatherer of life. As it crosses each direction, it draws into its rays the prāṇas (the lives) of all beings facing that direction. The sun is therefore the visible epitome of the cosmic prāṇa.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘prāṇān raśmiṣu sannidhatte’ means the sun does not exhaust life by shining but concentrates it into its rays — life and light are co-extensive. The sun is therefore the empirical sign of prāṇa as Praśna 2 will declare it."],["Sharvananda","The directional list (east, south, west, north, down, up, intermediate) is the six-plus-intermediate division of space; the Upaniṣad is teaching that prāṇa fills every spatial quadrant because the sun fills every spatial quadrant by its illumination."],["Sivananda","The verse is the scriptural ground for sūrya-upāsanā: the morning meditation that aligns the sādhaka's own prāṇa with the rising of the cosmic prāṇa."],["Krishnananda","The verb sannidhatte — ‘deposits’, ‘entrusts’ — is significant. The sun is not a thief of life; it is a custodian. The morning gathering of lives into its rays is matched by the evening release: the cycle of waking and sleeping in mantra 4.2 below traces the same arc within the body."],["Deussen","Parallels Chāndogya 3.1–3.5 (the madhu-vidyā: the sun as the honey of all beings, its rays as the bees). The Praśna 1.6 is a compressed Atharvan version of the same teaching."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.7","title":"The Vaiśvānara prāṇa rising as fire","sa":["स एष वैश्वानरो विश्वरूपः प्राणोऽग्निरुदयते । तदेतदृचाभ्युक्तम् ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["sa eṣa vaiśvānaro viśva-rūpaḥ prāṇo 'gnir udayate | tad etad ṛcābhyuktam || 7 ||"],"words":[["sa eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this very one"],["vaiśvānaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the universal-man (epithet of Agni / Sun)"],["viśva-rūpaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","having all forms"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","prāṇa"],["agniḥ","m. nom. sg.","fire"],["udayate","3 sg. pres. mid. of ud-i","rises"],["tat etat","n. nom. sg.","this very (thing)"],["ṛcā","f. instr. sg.","by a ṛc (verse)"],["abhyuktam","n. nom. sg. pp.","has been spoken / declared"]],"lit":"“This very (sun) is Vaiśvānara, all-formed, prāṇa, fire — it rises. This has been declared by a ṛc (verse).”","para":"The rising sun is summed up under four overlapping epithets — Vaiśvānara (the cosmic Person), viśvarūpa (all-formed), prāṇa (life), agni (fire) — and the mantra that follows in 1.8 is offered as the Ṛg-Vedic seal of this identification.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> Vaiśvānara names the prāṇa in its widest extension — not breath alone but the universal animator. The four epithets are not synonyms but four perspectives on one thing: Vaiśvānara (as universal Person), viśvarūpa (as multiplicity-of-forms), prāṇa (as life), agni (as combustive-energy)."],["Sharvananda","Reads vaiśvānara as the conjunctive epithet of the sun-prāṇa-agni triad, anticipating the Māṇḍūkya's vaiśvānara = the waking-state Self. Praśna 1.7 is therefore the cosmological foundation of Māṇḍūkya 3."],["Sivananda","The ‘rising’ (udayate) is the canonical meditation-image: as one practises, the prāṇa rises within just as the sun rises without; the two are formally the same act."],["Krishnananda","The phrase ‘tad etad ṛcābhyuktam’ — ‘this has been declared by a ṛc’ — marks the Atharvan compiler's awareness of (and authority over) the Ṛg-Veda; the following mantra (1.8) is presented as scriptural authority, not as independent doctrine."],["Deussen","The next mantra (1.8) is in fact a recasting of Ṛg-Veda 1.115.1 (sūryo devīṃ uṣasaṃ rocamānām) and 1.50 (the savitṛ-sūkta). The Praśna therefore embeds a Ṛg-Vedic anchor inside its Atharvan exposition — a rhetorical move common to late Upaniṣads."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.8","title":"The Sun as Prāṇa of all creatures — the ṛc","sa":["विश्वरूपं हरिणं जातवेदसं परायणं ज्योतिरेकं तपन्तम् ।","सहस्ररश्मिः शतधा वर्तमानः प्राणः प्रजानामुदयत्येष सूर्यः ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["viśva-rūpaṃ hariṇaṃ jāta-vedasaṃ parāyaṇaṃ jyotir ekaṃ tapantam |","sahasra-raśmiḥ śatadhā vartamānaḥ prāṇaḥ prajānām udayaty eṣa sūryaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["viśva-rūpam","m. acc. sg. bv.","all-formed"],["hariṇam","m. acc. sg.","tawny / yellow"],["jāta-vedasam","m. acc. sg.","knower-of-(all that has been)-born (= Agni)"],["parāyaṇam","n. acc. sg.","the (final) refuge / goal"],["jyotiḥ ekam","n. acc. sg.","the one light"],["tapantam","m. acc. sg. pap.","burning"],["sahasra-raśmiḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","(having) a thousand rays"],["śatadhā","indecl.","in a hundredfold (manner)"],["vartamānaḥ","m. nom. sg. pap.","existing / abiding"],["prāṇaḥ prajānām","m. nom. sg. + f. gen. pl.","(the) life of creatures"],["udayati","3 sg. pres. of ud-i","rises"],["eṣaḥ sūryaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this sun"]],"lit":"“All-formed, tawny, the knower-of-the-born, the (final) refuge, the one light, burning — thousand-rayed, abiding hundredfold, the prāṇa of (all) creatures — this Sun rises.”","para":"The sun, qualified by eight epithets (all-formed, tawny, jāta-vedas, refuge, one-light, burning, thousand-rayed, hundredfold-existent), is the prāṇa of all creatures. Its rising is therefore the rising of cosmic life itself.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> hariṇa carries both colour (tawny) and movement (haran, ‘carrying-away’); the sun both shines tawny and carries away the lives of beings at the end of life. parāyaṇam — ‘ultimate refuge’ — is a strong Vedāntic word; it appears in the Praśna here as an epithet of the sun (saguṇa) and will appear again in Praśna 5.7 below as an epithet of the Brahman beyond Oṃ (nirguṇa)."],["Sharvananda","jāta-vedas, normally an epithet of Agni, is applied here to the Sun as prāṇa — confirming the four-fold identity of 1.7 (sūrya = vaiśvānara = prāṇa = agni)."],["Sivananda","The eight epithets are the canonical bīja-meditation on the rising sun. He prescribes recitation of this mantra at sunrise, with attention moving through each epithet in sequence."],["Krishnananda","‘śatadhā vartamānaḥ’ — ‘abiding hundredfold’ — names the sun's distributed presence: a single sun, but a hundredfold-presence as the prāṇa of every creature. The same logic will recur in Praśna 6 below as the sixteen-kalā distribution of the one Puruṣa."],["Deussen","Confirms this mantra as an Atharvan recasting of Ṛg-Veda 1.50.10–11 and AV 13.2. The compiler has compressed several Ṛg-Vedic stanzas into a single anuṣṭubh-tristubh hybrid; the metrical irregularity is a sign of redactional age."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.9","title":"Year as Prajāpati; the southern path (pitṛ-yāṇa)","sa":["संवत्सरो वै प्रजापतिस्तस्यायने दक्षिणं चोत्तरं च । तद्ये ह वै तदिष्टापूर्ते कृतमित्युपासते ते चान्द्रमसमेव लोकमभिजयन्ते । त एव पुनरावर्तन्ते तस्मादेते ऋषयः प्रजाकामा दक्षिणं प्रतिपद्यन्ते । एष ह वै रयिर्यः पितृयाणः ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["saṃvatsaraḥ vai prajāpatiḥ tasya ayane dakṣiṇaṃ ca uttaraṃ ca | tad ye ha vai tat iṣṭā-pūrte kṛtam iti upāsate te cāndramasam eva lokam abhijayante | ta eva punar āvartante tasmād ete ṛṣayaḥ prajā-kāmā dakṣiṇaṃ pratipadyante | eṣa ha vai rayiḥ yaḥ pitṛ-yāṇaḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["saṃvatsaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","year"],["vai prajāpatiḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","(is) verily Prajāpati"],["tasya","m. gen. sg.","of him"],["ayane","n. nom. du.","the two ayanas (half-year solar courses)"],["dakṣiṇam ca uttaraṃ ca","n. nom. sg.","southern and northern"],["ye … upāsate","rel. m. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pres. mid. of upa-ās","those who meditate"],["iṣṭā-pūrte kṛtam iti","n. nom. sg. dv. + iti","‘what has been done by sacrifice (iṣṭā) and by works of public welfare (pūrta)’"],["cāndramasam lokam","m. acc. sg.","the lunar world"],["abhijayante","3 pl. pres. mid. of abhi-ji","conquer / win"],["punaḥ āvartante","indecl. + 3 pl. pres. mid. of ā-vṛt","(they) return again"],["tasmāt","indecl.","therefore"],["ṛṣayaḥ prajā-kāmāḥ","m. nom. pl. + m. nom. pl. bv.","ṛṣis desirous of progeny"],["dakṣiṇam pratipadyante","n. acc. sg. + 3 pl. pres. mid. of prati-pad","go by the southern (path)"],["eṣa ha vai rayiḥ","m. nom. sg.","this indeed is rayi"],["yaḥ pitṛ-yāṇaḥ","rel. + m. nom. sg.","which is the path-of-the-fathers"]],"lit":"“The year is verily Prajāpati; his two ayanas are the southern and the northern. Those who worship (Prajāpati) by ‘what is done by sacrifice and public works’ — they win the lunar world only, and they return again. Therefore these ṛṣis desirous of progeny take the southern (path). And this — the path of the fathers — is verily rayi.”","para":"The year itself is Prajāpati; its two half-courses are the southern (sun moving south, six months) and the northern (sun moving north, six months). Those who pursue Prajāpati through ritual works (iṣṭā = vedic sacrifice; pūrta = public welfare: wells, temples, gardens) win the lunar world — but they must return, for the lunar world is finite. This finite path is the path of the fathers (pitṛ-yāṇa), and it is identified with rayi.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The iṣṭā-pūrta pair (sacrificial works + public-welfare works) is the canonical karma-mārga; it bears fruit in the cāndramasa-loka (the world of the moon, the world of food). But the cāndramasa-loka is finite — once the fruit is exhausted, return is inevitable. Śaṅkara identifies this passage as the Praśna's articulation of the doctrine he expounds in his Brahmasūtra-bhāṣya at 3.1.8 (anuvṛtti)."],["Sharvananda","Iṣṭā = the five mahā-yajñas plus the śrauta sacrifices; pūrta = annadāna, kūpa-tāḍāga-prapā-vihāra (food-giving, well-tank-rest house construction), garden-making. Both produce return-fruits, both are rayi."],["Sivananda","‘te eva punar āvartante’ — ‘they return again’ — is the canonical warning against karma-only spirituality. Sivananda uses this verse repeatedly as the foundation for his argument that karma must be supplemented by jñāna."],["Krishnananda","The phrase ‘ṛṣayaḥ prajā-kāmāḥ’ is important: even ṛṣis, if they wish offspring (samsāra-continuation), take the southern path. The verse does not condemn the southern path; it locates it correctly — as the right path for those whose orientation is procreative-continuative."],["Deussen","Parallels Chāndogya 5.10 (the pañcāgni-vidyā) and Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 6.2 (the pravāhaṇa-vidyā), both of which describe the two paths (dakṣiṇa = pitṛyāṇa, uttara = devayāna). The Praśna's compression is striking — it gives in two mantras what the Chāndogya gives in two whole chapters."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.10","title":"The northern path (deva-yāna); no return","sa":["अथोत्तरेण तपसा ब्रह्मचर्येण श्रद्धया विद्ययाऽऽत्मानमन्विष्यादित्यमभिजयन्ते । एतद्वै प्राणानामायतनमेतदमृतमभयमेतत्परायणमेतस्मान्न पुनरावर्तन्त इत्येष निरोधस्तदेष श्लोकः ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["atha uttareṇa tapasā brahmacaryeṇa śraddhayā vidyayā ātmānam anviṣya ādityam abhijayante | etad vai prāṇānām āyatanam etad amṛtam abhayam etat parāyaṇam etasmāt na punaḥ āvartante iti eṣa nirodhaḥ tat eṣa ślokaḥ || 10 ||"],"words":[["atha uttareṇa","indecl. + n. instr. sg.","now, by the northern (path)"],["tapasā brahmacaryeṇa śraddhayā vidyayā","instr. sg.","by tapas, brahmacarya, śraddhā, knowledge"],["ātmānam anviṣya","m. acc. sg. + ger.","having sought the Self"],["ādityam abhijayante","m. acc. sg. + 3 pl. pres. mid.","(they) win the Sun"],["etad vai prāṇānām āyatanam","n. nom. sg.","this verily (is) the abode of (all) prāṇas"],["amṛtam abhayam","n. nom. sg.","immortal, fearless"],["parāyaṇam","n. nom. sg.","supreme goal"],["etasmāt na punaḥ āvartante","abl. sg. + negation","from this they do not return again"],["iti eṣa nirodhaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","this is the cessation (of return)"],["tat eṣa ślokaḥ","n. + m. nom. sg.","now (here is) the (summary) verse"]],"lit":"“Now, by the northern (path) — through tapas, brahmacarya, faith, and knowledge — having sought the Self, they win the Sun. This indeed is the abode of (all) prāṇas, the immortal, the fearless, the supreme refuge; from this they do not return again. This is the cessation (of return). Now, here is the (summary) verse.”","para":"The other path — the northern, deva-yāna — is taken by those who, through tapas, brahmacarya, śraddhā, and vidyā, seek the ātman and thereby win the sun. The sun, here, is no longer merely cosmic prāṇa but the abode of all prāṇas, immortal, fearless, parāyaṇa — and from it there is no return. This is nirodha — the stopping of saṃsāra.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The four means — tapas, brahmacarya, śraddhā, vidyā — are not coordinate but progressive: tapas prepares the body, brahmacarya the energy, śraddhā the orientation, vidyā the realisation. The deva-yāna therefore presupposes all four. ‘ādityam abhijayante’ here means the saguṇa-Brahman, the same as the parāyaṇa of mantra 5.7 below — Brahmaloka, from which final return is delayed until cosmic dissolution (krama-mukti)."],["Sharvananda","This is the Upaniṣad's canonical statement of krama-mukti — gradual liberation through Brahmaloka — to be contrasted with sadyo-mukti (immediate liberation) reserved for the direct knower of nirguṇa Brahman (Praśna 6.5 below)."],["Sivananda","‘tapasā brahmacaryeṇa śraddhayā vidyayā’ is the four-fold daily-practice formula on which the Divine Life Society's training programme is built. He cites this verse in nearly every lecture on the structure of sādhanā."],["Krishnananda","Note the asymmetry between the southern and northern paths: the southern wins a loka (cāndramasa), the northern wins not a loka but a sūrya (the sun itself, that is, the Self as the abode of prāṇas). Locality is the southern path's destiny; identity is the northern path's destiny."],["Deussen","Reads ‘ādityam abhijayante’ as Brahmaloka (= satya-loka) and notes the precise alignment with Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 6.2.15 and Chāndogya 5.10.1–2. The Praśna here is fully in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka-Chāndogya tradition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.11","title":"The summary verse: five-footed, twelve-formed Father","sa":["पञ्चपादं पितरं द्वादशाकृतिं दिव आहुः परे अर्धे पुरीषिणम् ।","अथेमे अन्य उ परे विचक्षणं सप्तचक्रे षडर आहुरर्पितमिति ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["pañca-pādaṃ pitaraṃ dvādaśa-ākṛtiṃ diva āhuḥ pare ardhe purīṣiṇam |","athā ime anya u pare vicakṣaṇaṃ sapta-cakre ṣaḍ-ara āhuḥ arpitam iti || 11 ||"],"words":[["pañca-pādam","m. acc. sg. bv.","five-footed"],["pitaram","m. acc. sg.","the Father (= Prajāpati = year)"],["dvādaśa-ākṛtim","m. acc. sg. bv.","twelve-formed"],["divaḥ","m. gen. sg.","of the sky / of heaven"],["āhuḥ","3 pl. pf. of ah","they call / declare"],["pare ardhe","n. loc. sg.","in the upper half (of the sky)"],["purīṣiṇam","m. acc. sg.","full of water / abounding (in showers)"],["atha","indecl.","now, but"],["ime anye u pare","m. nom. pl.","these other (sages), again, on the contrary"],["vicakṣaṇam","m. acc. sg.","the all-seeing"],["sapta-cakre","n. loc. sg. bv.","in (the chariot with) seven wheels"],["ṣaḍ-are","n. loc. sg. bv.","in (the chariot with) six spokes"],["arpitam","m. acc. sg. pp.","fixed / mounted"],["iti","indecl.","(thus, ending the citation)"]],"lit":"“Some call (Prajāpati) the five-footed, twelve-formed Father, dwelling in the upper half of heaven, full of waters. But these other (sages) (call) the all-seeing (Sun) mounted in (a chariot of) seven wheels and six spokes.”","para":"Two Vedic descriptions of Prajāpati-as-Year are juxtaposed. (i) The Atharvan image: five-footed (five seasons), twelve-formed (twelve months), in the higher half of the sky, rain-bearing (purīṣa = water/rain). (ii) The Ṛg-Vedic image (Ṛg-Veda 1.164.12): seven-wheeled (seven rays of the sun, or seven solar metres), six-spoked (six seasons). Both image the same cosmic Prajāpati from different angles.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The verse cites two existing scriptural views as a literary device. The ‘five feet’ are the five Indian seasons (vasanta, grīṣma, varṣā, śarad, hemanta, śiśira — counted as five if hemanta-śiśira are combined). The ‘twelve forms’ are the twelve months. The ‘seven wheels’ are the seven solar rays of the Ṛg-Veda. ‘Six spokes’ are the six seasons (counted fully). The juxtaposition shows that Prajāpati is one, however numerically partitioned."],["Sharvananda","‘pare ardhe purīṣiṇam’ — ‘in the upper half, full of water’ — is the macrocosmic image of the year as the rain-bearing celestial body. The Praśna here connects the year-Prajāpati of mantra 1.9 with the rain-Prajāpati of the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka."],["Sivananda","A verse for cosmic-contemplation: visualise the year as a great chariot, six seasons its spokes, twelve months its forms, and oneself as a single rotation within it. The image is meant to dissolve the egoic time-scale into the cosmic time-scale."],["Krishnananda","The verse is unusually difficult, but its function is clear: it offers a meditation-image (the Father with five feet and twelve forms; the Sun with seven wheels and six spokes) that the praśna-cycle is now winding back down to the body, via month and day in 1.12–1.13."],["Deussen","Direct citation of Ṛg-Veda 1.164.12 (the famous ‘riddle hymn’ of Dīrghatamas), with Atharvan recasting. The Praśna's compiler treats the riddle-hymn as a precedent for his own dual-image cosmology."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.12","title":"Month as Prajāpati: dark = rayi, bright = prāṇa","sa":["मासो वै प्रजापतिस्तस्य कृष्णपक्ष एव रयिः शुक्लः प्राणस्तस्मादेत ऋषयः शुक्ल इष्टं कुर्वन्तीतर इतरस्मिन् ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["māso vai prajāpatiḥ tasya kṛṣṇa-pakṣaḥ eva rayiḥ śuklaḥ prāṇaḥ tasmāt ete ṛṣayaḥ śukle iṣṭaṃ kurvanti itaraḥ itarasmin || 12 ||"],"words":[["māsaḥ","m. nom. sg.","month"],["vai prajāpatiḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","(is) verily Prajāpati"],["tasya","m. gen. sg.","of him"],["kṛṣṇa-pakṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the dark fortnight"],["eva rayiḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","is rayi"],["śuklaḥ","m. nom. sg. (sc. pakṣaḥ)","the bright (fortnight)"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) prāṇa"],["tasmāt","indecl.","therefore"],["ete ṛṣayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","these ṛṣis"],["śukle iṣṭam kurvanti","m. loc. sg. + n. acc. sg. + 3 pl. pres. of kṛ","in the bright (fortnight) perform sacrifice"],["itaraḥ itarasmin","m. nom. sg. + m. loc. sg.","the other (kind) in the other"]],"lit":"“The month is verily Prajāpati. His dark fortnight is rayi; the bright (fortnight) is prāṇa. Therefore these ṛṣis perform their sacrifices in the bright (fortnight); the other (kind of ritualists perform theirs) in the other.”","para":"Just as the year is Prajāpati partitioned by ayana, the month is Prajāpati partitioned by pakṣa (fortnight): the dark half is rayi (matter), the bright half is prāṇa (life). Hence the prāṇa-oriented ṛṣis perform their sacrifices in the bright fortnight (śukla-pakṣa); those of the contrary orientation, in the dark fortnight.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The pakṣa-rule is the canonical Vedic timing principle for śrauta sacrifices. ‘itara itarasmin’ — ‘the other in the other’ — refers, says Śaṅkara, to those whose intention is procreative-continuative (= rayi-orientation); they prefer the dark half. The verse therefore reinforces the dakṣiṇāyana/uttarāyaṇa logic at the monthly scale."],["Sharvananda","The verse is the scriptural authority for the lunar-calendar timing of vedic rituals. Pradoṣa-vrata (dark fortnight) and śukla-paurṇimā observances are read by this verse as orientation-markers."],["Sivananda","Practical correlate: meditate at brāhma-muhūrta during śukla-pakṣa (bright fortnight) for prāṇa-cultivation; during kṛṣṇa-pakṣa (dark fortnight), use the rayi-rest for body-recuperation and study."],["Krishnananda","The verse pre-empts a possible objection — if the year-as-Prajāpati already divides into prāṇa-ayana and rayi-ayana, why also divide month and day? The answer: cosmogony recurs at every scale (year, month, day), so that the seeker may locate the rayi-prāṇa pair wherever they find themselves in the calendar."],["Deussen","Compare AV 13.2.41–45, where the dark and bright fortnights are described as the alternating ‘black and white horses’ of the lunar deity. The Praśna's formulation is more abstract — pakṣa as rayi/prāṇa rather than as horse-colour — and is therefore later."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.13","title":"Day and night as Prajāpati; brahmacarya by daylight","sa":["अहोरात्रो वै प्रजापतिस्तस्याहरेव प्राणो रात्रिरेव रयिः । प्राणं वा एते प्रस्कन्दन्ति ये दिवा रत्या संयुज्यन्ते ब्रह्मचर्यमेव तद्यद्रात्रौ रत्या संयुज्यन्ते ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["ahorātraḥ vai prajāpatiḥ tasya ahaḥ eva prāṇaḥ rātriḥ eva rayiḥ | prāṇaṃ vai ete praskandanti ye divā ratyā saṃyujyante brahmacaryam eva tat yat rātrau ratyā saṃyujyante || 13 ||"],"words":[["ahorātraḥ","m. nom. sg. dv.","day-and-night"],["vai prajāpatiḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","(is) verily Prajāpati"],["tasya","m. gen. sg.","of him"],["ahaḥ eva prāṇaḥ","n. nom. sg. + m. nom. sg.","day itself (is) prāṇa"],["rātriḥ eva rayiḥ","f. nom. sg. + m. nom. sg.","night itself (is) rayi"],["prāṇam vai praskandanti","m. acc. sg. + 3 pl. pres. of pra-skand","verily lose / spill prāṇa"],["ye divā ratyā saṃyujyante","m. nom. pl. + indecl. + f. instr. sg. + 3 pl. pres. mid.","(those) who by daylight unite in coitus"],["brahmacaryam eva tat","n. nom. sg. + dem.","that itself (is) brahmacarya"],["yat rātrau ratyā saṃyujyante","rel. + f. loc. sg. + …","in that (they) unite at night"]],"lit":"“Day-and-night is verily Prajāpati. Day itself is prāṇa, night itself is rayi. Those who unite (sexually) by day verily spill prāṇa; that they unite at night — that itself is brahmacarya.”","para":"At the smallest cosmic scale, day-and-night is Prajāpati: day = prāṇa, night = rayi. The body that practises coitus by day loses prāṇa (which is solar, ascending, the seeker's resource); but coitus at night, when prāṇa is in any case withdrawn into rayi-rest, is brahmacarya — it does not deplete the prāṇic store.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The verse is the canonical Upaniṣadic ruling on the timing of sexual continence for the householder. Śaṅkara is careful to note that ‘brahmacarya’ here is the householder-grade — not the absolute continence of the brahmacārin proper, but the rayi-aligned (night-aligned) marital continence that does not waste prāṇa."],["Sharvananda","Notes that the verse anticipates the later Smārta rule against day-coitus (cf. Manu 4.40) and grounds it not in moralism but in prāṇa-physiology."],["Sivananda","A verse he cites in his Brahmacharya writings — for the gṛhastha, sexual continence is to be measured prāṇa-physiologically, not moralistically. The Praśna's rule is straightforwardly empirical: solar-time spending depletes solar-resource."],["Krishnananda","The doctrinal point under the practical rule: every act is to be timed to its cosmic phase. To act against the cosmic phase is to spill (praskand) prāṇa; to act with it is to conserve."],["Deussen","Parallels Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 6.4.6–7 (the prajāpati-vidyā in the form of marital ritual), where the timing of coitus is regulated by stars and seasons. The Praśna's formulation is more compressed and more philosophical."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.14","title":"Food, semen, and the birth of creatures","sa":["अन्नं वै प्रजापतिस्ततो ह वै तद्रेतस्तस्मादिमाः प्रजाः प्रजायन्त इति ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["annaṃ vai prajāpatiḥ tataḥ ha vai tat retaḥ tasmāt imāḥ prajāḥ prajāyante iti || 14 ||"],"words":[["annam","n. nom. sg.","food"],["vai prajāpatiḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","(is) verily Prajāpati"],["tataḥ","indecl. abl.","from that"],["ha vai","indecl.","indeed"],["tat retaḥ","n. nom. sg.","that (becomes) semen"],["tasmāt","indecl.","from that"],["imāḥ prajāḥ prajāyante","f. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pres. mid.","these creatures are born"],["iti","indecl.","(thus, closing the chain)"]],"lit":"“Food, indeed, is Prajāpati; from that (food) comes that semen; from that (semen) are these creatures born.”","para":"The chain closes at the body. Food is the final, immanent form of Prajāpati; food becomes semen; semen becomes offspring. The whole cosmogonic descent — Prajāpati → rayi-prāṇa → year → month → day → food → semen → creatures — is a continuous transmission.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘annaṃ vai prajāpatiḥ’ is the Praśna's restatement of Taittirīya 2.1 (annād bhūtāni jāyante). The whole praśna-1 chain has descended from the cosmogonic Prajāpati to the alimentary Prajāpati — without break."],["Sharvananda","‘retas’ — semen — is the male principle here, but Sharvananda notes the Upaniṣad does not exclude the female contribution; it simply uses the male principle as the metonymic representative of the alimentary-cum-procreative chain."],["Sivananda","The verse is the scriptural authority for the dietary regulation of brahmacarya: regulate food (the proximate form of Prajāpati) and one regulates semen (its next transformation) and one regulates progeny (the third)."],["Krishnananda","With this verse the cosmogonic answer to Kabandhī's question is given in full: ‘from where are creatures born?’ — from food. But ‘food’ here is not the empirical food but Prajāpati-as-food; the answer is therefore not reductive but the opposite — every meal is an encounter with Prajāpati."],["Deussen","Compare Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 6.4.1 (‘eṣāṃ ha vā eṣa ātmā paramo yo'nnam annāṃ ca prajātaṃ ca’). The Praśna's verse is a compressed Atharvan version of the same Bṛhadāraṇyaka teaching."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.15","title":"The Prajāpati-vrata; brahmaloka of those rooted in tapas","sa":["तद्ये ह वै तत्प्रजापतिव्रतं चरन्ति ते मिथुनमुत्पादयन्ते । तेषामेवैष ब्रह्मलोको येषां तपो ब्रह्मचर्यं येषु सत्यं प्रतिष्ठितम् ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["tat ye ha vai tat prajāpati-vratam caranti te mithunam utpādayante | teṣām eva eṣa brahma-lokaḥ yeṣām tapaḥ brahmacaryaṃ yeṣu satyam pratiṣṭhitam || 15 ||"],"words":[["tat","n. nom. sg. dem.","now (regarding) that"],["ye ha vai","rel. + indecl.","those who indeed"],["tat prajāpati-vratam","n. acc. sg.","that vow of Prajāpati"],["caranti","3 pl. pres. of car","observe / practise"],["te mithunam utpādayante","m. nom. pl. + n. acc. sg. + 3 pl. pres. mid. caus.","they (also) produce a pair (= offspring)"],["teṣām eva eṣa brahma-lokaḥ","m. gen. pl. + m. nom. sg.","of those alone is this brahma-world"],["yeṣām tapaḥ brahmacaryam","m. gen. pl. + n. nom. sg.","in whom (are) tapas and brahmacarya"],["yeṣu satyam pratiṣṭhitam","m. loc. pl. + n. nom. sg. pp.","in whom truth is established"]],"lit":"“Now, those who observe that vow of Prajāpati — they too produce a pair (i.e. offspring). The brahma-world belongs to them alone in whom are tapas and brahmacarya, in whom truth is established.”","para":"Even the gṛhastha-householder who performs the Prajāpati-vrata (the householder's full ritual cycle, including procreation) produces children — but the brahma-world is not won by the mere fact of procreation. It is won by those in whom tapas, brahmacarya, and satya are firmly rooted.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘prajāpati-vrata’ here is the householder's vow — to maintain the procreative-cum-ritual obligation — but it is not by itself sufficient for brahma-loka. Tapas-brahmacarya-satya are the additional requirement; without them, the procreative life-cycle wins at most the cāndramasa-loka (cf. 1.9 above)."],["Sharvananda","‘satyaṃ pratiṣṭhitam’ — ‘truth established’ — recalls the famous Muṇḍaka 3.1.6 (‘satyam eva jayate’). The Praśna shares with the Muṇḍaka the Atharvan emphasis on satya as the central transformative discipline."],["Sivananda","Cites this verse as the foundational text of his ‘brahmacharya for householders’ instruction: the gṛhastha may produce a child, but the brahma-loka is reserved for those in whom continence-in-thought, tapas-in-conduct, and truth-in-speech are unbroken."],["Krishnananda","The verse is the Praśna's first explicit answer to the implicit deeper version of Kabandhī's question — not just ‘whence creatures?’ but ‘by what discipline does one transcend creature-hood?’ Answer: tapas, brahmacarya, satya."],["Deussen","The triad tapas-brahmacarya-satya is the Atharvan Upaniṣadic shortlist for the moral pre-conditions of mokṣa; compare AV 11.7 and Muṇḍaka 3.1.5–6. The Praśna belongs squarely to this Atharvan ethical-meditative cluster."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 1.16","title":"The flawless brahma-world","sa":["तेषामसौ विरजो ब्रह्मलोको न येषु जिह्ममनृतं न माया चेति ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["teṣām asau virajaḥ brahma-lokaḥ na yeṣu jihmam anṛtam na māyā ca iti || 16 ||"],"words":[["teṣām","m. gen. pl.","of them"],["asau","m. nom. sg. dem.","that (well-known)"],["virajaḥ","m. nom. sg.","stainless / dust-free"],["brahma-lokaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the brahma-world"],["na yeṣu","negation + m. loc. pl.","in whom (there is) not"],["jihmam","n. nom. sg.","crookedness"],["anṛtam","n. nom. sg.","untruth"],["na māyā ca","negation + f. nom. sg.","nor illusion / deceit"],["iti","indecl.","(thus, closing the praśna)"]],"lit":"“That stainless brahma-world (belongs) to them — in whom (there is) no crookedness, no untruth, nor deceit.”","para":"Praśna 1 closes with the negative criterion of brahma-loka. The brahma-world is virajas — utterly free of rajas / dust / impurity — and it is reserved for those in whom there is no jihmam (crookedness of conduct), no anṛta (falsehood of speech), no māyā (deceit of mind).","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The three negatives — jihmam (crookedness of body / action), anṛtam (untruth of speech), māyā (deceit of mind) — exactly cover the kāyika-vācika-mānasika triad of moral evaluation. The brahma-world is for those who are purified at all three levels."],["Sharvananda","‘virajas’ here carries the full Atharvan force — not merely ‘pure’ but ‘without the dust of rajoguṇa’, i.e. without the passional disturbance that drives creature-life. The brahma-world is the post-rajas state."],["Sivananda","A core verse of his ‘three-fold purity’ teaching — body, speech, and mind to be purified together. The Praśna's tri-negation is, for Sivananda, the daily examen."],["Krishnananda","‘māyā’ here is not the technical Vedāntic māyā of Śaṅkara's later usage but the older Atharvan sense — deceitful intent, magical illusion-making. The brahma-world is the world without will-to-deceive."],["Deussen","Cross-reference Chāndogya 8.4.1 (‘atha ya ātmā sa setur vidhṛtir eṣāṃ lokānām asambhedāya … na pāpmanas taranti’) — the brahma-world as the bridge across which falsehood and crookedness cannot pass. The Praśna's closing verse is a compressed Atharvan version of the same Chāndogya teaching."]],"notes":["End of Praśna 1."]}]},{"t":"Praśna 2 — Bhārgava Vaidarbhi: Which deva sustains the body? (13 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Praśna 2","title":"Bhārgava Vaidarbhi: Which deva sustains the body? (13 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Where Praśna 1 traced the descent of creatures from Prajāpati to food and semen, Praśna 2 turns to the interior question: of the various ‘devas’ active within the embodied creature — the five elements (ākāśa, vāyu, agni, āpas, pṛthivī) and the four sense-faculties (vāk, manas, cakṣus, śrotra) — which is supreme? The praśna's answer, dramatised by an internal-dispute parable, is Prāṇa: not as one deva among the rest but as the principle by which all the others operate."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 2.1","title":"Bhārgava asks which deva is greatest","sa":["अथ हैनं भार्गवो वैदर्भिः पप्रच्छ । भगवन्कत्येव देवाः प्रजां विधारयन्ते कतर एतत्प्रकाशयन्ते कः पुनरेषां वरिष्ठ इति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha ha enaṃ bhārgavo vaidarbhiḥ papraccha | bhagavan kati eva devāḥ prajāṃ vidhārayante katara etat prakāśayante kaḥ punar eṣāṃ variṣṭha iti || 1 ||"],"words":[["atha","indecl.","then"],["ha enam","indecl. + m. acc. sg.","(addressing) him"],["bhārgavaḥ vaidarbhiḥ","m. nom. sg.","Bhārgava of Vidarbha"],["papraccha","3 sg. pf. of pracch","asked"],["bhagavan","m. voc. sg.","O Blessed One"],["kati eva devāḥ","indecl. + m. nom. pl.","just how many devas"],["prajām","f. acc. sg.","the creature"],["vidhārayante","3 pl. pres. mid. of vi-dhṛ","uphold / sustain"],["kataraḥ etat prakāśayante","m. nom. sg. interrog. + 3 pl. pres. caus.","which (of them) illumine this"],["kaḥ punaḥ eṣām variṣṭhaḥ","m. nom. sg. interrog. + m. gen. pl. + m. nom. sg.","and who, again, is the greatest among them"],["iti","indecl.","(end of question)"]],"lit":"“Then Bhārgava the Vaidarbha asked him: ‘Bhagavan, just how many devas sustain the creature? Which of them illumine it? And who again is the greatest among them?’”","para":"Bhārgava asks three sub-questions in a single mantra: (i) how many devas hold the body together? (ii) which of them illuminate it (i.e., make it conscious / sense-active)? (iii) which is the foremost?","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The threefold question is not arbitrary — it tracks the threefold function of the devas in the body: dhāraṇa (sustenance, structural integrity), prakāśana (illumination, sensory cognition), and śraiṣṭhya (supremacy, ultimate dependence). The answer must cover all three."],["Sharvananda","‘vaidarbhi’ — of Vidarbha (modern eastern Maharashtra) — locates Bhārgava geographically; the question's interiority-orientation is consistent with the Vidarbha region's later reputation as a centre of subtle psychology."],["Sivananda","Reads ‘prajām vidhārayante’ as ‘support the body’ in the most concrete sense — the question is, who keeps me upright? Who keeps me alive? Who lights me up? This is the question every embodied being should ask of themselves daily."],["Krishnananda","The question moves the inquiry inward from Praśna 1's cosmology. Where the first praśna asked ‘whence creatures?’, the second asks ‘what holds a creature together?’ — the move from genesis to subsistence."],["Deussen","‘kati’ — how many — is a quintessentially Upaniṣadic question-word; compare Yājñavalkya's catechism in Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.9 (‘kati devāḥ?’). The Praśna's question is a deliberate echo of that famous exchange."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.2","title":"The nine devas declare: we sustain the body","sa":["तस्मै स होवाच आकाशो ह वा एष देवो वायुरग्निरापः पृथिवी वाङ्मनश्चक्षुः श्रोत्रं च । ते प्रकाश्याभिवदन्ति वयमेतद्बाणमवष्टभ्य विधारयामः ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["tasmai sa hovāca ākāśo ha vā eṣa devaḥ vāyuḥ agniḥ āpaḥ pṛthivī vāk manaḥ cakṣuḥ śrotraṃ ca | te prakāśya abhivadanti vayam etad bāṇam avaṣṭabhya vidhārayāmaḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["tasmai","m. dat. sg.","to him"],["sa ha uvāca","indecl. + 3 sg. pf.","he said"],["ākāśaḥ … pṛthivī","m./f. nom. sg.","ether, wind, fire, water, earth"],["vāk manaḥ cakṣuḥ śrotram","f./n./n./n. nom. sg.","speech, mind, eye, ear"],["eṣaḥ devaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this deva (i.e. each of these is a deva)"],["te prakāśya","m. nom. pl. + ger.","they, having illumined (the body)"],["abhivadanti","3 pl. pres. of abhi-vad","declare"],["vayam etat bāṇam avaṣṭabhya","1 pl. nom. + m. acc. sg. + ger. of ava-stambh","we, having propped up this reed (= body)"],["vidhārayāmaḥ","1 pl. pres. caus.","(are the ones who) sustain (it)"]],"lit":"“To him he said: ‘Ākāśa indeed is a deva, (and so are) vāyu, agni, āpas, pṛthivī, vāk, manas, cakṣus, and śrotram. These, having illumined (the body), declare: «We, having propped up this reed (of a body), sustain it.»’”","para":"Pippalāda lists nine devas — five elemental (space, wind, fire, water, earth) and four organ-faculties (speech, mind, eye, ear) — and reports their collective claim: «we sustain the body».","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The word bāṇa — reed / arrow / stalk — is metaphorical for the hollow stalk-like body. The nine devas claim that they are what keeps the hollow body upright. The word is striking: it concedes in advance that the body is, structurally, a fragile reed — sustained only by what fills it."],["Sharvananda","Note the symmetry: five elemental devas plus four organ-devas, totalling nine. Prāṇa, the tenth, will reveal itself as their underlying principle. The Praśna's count therefore implicitly preserves the canonical daśa-prāṇa (ten-prāṇa) scheme of the Atharva-Veda, with Prāṇa proper at the top."],["Sivananda","A daily contemplation — sit and witness which of the nine devas is currently most active in the body. The Praśna teaches that none of them, severally, sustains the body; only their common animator, prāṇa, does."],["Krishnananda","The plural ‘vayam vidhārayāmaḥ’ — ‘we sustain’ — is the false claim of distributed sovereignty. Each deva, in claiming credit, mistakes its function for its ground. Prāṇa's reply in 2.3 will correct exactly this error."],["Deussen","The list of nine is older than the Praśna; compare AV 10.7 and 11.4 where similar lists of elemental-plus-organ devas are enumerated. The Praśna's contribution is the dramatic parable that follows — the dispute and the resolution."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.3","title":"Prāṇa's reply: I alone, dividing fivefold, sustain","sa":["तान्वरिष्ठः प्राण उवाच मा मोहमापद्यथाहमेवैतत्पञ्चधाऽऽत्मानं प्रविभज्यैतद्बाणमवष्टभ्य विधारयामीति । तेऽश्रद्दधाना बभूवुः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["tān variṣṭhaḥ prāṇaḥ uvāca mā moham āpadyatha aham eva etat pañcadhā ātmānaṃ pravibhajya etat bāṇam avaṣṭabhya vidhārayāmi iti | te aśraddadhānāḥ babhūvuḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["tān","m. acc. pl.","(to) them"],["variṣṭhaḥ prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the foremost Prāṇa"],["uvāca","3 sg. pf.","said"],["mā moham āpadyatha","prohib. + n. acc. sg. + 2 pl. aor.","do not fall into delusion"],["aham eva","1 sg. nom. + emph.","I alone"],["etat pañcadhā ātmānam pravibhajya","n. acc. sg. + adv. + m. acc. sg. + ger.","having divided myself fivefold"],["etat bāṇam avaṣṭabhya","m. acc. sg. + ger.","having propped up this reed (= body)"],["vidhārayāmi","1 sg. pres. caus.","(I) sustain (it)"],["te aśraddadhānāḥ babhūvuḥ","m. nom. pl. + adj. + 3 pl. pf. of bhū","they became disbelieving"]],"lit":"“To them the foremost Prāṇa said: ‘Do not fall into delusion! I alone, having divided myself fivefold, propping up this reed (body), sustain it.’ They became disbelieving.”","para":"Prāṇa replies: it is not nine devas but one Prāṇa, dividing itself into five (prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna — the daśa-prāṇa abbreviated to five), that sustains the body. But the nine refuse to believe him.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘pañcadhā ātmānam pravibhajya’ is the canonical reference to the five prāṇa-divisions, to be elaborated in Praśna 3.5–3.10. Prāṇa's argument here is not numerical (one vs. nine) but ontological: the nine are functions; the one is the principle of function."],["Sharvananda","‘variṣṭhaḥ prāṇaḥ’ — Prāṇa's title throughout this parable. He is the variṣṭha, the most exalted, not by self-assertion but by an upcoming demonstration."],["Sivananda","‘mā moham āpadyatha’ — ‘do not fall into delusion’ — is the imperative Sivananda applies to the senses themselves in his ‘mind under control’ instruction: the senses, taking themselves for their own ground, fall into the delusion of distributed sovereignty."],["Krishnananda","The dramatic refusal of belief (te aśraddadhānāḥ babhūvuḥ) is the Upaniṣad's psychological realism: the proof must come not by argument but by demonstration, since the nine cannot see beyond their own functional appearance."],["Deussen","Direct parallel to the prāṇa-vivāda of Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 6.1 and Chāndogya 5.1, where the prāṇas similarly dispute and prāṇa demonstrates supremacy by leaving the body. The Praśna here adopts the older Chāndogya-Bṛhadāraṇyaka parable."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.4","title":"Prāṇa's demonstration: as he rose, all rose","sa":["सोऽभिमानादूर्ध्वमुत्क्रामत इव तस्मिन्नुत्क्रामत्यथेतरे सर्व एवोत्क्रामन्ते तस्मिꣳश्च प्रतिष्ठमाने सर्व एव प्रातिष्ठन्ते । तद्यथा मक्षिका मधुकरराजानमुत्क्रामन्तं सर्वा एवोत्क्रामन्ते तस्मिꣳश्च प्रतिष्ठमाने सर्वा एव प्रातिष्ठन्त एवं वाङ्मनश्चक्षुः श्रोत्रं च ते प्रीताः प्राणं स्तुन्वन्ति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["so 'bhimānāt ūrdhvam utkrāmate iva tasmin utkrāmati athetare sarva eva utkrāmante tasmiṃś ca pratiṣṭhamāne sarva eva prātiṣṭhante | tad yathā makṣikā madhukara-rājānam utkrāmantaṃ sarvā eva utkrāmante tasmiṃś ca pratiṣṭhamāne sarvā eva prātiṣṭhante evaṃ vāṅ manaḥ cakṣuḥ śrotraṃ ca te prītāḥ prāṇaṃ stunvanti || 4 ||"],"words":[["saḥ abhimānāt","m. nom. sg. + m. abl. sg.","he, out of (just) indignation"],["ūrdhvam utkrāmate iva","indecl. + 3 sg. pres. of ut-kram + iva","as if (he) went upward / departed"],["tasmin utkrāmati","m. loc. sg. + pap.","when he was departing"],["itare sarve eva utkrāmante","m. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pres.","all the others too departed"],["tasmin ca pratiṣṭhamāne","loc. abs. (pap.)","and when he settled (back)"],["sarve eva prātiṣṭhante","3 pl. pres.","all also settled back"],["tad yathā","indecl.","just as"],["makṣikāḥ madhukara-rājānam utkrāmantam","f. nom. pl. + m. acc. sg. + pap.","the bees, (with) the king-bee departing"],["sarvāḥ eva utkrāmante","f. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pres.","all (the bees) also depart"],["evaṃ vāṅ manaḥ cakṣuḥ śrotram ca","indecl. + n. nom. sg.","so too speech, mind, eye and ear"],["te prītāḥ prāṇam stunvanti","m. nom. pl. + m. acc. sg. + 3 pl. pres.","they, gladdened, praise Prāṇa"]],"lit":"“He, out of just indignation, made as if to depart upward. As he departed, all the others also departed. As he settled back, all also settled back. Just as the bees, when the king-bee departs, all depart; when he settles, all settle — so too vāk, manas, cakṣus, and śrotra. They, gladdened (by the demonstration), now praise Prāṇa.”","para":"Prāṇa, indignant at not being believed, made a feint of leaving the body. The instant he stirred to leave, all the other devas were pulled along with him; and when he settled back, they too settled. Like bees following the king-bee, the senses cannot remain when their animator stirs to depart. The senses are now convinced and begin a hymn of praise.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The bee simile is the Atharvan signature; cf. the madhu-vidyā of Chāndogya 3.1. The point is that the swarm has no will independent of the queen; analogously, the sensory devas have no operation independent of prāṇa. Note also that Prāṇa departs only ‘iva’ — ‘as if’ — he does not actually leave; the demonstration is sufficient."],["Sharvananda","‘abhimāna’ here is not ‘arrogance’ but ‘legitimate self-assertion’ (Śaṅkara: nyāyya-abhimāna). Prāṇa's response is not pride but a justified demonstration."],["Sivananda","The verse is the scriptural ground for kuṃbhaka practice: when prāṇa stills (kumbhaka), all the senses still with it; when prāṇa moves, all senses move. The connection between prāṇāyāma and pratyāhāra is here exact."],["Krishnananda","The pivotal observation: the senses cannot prove the supremacy of prāṇa by argument; they can only see it when prāṇa stirs. This is why all prāṇa-vidyā in the Upaniṣads is taught via parable rather than via syllogism."],["Deussen","The bee-king simile appears here for the first time in the Atharvan tradition; later it becomes the standard prāṇa-mukhya simile in the Yoga-Upaniṣads. The Praśna therefore originates a long simile-tradition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.5","title":"Hymn to Prāṇa: agni-sūrya-parjanya-vāyu","sa":["एषोऽग्निस्तपत्येष सूर्य एष पर्जन्यो मघवानेष वायुः ।","एष पृथिवी रयिर्देवः सदसच्चामृतं च यत् ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["eṣaḥ agniḥ tapati eṣaḥ sūryaḥ eṣaḥ parjanyaḥ maghavān eṣaḥ vāyuḥ |","eṣaḥ pṛthivī rayiḥ devaḥ sat asat ca amṛtaṃ ca yat || 5 ||"],"words":[["eṣaḥ agniḥ tapati","m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres. of tap","this (Prāṇa, as) Agni, burns"],["eṣaḥ sūryaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this (is) the Sun"],["eṣaḥ parjanyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this (is) Parjanya (the rain-cloud)"],["maghavān","m. nom. sg.","the bountiful (= Indra)"],["eṣaḥ vāyuḥ","m. nom. sg.","this (is) Vāyu"],["eṣaḥ pṛthivī rayiḥ devaḥ","f./m./m. nom. sg.","this (is) earth, rayi, deva"],["sat asat ca amṛtam ca yat","n. nom. sg. + …","and (this is) what is being, non-being, and immortal"]],"lit":"“This (Prāṇa) is Agni that burns; this is the Sun; this is Parjanya the Bountiful; this is Vāyu. This is the Earth, rayi, the Deva; (this is) what is being and non-being, and what is immortal.”","para":"The hymn begins by identifying Prāṇa with all the great Vedic cosmic powers: he burns as Agni, shines as Sūrya, rains as Parjanya / Indra, blows as Vāyu, supports as Earth, sustains as rayi, lives as deva — and is being and non-being and the immortal as well.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The hymn deliberately collapses the polytheistic Vedic pantheon into Prāṇa. Every great Vedic deva (Agni, Sūrya, Parjanya, Indra, Vāyu) is named, and each is said to be Prāṇa. The Praśna is performing here, in compressed form, the same monotheising move the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka performs at 3.9 (Yājñavalkya: ‘really only one deva’)."],["Sharvananda","‘sat asat ca amṛtam ca yat’ — Prāṇa is being, non-being, and that which is beyond both (the immortal). The hymn anticipates Nāsadīya (Ṛg-Veda 10.129): sad-asat is the universal pair, amṛta is what transcends the pair."],["Sivananda","A canonical hymn for prāṇāyāma sādhanā: as you breathe, recite this verse, identifying each cosmic name with the breath you are drawing."],["Krishnananda","The verse is the Praśna's monotheism — but it is a monotheism not of a deva above devas, but of prāṇa beneath devas. Prāṇa is the underlying principle, not the overlording principle."],["Deussen","Compare AV 11.4.10–11 (the prāṇa-sūkta), where the same identification of Prāṇa with the cosmic powers is performed in mantra form. The Praśna's hymn is a Brahmaṇa-paraphrase of that older Atharvan sūkta."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.6","title":"Prāṇa as the hub of all that is","sa":["अरा इव रथनाभौ प्राणे सर्वं प्रतिष्ठितम् ।","ऋचो यजूꣳषि सामानि यज्ञः क्षत्रं ब्रह्म च ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["arā iva ratha-nābhau prāṇe sarvaṃ pratiṣṭhitam |","ṛcaḥ yajūṃṣi sāmāni yajñaḥ kṣatraṃ brahma ca || 6 ||"],"words":[["arāḥ iva ratha-nābhau","m. nom. pl. + indecl. + m. loc. sg.","as spokes in the hub of a wheel"],["prāṇe","m. loc. sg.","in Prāṇa"],["sarvam pratiṣṭhitam","n. nom. sg. + pp.","everything is fixed"],["ṛcaḥ yajūṃṣi sāmāni","f./n./n. nom. pl.","the Ṛc-verses, Yajus-formulas, Sāman-melodies"],["yajñaḥ kṣatram brahma ca","m./n. nom. sg.","sacrifice, kṣatra-power, brahma-priesthood"]],"lit":"“As spokes in the hub of a wheel, in Prāṇa is everything fixed — the Ṛcs, the Yajuses, the Sāmans, sacrifice, kṣatra, and brahman.”","para":"The famous spokes-in-the-hub image is applied to Prāṇa. The three Vedas, the sacrifice itself, kṣatra-power, and brahman-priesthood — all are fixed in Prāṇa as spokes in the wheel's hub. Prāṇa is therefore not merely the body's principle but the principle of the whole socio-ritual cosmos.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The ratha-nābhi image is the Upaniṣad's standard hub-and-spokes image of unity-in-multiplicity. The Praśna applies it here with maximum scope: not just the senses, but the Vedas, the sacrifices, and the social orders (kṣatra, brahma)."],["Sharvananda","‘yajñaḥ kṣatraṃ brahma ca’ is a striking inclusion. The Praśna is asserting that even the social-cosmic principle of kṣatra (rulership) and brahma (priesthood) is held together by Prāṇa. The verse is the ultimate Atharvan compliment to Prāṇa."],["Sivananda","‘arā iva ratha-nābhau’ — use this image in meditation: imagine yourself as a wheel, prāṇa as the hub, all your activities as spokes. Bring attention back to the hub."],["Krishnananda","The verse extends the body-parable cosmically. As the senses depend on prāṇa in the body, so the Vedas and the social orders depend on prāṇa in the cosmos. The macrocosm and microcosm are governed by the same principle."],["Deussen","The spoke-in-hub image is older than the Praśna; it occurs at Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.5.15 (with Brahman as the hub) and at Muṇḍaka 2.2.6 (with the heart as the hub). The Praśna's innovation is to put Prāṇa in the hub-position, in keeping with its Atharvan prāṇa-vidyā emphasis."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.7","title":"Prāṇa as Prajāpati in the womb; Prāṇa as the reborn","sa":["प्रजापतिश्चरसि गर्भे त्वमेव प्रतिजायसे ।","तुभ्यं प्राण प्रजास्त्विमा बलिं हरन्ति यः प्राणैः प्रतितिष्ठसि ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["prajāpatiḥ carasi garbhe tvam eva pratijāyase |","tubhyaṃ prāṇa prajāḥ tu imāḥ baliṃ haranti yaḥ prāṇaiḥ pratitiṣṭhasi || 7 ||"],"words":[["prajāpatiḥ carasi garbhe","m. nom. sg. + 2 sg. pres. of car + m. loc. sg.","(as) Prajāpati you move in the womb"],["tvam eva pratijāyase","2 sg. nom. + 2 sg. pres. mid.","you alone are born again"],["tubhyam prāṇa","2 sg. dat. + m. voc. sg.","to you, O Prāṇa"],["prajāḥ tu imāḥ","f. nom. pl. + emph.","these creatures verily"],["balim haranti","m. acc. sg. + 3 pl. pres.","bear (their) tribute"],["yaḥ prāṇaiḥ pratitiṣṭhasi","rel. + m. instr. pl. + 2 sg. pres.","(O you) who stand by (your divisions, the) prāṇas"]],"lit":"“As Prajāpati you move in the womb; you alone are born again (as the child). To you, O Prāṇa, do these creatures bring tribute — you who stand firm through the (five) prāṇas.”","para":"The hymn now addresses Prāṇa directly. Prāṇa moves as Prajāpati in the womb (the embryonic process is itself prāṇa-activity); Prāṇa is what is reborn in the child. All creatures pay him tribute (bali = the homely offering at every meal). He is the stable centre, established through his five divisions.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘prajāpatiś carasi garbhe’ — the Praśna identifies the embryological development with prāṇa's movement in the womb. The child is Prajāpati reborn — but Prajāpati is Prāṇa; so the child is Prāṇa reborn. The verse therefore makes prāṇa the principle of birth, not its consequence."],["Sharvananda","‘balim haranti’ — bring tribute — refers to the daily pañca-balikarma (five-fold home-offering) prescribed in the Smṛti texts; Sharvananda notes that the food offered to gods, ṛṣis, fathers, men, and animals is in fact all offered to Prāṇa, since all five recipient-classes are themselves Prāṇa."],["Sivananda","‘yaḥ prāṇaiḥ pratitiṣṭhasi’ — the stability of the body is the stability of prāṇa's five-fold balance. He cites this verse as the scriptural ground for his prāṇāyāma-as-stability instruction."],["Krishnananda","The verse is the Praśna's embryology: the foetus is not a body that gradually acquires prāṇa, but prāṇa-itself, taking on the form of a body. Prāṇa is the prius; the body is the consequent."],["Deussen","Parallels Aitareya-Upaniṣad 2.1–4 (the embryological vidyā where the ātman is described as entering the foetus and being reborn as the child). The Praśna's compression — ‘prajāpatiś carasi garbhe’ — is one of the most condensed embryological statements in the Upaniṣadic corpus."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.8","title":"Prāṇa as the conveyor of oblations","sa":["देवानामसि वह्नितमः पितॄणां प्रथमा स्वधा ।","ऋषीणां चरितं सत्यमथर्वाङ्गिरसामसि ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["devānām asi vahnitamaḥ pitṝṇāṃ prathamā svadhā |","ṛṣīṇām caritaṃ satyam atharva-aṅgirasām asi || 8 ||"],"words":[["devānām asi vahnitamaḥ","m. gen. pl. + 2 sg. pres. + m. nom. sg. superl.","of the devas you are the chief carrier (of oblations)"],["pitṝṇām prathamā svadhā","m. gen. pl. + f. nom. sg. superl. + f. nom. sg.","of the fathers, (you are) the first svadhā-offering"],["ṛṣīṇām caritam satyam","m. gen. pl. + n. nom. sg.","of the ṛṣis, the true conduct"],["atharva-aṅgirasām asi","m. gen. pl. + 2 sg. pres.","of the Atharva-Aṅgirases you are (their essence)"]],"lit":"“Of the devas you are the chief carrier (vahnitama, = Agni); of the fathers, the first svadhā-offering; of the ṛṣis, the true conduct; you are of the Atharva-Aṅgirases (their essence).”","para":"Prāṇa is identified with the conveyor-function in all the principal ritual contexts: he is Agni (who carries the deva-oblation), he is the first svadhā (the fathers' offering), he is the satya-carita of the ṛṣis, he is the essence of the Atharvan-Āṅgirasa ṛṣi-lineage.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘vahnitama’ — the most-carrying — names Prāṇa as the function that delivers the oblation to its recipient. Whether the offering goes to deva (via agni), to pitṛ (via svadhā), or to ṛṣi (via satya), the conveying-power in each case is prāṇa."],["Sharvananda","The verse explicitly names the Atharvan-Āṅgirasa lineage (the Veda in which this Upaniṣad sits) and identifies its essence with Prāṇa. This is the Praśna's self-locating signature: ‘we, the Atharvans, hold Prāṇa as our central deity.’"],["Sivananda","Reads ‘ṛṣīṇāṃ caritaṃ satyam’ — the true conduct of the ṛṣis — as the daily ethical-aspirational ideal: live in such a way that the very breath of your life is the satya-carita the Praśna describes."],["Krishnananda","‘vahnitama’ has a double meaning — both ‘the most-carrying’ (carrier of oblations) and ‘the highest fire’. The Praśna leaves both senses active: Prāṇa is both the cosmic Agni and the conveyor-of-everything."],["Deussen","‘atharvāṅgirasām’ — of the Atharva-Aṅgirases — is the Praśna's only explicit self-identifying tag for its Vedic affiliation. The verse confirms the Atharvan provenance of the Upaniṣad."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.9","title":"Prāṇa as Indra, Rudra, the sun, the all-gods","sa":["इन्द्रस्त्वं प्राण तेजसा रुद्रोऽसि परिरक्षिता ।","त्वमन्तरिक्षे चरसि सूर्यस्त्वं ज्योतिषां पतिः ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["indraḥ tvam prāṇa tejasā rudraḥ asi parirakṣitā |","tvam antarikṣe carasi sūryaḥ tvam jyotiṣāṃ patiḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["indraḥ tvam prāṇa tejasā","m. nom. sg. + 2 sg. + m. voc. sg. + n. instr. sg.","Indra you (are), O Prāṇa, by (your) tejas"],["rudraḥ asi parirakṣitā","m. nom. sg. + 2 sg. pres. + m. nom. sg.","Rudra you are, the protector-around"],["tvam antarikṣe carasi","2 sg. + n. loc. sg. + 2 sg. pres.","you move in the mid-region"],["sūryaḥ tvam jyotiṣām patiḥ","m. nom. sg. + 2 sg. + n. gen. pl. + m. nom. sg.","Sun are you, lord of (all) lights"]],"lit":"“Indra you are, O Prāṇa, by your tejas; Rudra you are, the protector-around. You move in the mid-region; the Sun you are, the lord of (all) lights.”","para":"Prāṇa is identified with the great solar-cosmic deities: Indra (by tejas — fierce energy), Rudra (as parirakṣitā — the surrounding protector), the wind-in-mid-region (antarikṣa-carin), and Sūrya the lord of lights. Each cosmic power is one aspect of the one Prāṇa.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘tejasā’ specifies the mode of Prāṇa-as-Indra: not as kingship (rajya) but as tejas (fiery energy). ‘parirakṣitā’ — the protector-around — names Prāṇa-as-Rudra in his protective rather than destructive aspect. The verse therefore presents two aspects of fierce power, both subsumed under Prāṇa."],["Sharvananda","‘jyotiṣāṃ patiḥ’ — lord of (all) lights — recalls Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.16 (‘yo vai jīvann itaḥ paretya kṣetrajñam ūrdhvam adho vā gacchati, sa hi sarveṣāṃ jyotiṣāṃ jyotiḥ’). Prāṇa is the light behind every light."],["Sivananda","Daily meditation: name Prāṇa successively as Indra, as Rudra, as Sūrya — and feel the corresponding mode of energy in the body. The verse is a bīja-meditation in three names."],["Krishnananda","The verse moves Prāṇa upward through the Vedic pantheon — from Indra (heroic) through Rudra (protective) to Sūrya (luminous-supreme). The implicit message: any deva-meditation is in fact a Prāṇa-meditation."],["Deussen","Parallel to AV 11.4.12 (the prāṇa-sūkta). The Praśna here is dependent on the older Atharvan hymn but compresses its theology."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.10","title":"Prāṇa as the cosmic rain-bearer; everything is fixed in him","sa":["यदा त्वमभिवर्षस्यथेमाः प्राण ते प्रजाः ।","आनन्दरूपास्तिष्ठन्ति कामायान्नं भविष्यतीति ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["yadā tvam abhivarṣasi atha imāḥ prāṇa te prajāḥ |","ānanda-rūpāḥ tiṣṭhanti kāmāya annaṃ bhaviṣyati iti || 10 ||"],"words":[["yadā tvam abhivarṣasi","indecl. + 2 sg. + 2 sg. pres. of abhi-vṛṣ","when you rain forth"],["atha imāḥ prāṇa te prajāḥ","indecl. + f. nom. pl. + m. voc. sg. + 2 sg. gen. + f. nom. pl.","then these creatures of yours, O Prāṇa"],["ānanda-rūpāḥ tiṣṭhanti","m. nom. pl. bv. + 3 pl. pres.","stand in the form of bliss"],["kāmāya annam bhaviṣyati iti","m. dat. sg. + n. nom. sg. + 3 sg. fut. + iti","‘food will be (had) for desire’"]],"lit":"“When you rain forth, O Prāṇa, then these creatures of yours stand in the form of bliss — (thinking) ‘there will be food for (every) desire’.”","para":"When Prāṇa rains (as Parjanya), creatures stand in ānanda-rūpa — the form of bliss — expecting that food will now be plentiful. The verse reads the cosmic rain not naturalistically but as Prāṇa's gift of food, which is the body's joy.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘ānanda-rūpāḥ’ is the precise psychological description: at the first rain, every creature stands in the bodily mode of joy. The Upaniṣad reads this universal-creature-joy as evidence of Prāṇa's gift-character — and therefore as evidence of his variṣṭhatva."],["Sharvananda","‘kāmāyānnaṃ bhaviṣyati’ — the implicit thought of the creature: now food will be available for whatever I desire. The verse is a finely-observed psychological note on the relation of rain, food, and bodily joy."],["Sivananda","A verse for cultivation of gratitude: every meal is the result of Prāṇa's prior rain; every desire-fulfilling meal is an instance of Prāṇa's gift."],["Krishnananda","‘imāḥ te prajāḥ’ — ‘these creatures of yours’ — completes the parable. The nine devas began (in 2.2) by saying ‘we sustain prajā (the body)’; here Prāṇa says ‘these creatures are mine.’ The possessive pronoun marks the praśna's conclusion."],["Deussen","Parallel to AV 11.4.16. The image of the rain-deva as Prāṇa is older than the Praśna but is here given a uniquely psychological gloss (ānanda-rūpāḥ)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.11","title":"Prāṇa as vrātya, ekarṣi, Mātariśvan","sa":["व्रात्यस्त्वं प्राणैकर्षिरत्ता विश्वस्य सत्पतिः ।","वयमाद्यस्य दातारः पिता त्वं मातरिश्व नः ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["vrātyaḥ tvam prāṇa eka-ṛṣiḥ attā viśvasya sat-patiḥ |","vayam ādyasya dātāraḥ pitā tvam mātariśva naḥ || 11 ||"],"words":[["vrātyaḥ tvam prāṇa","m. nom. sg. + 2 sg. + m. voc. sg.","vrātya are you, O Prāṇa"],["eka-ṛṣiḥ","m. nom. sg.","the one-ṛṣi (= the primordial Seer)"],["attā","m. nom. sg.","the eater (of all)"],["viśvasya sat-patiḥ","m. gen. sg. + m. nom. sg.","the lord-of-being of (all) the universe"],["vayam ādyasya dātāraḥ","1 pl. nom. + n. gen. sg. + m. nom. pl.","we (are) the givers of food (to you)"],["pitā tvam","m. nom. sg. + 2 sg.","father (are) you"],["mātariśva naḥ","m. voc. sg. + 1 pl. dat.","O Mātariśvan, to us"]],"lit":"“Vrātya you are, O Prāṇa, the one-ṛṣi, the eater (of all), the lord-of-being of the universe. We are the givers of food (to you). To us, O Mātariśvan, you are the father.”","para":"Four exalted Vedic titles for Prāṇa: vrātya (the autonomous wandering brahman of AV 15), ekarṣi (the one-seer, the primordial Hiraṇyagarbha), attā (the eater, who consumes all in dissolution), sat-pati (lord of being). The hymn now reverses the gift-flow: we creatures are the givers of food to Prāṇa (food = our offerings, our breath itself), while he, as Mātariśvan (the inner wind), is our father.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘vrātya’ here is the technical title from Atharva-Veda 15, where the Vrātya is the un-initiated yet self-sufficient brahman who wanders independently of orthodoxy. Calling Prāṇa ‘vrātya’ asserts his autonomy from ritual: he needs no consecration; he is already pure. ‘ekarṣi’ is the primordial single Seer of pre-creation; ‘attā’ is the eater in the pralaya. The four titles span Prāṇa's roles before, during, and after the cosmic cycle."],["Sharvananda","The reversal ‘vayam ādyasya dātāraḥ’ — ‘we are the givers (of food)’ — is the climax of the hymn. The senses, having been corrected by Prāṇa, now offer their tribute back to him. The mutual obligation is established: he sustains us, we feed him."],["Sivananda","‘pitā tvaṃ mātariśva naḥ’ — Mātariśvan, the wind that ‘grows in the mother (= space)’, is the inner-bodily prāṇa as one's own father. Daily meditation: regard the breath as one's own father; respect it as one would a parent."],["Krishnananda","Note the gender-shift in ‘mātariśvan’ — the wind that grows in the mother. Prāṇa, who has been male throughout the hymn, is now invoked as he-who-grows-in-mother, a feminine-rooted masculine. The Upaniṣad refuses to fix Prāṇa to a single gender."],["Deussen","‘vrātya’ as an Atharvan technical term confirms the Praśna's Atharvan affiliation; the term is rare elsewhere. The Praśna is one of the few late Atharvan texts that preserves the vrātya-tradition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.12","title":"Prāṇa in speech, ear, eye, mind — withhold not","sa":["या ते तनूर्वाचि प्रतिष्ठिता या श्रोत्रे या च चक्षुषि ।","या च मनसि सन्तता शिवां तां कुरु मोत्क्रमीः ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["yā te tanūḥ vāci pratiṣṭhitā yā śrotre yā ca cakṣuṣi |","yā ca manasi santatā śivāṃ tāṃ kuru mā utkramīḥ || 12 ||"],"words":[["yā te tanūḥ vāci pratiṣṭhitā","f. nom. sg. + 2 sg. gen. + f. loc. sg. + pp.","that body of yours that is established in speech"],["yā śrotre yā ca cakṣuṣi","f. nom. sg.","(and that) which (is) in the ear, and which (is) in the eye"],["yā ca manasi santatā","f. nom. sg. + f. loc. sg. + pp. of sam-tan","and which is extended in the mind"],["śivām tām kuru","f. acc. sg. + f. acc. sg. + 2 sg. impv.","make that (form) auspicious"],["mā utkramīḥ","prohib. + 2 sg. aor.","do not depart (from us)"]],"lit":"“That form of yours which is established in speech, in the ear, in the eye, and that which is extended in the mind — make that (form) auspicious; do not depart!”","para":"The senses now address Prāṇa with a petition: in each of the four senses (speech, ear, eye, mind), Prāṇa has a particular ‘body’ (tanu) — that is, a particular mode of presence. May he make each of these modes auspicious, and may he not depart.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘tanūḥ’ — ‘body’ or ‘form’ — is the Upaniṣadic technical term for the deva's localised presence in a particular faculty (cf. Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.5.21–22). The Praśna applies the term to Prāṇa: he has four ‘bodies’, one in each of the four sense-faculties named."],["Sharvananda","The petition ‘mā utkramīḥ’ — do not depart — is the senses' final acknowledgement of Prāṇa's supremacy: they have learned that his departure means their departure."],["Sivananda","A precise prāṇāyāma-prayer: as you settle into prāṇāyāma, ask the prāṇa not to depart from the four faculties named, but to abide there auspiciously. The mantra is to be recited at the start of each prāṇāyāma session."],["Krishnananda","The verse names exactly the four faculties involved in cognition: vāk (expression), śrotra (reception of sound), cakṣus (reception of form), manas (inner integration). Prāṇa's auspicious presence in all four is the condition of cognitive functioning."],["Deussen","The four-faculty list — vāk, śrotra, cakṣus, manas — is the older Atharvan list; later lists (e.g. Yoga-sūtra 2.18) add ghrāṇa (smell), rasanā (taste), tvac (touch). The Praśna's archaism is significant."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2.13","title":"Prāṇa is all — three worlds rest on him","sa":["प्राणस्येदं वशे सर्वं त्रिदिवे यत्प्रतिष्ठितम् ।","मातेव पुत्रान्रक्षस्व श्रीश्च प्रज्ञां च विधेहि न इति ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["prāṇasya idaṃ vaśe sarvaṃ tridive yat pratiṣṭhitam |","mātā iva putrān rakṣasva śrīḥ ca prajñāṃ ca vidhehi naḥ iti || 13 ||"],"words":[["prāṇasya idam vaśe sarvam","m. gen. sg. + n. nom. sg. + m. loc. sg. + n. nom. sg.","all this (is) in the power of Prāṇa"],["tri-dive yat pratiṣṭhitam","n. loc. sg. + rel. + pp.","what is established in the three (heavens)"],["mātā iva putrān rakṣasva","f. nom. sg. + indecl. + m. acc. pl. + 2 sg. impv.","as a mother (protects her) sons, protect (us)"],["śrīḥ ca prajñām ca vidhehi naḥ","f. acc. sg. + f. acc. sg. + 2 sg. impv. + 1 pl. dat.","and grant us prosperity and wisdom"],["iti","indecl.","(end of hymn)"]],"lit":"“All this — whatever is established in the three (heavens) — is in Prāṇa's power. As a mother (protects her) sons, protect (us); grant us prosperity and wisdom.”","para":"The hymn closes with two final petitions. First, the doctrine: all that stands in the three worlds is in Prāṇa's power (vaśa). Second, the prayer: as a mother protects her sons, protect us; grant śrī (prosperity, beauty, the goddess of fortune) and prajñā (wisdom, the inner discerning faculty).","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘tridive yat pratiṣṭhitam’ — ‘what is established in the three heavens (= three worlds: bhūr-bhuvar-svar)’. The phrase makes Prāṇa's lordship cosmic and complete. The closing petition for śrī and prajñā shows that Prāṇa is the source not only of life but of both worldly success and ultimate wisdom."],["Sharvananda","‘mātā iva putrān’ — once again the gender flexibility (cf. 2.11's mātariśvan): Prāṇa, who has been father, is now prayed to as mother. The fluidity is doctrinal: Prāṇa is the parental principle, neither exclusively male nor female."],["Sivananda","The verse is among the most-recited closing-prayers in Sivananda's daily liturgy. ‘śrīṃ ca prajñāṃ ca vidhehi naḥ’ — grant us both worldly grace and inner wisdom — is the householder-yogin's two-fold petition."],["Krishnananda","With this verse Praśna 2 closes. The Upaniṣad has answered all three of Bhārgava's sub-questions: how many devas sustain the body (nine + one), which illumine it (all of them, but by Prāṇa's power), which is greatest (Prāṇa). The next Praśna will trace Prāṇa's anatomy."],["Deussen","‘śrīḥ ca prajñāṃ ca’ — the pair recalls the close of the Īśā Upaniṣad's invocation (vidyā-avidyā), and the famous Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.5.13 (vijñānaghana). The Praśna closes with the same two-fold petition characteristic of late-Upaniṣadic concluding prayers."]],"notes":["End of Praśna 2."]}]},{"t":"Praśna 3 — Kausalya Āśvalāyana: The genesis and physiology of Prāṇa (12 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Praśna 3","title":"Kausalya Āśvalāyana: The genesis and physiology of Prāṇa (12 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Where Praśna 2 established Prāṇa as the foremost of the body's devas by a dramatic demonstration, Praśna 3 turns to the technical question: how does Prāṇa work? Kausalya, son of Aśvala, asks five sub-questions in 3.1 — whence is Prāṇa born? how does he enter the body? how, having divided himself, does he abide? how does he depart? how does he support the outer and the inner? — and Pippalāda answers them in order. The result is the most compact and authoritative Upaniṣadic prāṇa-anatomy: a five-fold functional division (prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna), a 101-nāḍī inner topography, and an explicit alignment of inner prāṇa-divisions with outer cosmic powers (sun, earth, ākāśa, wind)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 3.1","title":"Kausalya's five-fold question on Prāṇa","sa":["अथ हैनं कौसल्यश्चाश्वलायनः पप्रच्छ । भगवन् कुत एष प्राणो जायते कथमायात्यस्मिञ्शरीर आत्मानं वा प्रविभज्य कथं प्रातिष्ठते केनोत्क्रमते कथं बाह्यमभिधत्ते कथमध्यात्ममिति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha ha enaṃ kausalyaḥ cāśvalāyanaḥ papraccha | bhagavan kuta eṣa prāṇaḥ jāyate kathaṃ āyāti asmin śarīra ātmānaṃ vā pravibhajya kathaṃ prātiṣṭhate kena utkramate kathaṃ bāhyam abhidhatte kathaṃ adhyātmam iti || 1 ||"],"words":[["atha","indecl.","then"],["ha enam","indecl. + m. acc. sg.","(addressing) him"],["kausalyaḥ ca āśvalāyanaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Kausalya, the son of Aśvala"],["papraccha","3 sg. pf.","asked"],["bhagavan","m. voc. sg.","O Blessed One"],["kutaḥ eṣa prāṇaḥ jāyate","indecl. + m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","whence is this Prāṇa born?"],["katham āyāti asmin śarīre","indecl. + 3 sg. pres. + n. loc. sg.","how does (he) come into this body?"],["ātmānaṃ vā pravibhajya","m. acc. sg. + ger.","or, having divided himself"],["kathaṃ prātiṣṭhate","indecl. + 3 sg. pres. mid.","how does (he) abide?"],["kena utkramate","indecl. instr. + 3 sg. pres.","by what (means) does (he) depart?"],["kathaṃ bāhyam abhidhatte","indecl. + n. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres. mid.","how does (he) sustain the outer?"],["kathaṃ adhyātmam","indecl. + n. acc. sg.","how the inner (= adhyātma)?"],["iti","indecl.","(end of question)"]],"lit":"“Then Kausalya, the son of Aśvala, asked him: ‘Bhagavan, whence is this Prāṇa born? How does he come into this body? How, having divided himself, does he abide? By what (means) does he depart? How does he sustain the outer? How (does he sustain) the inner?’”","para":"Kausalya asks five sub-questions: (1) origin of Prāṇa; (2) entry into the body; (3) self-division and abiding; (4) mode of departure; (5) the outer-and-inner correspondence. The whole of Praśna 3 will be Pippalāda's structured answer to these five.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The five questions are not random but cover the complete prāṇa-vidyā: utpatti (origin), āyāti (entry), pravibhāga-pratiṣṭhā (self-division and abidance), utkramaṇa (departure), and bāhya-adhyātma-yoga (outer-inner correspondence). Pippalāda's answer in 3.3 onwards will track these five in order. Cf. the summary verse 3.12 below: ‘utpattim āyatim sthānaṃ vibhutvaṃ cāpi pañcadhā / adhyātmaṃ caiva prāṇasya’ — exactly Kausalya's five."],["Sharvananda","‘kena utkramate’ is the deepest of the five — by what means / by what instrumentation does Prāṇa depart? Pippalāda will answer in 3.7–3.10 that the answer is udāna, joined to the conscious thought of the dying person."],["Sivananda","The five questions are the five contemplation-points of any prāṇa-meditation. Daily ask: where does my breath come from? how did it enter me? what holds it here? by what would it leave? how does it relate inner to outer?"],["Krishnananda","Note Kausalya's careful patronymic — he is named son of Aśvala (the famous priest who appears at Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.1 as Janaka's hotṛ). Kausalya is a hereditary ritualist, and his question is therefore the most technically demanding of the six — concerned with the precise mechanics of life-and-death."],["Deussen","The five-fold question structure is rare in the Upaniṣadic corpus — usually questioners ask one thing at a time. Kausalya's bundle suggests, says Deussen, an editorial composition: Pippalāda's answer is so long and technical that the questions had to be pre-bundled to set up the structure."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.2","title":"Pippalāda's praise: ‘you ask the most exalted questions’","sa":["तस्मै स होवाच । अतिप्रश्नान्पृच्छसि ब्रह्मिष्ठोऽसीति तस्मात्तेऽहं ब्रवीमि ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["tasmai sa hovāca | ati-praśnān pṛcchasi brahmiṣṭhaḥ asi iti tasmāt te ahaṃ bravīmi || 2 ||"],"words":[["tasmai sa hovāca","m. dat. sg. + indecl. + 3 sg. pf.","to him he said"],["ati-praśnān","m. acc. pl.","the most exalted questions"],["pṛcchasi","2 sg. pres.","you ask"],["brahmiṣṭhaḥ asi","m. nom. sg. superl. + 2 sg. pres.","you are most-established-in-Brahman"],["iti tasmāt te ahaṃ bravīmi","indecl. + 2 sg. dat. + 1 sg. + 1 sg. pres.","therefore I tell you"]],"lit":"“To him he said: ‘You ask the most exalted questions; you are most-established-in-Brahman. Therefore I tell you.’”","para":"Pippalāda concedes Kausalya's questions are of a higher order (ati-praśna = ‘beyond-question’, i.e. questions that approach what cannot ordinarily be asked) and that Kausalya is therefore qualified (brahmiṣṭha = most established in Brahman) — and only on that ground does he agree to answer.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘ati-praśna’ is the technical word for a question that exceeds the proper scope of inquiry — usually unanswerable, or answerable only to the qualified. Pippalāda implies that he would have refused if Kausalya were not brahmiṣṭha. The verse establishes the principle that prāṇa-physiology is not a publicly available teaching but a quasi-esoteric one."],["Sharvananda","‘brahmiṣṭha’ — the superlative — is technical: the student who has reached the maximum of Brahman-fixation that a student can reach short of realisation itself. The praśna 3 teachings are reserved for such students."],["Sivananda","‘ati-praśnān pṛcchasi’ — Pippalāda's compliment is itself a teaching: ask not small questions but ati-questions. The Upaniṣadic seeker must always ask beyond the bounds."],["Krishnananda","Pippalāda's praise serves a structural function: it announces that what follows is technical and exacting, and not to be paraphrased loosely. The rest of Praśna 3 is therefore the most precise prose in the whole Upaniṣad."],["Deussen","‘brahmiṣṭho 'si’ is an Atharvan formula of qualification (cf. AV 10.7); it functions as a kind of pre-initiation acknowledgement."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.3","title":"Prāṇa is born of the Self; comes by mind-act","sa":["आत्मन एष प्राणो जायते । यथैषा पुरुषे छायैतस्मिन्नेतदाततं मनोकृतेनायात्यस्मिञ्शरीरे ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["ātmana eṣa prāṇo jāyate | yathā eṣā puruṣe chāyā etasmin etat ātataṃ mano-kṛtena āyāti asmin śarīre || 3 ||"],"words":[["ātmanaḥ","m. abl. sg.","from the Self"],["eṣa prāṇaḥ jāyate","m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","this Prāṇa is born"],["yathā","indecl.","as"],["eṣā puruṣe chāyā","f. nom. sg. + m. loc. sg. + f. nom. sg.","this shadow upon a person"],["etasmin etat ātatam","n. loc. sg. + n. nom. sg. + pp.","upon this it (the shadow) is spread"],["mano-kṛtena","n. instr. sg. tp.","by a mind-act / by what is wrought by mind"],["āyāti","3 sg. pres.","comes"],["asmin śarīre","n. loc. sg.","into this body"]],"lit":"“From the Self this Prāṇa is born. Just as this shadow (rests) upon a person, so it (Prāṇa) is spread upon this (Self). By a mind-act (Prāṇa) comes into this body.”","para":"First answer (origin and entry): Prāṇa is born of the ātman, related to the ātman as a shadow to its body — inseparable, conformable, but not identical. He enters this particular body by mano-kṛta (the prior thought-act of the soul that takes embodiment).","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The chāyā-simile is precise — the shadow is not the body, not its substance, yet not separable from it. So too Prāṇa is not the ātman, not its substance, yet not separable from it. ‘mano-kṛtena’ is the technical karman-language: it is by the residual mental act (saṅkalpa-vāsanā) of the previous life that this prāṇa-stream enters this body. The verse therefore links prāṇa-vidyā to the karma-doctrine."],["Sharvananda","‘yathā eṣā puruṣe chāyā etasmin etat ātatam’ — the precise correspondence: the shadow upon the person is just like the prāṇa upon the ātman. Both follow their substance everywhere; neither is its substance. The simile rules out both materialism (prāṇa is not the ātman) and dualism (prāṇa is not separate from the ātman)."],["Sivananda","‘mano-kṛtena āyāti’ — by mind-act it comes. The verse is the scriptural ground for the Yogic conviction that thought precedes breath: shift the manas and the prāṇa will follow. This is the basis of all Yoga psychophysiology."],["Krishnananda","The shadow-simile is unique to the Praśna in the Upaniṣadic corpus. It carries a subtle implication: a shadow has direction but no substance of its own. So too prāṇa has direction (towards-the-body) but no substance of its own — only the ātman has substance."],["Deussen","‘mano-kṛtena’ is the older Upaniṣadic equivalent of the later Sāṅkhya kāraṇa-śarīra (causal body composed of saṃskāras). The Praśna anticipates the technical Sāṅkhya term."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.4","title":"Prāṇa as king, the other prāṇas as appointed officials","sa":["यथा सम्राडेवाधिकृतान्विनियुङ्क्ते । एतान् ग्रामानेतान् ग्रामानधितिष्ठस्वेति । एवमेवैष प्राण इतरान्प्राणान्पृथक् पृथगेव सन्निधत्ते ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["yathā samrāṭ eva adhikṛtān viniyuṅkte | etān grāmān etān grāmān adhitiṣṭhasva iti | evam evaiṣa prāṇaḥ itarān prāṇān pṛthak pṛthak eva sannidhatte || 4 ||"],"words":[["yathā samrāṭ eva","indecl. + m. nom. sg. + emph.","just as a sovereign-king"],["adhikṛtān viniyuṅkte","m. acc. pl. pp. + 3 sg. pres. mid.","appoints those-set-over (his ministers)"],["etān grāmān etān grāmān adhitiṣṭhasva iti","m. acc. pl. + 2 sg. impv. of adhi-sthā + iti","‘rule these villages, rule these (other) villages’ (he says)"],["evam evaiṣa prāṇaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","even so this Prāṇa"],["itarān prāṇān","m. acc. pl.","the other prāṇas"],["pṛthak pṛthak eva","indecl.","severally, distinctly"],["sannidhatte","3 sg. pres. mid.","deposits / appoints"]],"lit":"“Just as a sovereign-king appoints his officers — saying ‘govern these villages! govern these (other) villages!’ — even so this Prāṇa deposits the other prāṇas in their several stations.”","para":"Second answer (self-division and abiding): once Prāṇa has entered the body, he behaves as a sovereign appointing officers. He posts each of the subordinate prāṇas (apāna, vyāna, udāna, samāna) to a distinct functional district within the body.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The samrāṭ-grāma simile re-affirms the doctrine of Praśna 2: there is one Prāṇa, the rest are appointed functions of him. The political simile is significant — Prāṇa rules but does not micromanage; he posts and lets each official run his district."],["Sharvananda","‘pṛthak pṛthak eva’ — severally, distinctly — emphasises that the four sub-prāṇas have non-overlapping districts. The next mantra (3.5) will enumerate them."],["Sivananda","The political simile is, for Sivananda, a meditation aid: visualise prāṇa as the king, the four sub-prāṇas as ministers, the body as the kingdom. Settle into this organisational image during prāṇa-vidyā contemplation."],["Krishnananda","‘adhikṛtān’ — those-set-over — are technically not common ministers but appointed governors of provinces. The word elevates the four sub-prāṇas: they are not menials of prāṇa but his territorial governors."],["Deussen","The samrāṭ-simile reappears in late Smṛti and Tantric texts. The Praśna is the locus classicus for the political imagination of the body."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.5","title":"The five-fold posting: apāna, prāṇa, samāna, vyāna, udāna","sa":["पायूपस्थेऽपानं चक्षुःश्रोत्रे मुखनासिकाभ्यां प्राणः स्वयं प्रातिष्ठते मध्ये तु समानः । एष ह्येतद्धुतमन्नं समं नयति तस्मादेताः सप्तार्चिषो भवन्ति ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["pāyu-upasthe apānaṃ cakṣuḥ-śrotre mukha-nāsikābhyāṃ prāṇaḥ svayaṃ prātiṣṭhate madhye tu samānaḥ | eṣa hi etat hutam annaṃ samaṃ nayati tasmāt etāḥ saptārciṣaḥ bhavanti || 5 ||"],"words":[["pāyu-upasthe","n. loc. du. dv.","in the anus and genitals"],["apānam","m. acc. sg.","(he posts) apāna"],["cakṣuḥ-śrotre","n. loc. du.","in the eye and ear"],["mukha-nāsikābhyām","n./f. instr. du.","by mouth and nostrils"],["prāṇaḥ svayam prātiṣṭhate","m. nom. sg. + indecl. + 3 sg. pres. mid.","Prāṇa himself abides (at the upper openings)"],["madhye tu samānaḥ","m. loc. sg. + emph. + m. nom. sg.","but in the middle (region, around the navel), samāna"],["eṣaḥ hi etat hutam annam","m. nom. sg. + emph. + n. acc. sg. pp. + n. acc. sg.","for this one (samāna), this offered food"],["samaṃ nayati","indecl. + 3 sg. pres.","leads / distributes equally"],["tasmāt etāḥ sapta-arciṣaḥ bhavanti","indecl. + f. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pres.","therefore these (digestive products) become the seven flames (= seven sensory products)"]],"lit":"“(He posts) apāna in the anus and genitals; Prāṇa himself abides at the eye and ear, by mouth and nostrils; but in the middle (around the navel), samāna — for he distributes equally the food that has been offered (into the body-fire), and from that (equal distribution) become these seven flames (= seven sensory functions).”","para":"The five-fold map: apāna sits at the lower openings (excretory and reproductive); Prāṇa proper holds the upper openings (eyes, ears, mouth, nostrils); samāna sits in the middle (around the navel) and distributes the digested food equally throughout, producing the body's ‘seven flames’ — the lights / flames of the seven sense-organs.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The ‘seven flames’ (sapta-arciṣaḥ) are explained by Śaṅkara as the seven openings of the head — two eyes, two ears, two nostrils, one mouth — that ‘flame up’ with sensory activity because they are fed by samāna's equally-distributed food. The image is luminous: samāna lights seven lamps from one central fire."],["Sharvananda","‘svayam prātiṣṭhate’ — ‘he himself abides’ — marks that Prāṇa-proper, unlike the other prāṇas, is not appointed-by-someone-else; he holds his own station personally. This grammatical detail preserves Prāṇa's variṣṭhatva even within the five-fold scheme."],["Sivananda","A precise yogic-physiological map: contemplate apāna at mūlādhāra (lower openings), prāṇa at the throat-head (upper openings), samāna at the navel (maṇipūra). This Praśna verse is the scriptural foundation of Hatha-Yoga's prāṇa-anatomy."],["Krishnananda","‘etat hutam annam’ — ‘this food (that has been) offered’ — uses the technical sacrificial term huta. Eating is being read as homa: food is offered into the body-fire (jāṭhara-agni), and samāna is the priest who distributes the oblation. This is the praśna's most explicit body-as-sacrifice statement."],["Deussen","The five-prāṇa scheme appears for the first time in the Upaniṣadic corpus in fully systematic form here. Earlier texts (Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.5.3, Chāndogya 5.1) speak only of prāṇa-and-apāna or prāṇa-apāna-vyāna; the Praśna is the locus classicus for the canonical five."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.6","title":"The heart, the 101 nāḍīs, and the path of vyāna","sa":["हृदि ह्येष आत्मा । अत्रैतदेकशतं नाडीनां तासां शतं शतमेकैकस्या द्वासप्ततिर्द्वासप्ततिः प्रतिशाखानाडीसहस्राणि भवन्त्यासु व्यानश्चरति ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["hṛdi hi eṣa ātmā | atra etat eka-śataṃ nāḍīnāṃ tāsāṃ śataṃ śatam ekaikasyāḥ dvā-saptatiḥ dvā-saptatiḥ pratiśākhā-nāḍī-sahasrāṇi bhavanti āsu vyānaḥ carati || 6 ||"],"words":[["hṛdi hi eṣa ātmā","n. loc. sg. + emph. + m. nom. sg.","for in the heart (is) this Self"],["atra etat eka-śatam nāḍīnām","indecl. + n. nom. sg. + f. gen. pl.","here are these one-hundred-and-one nāḍīs"],["tāsām śatam śatam ekaikasyāḥ","f. gen. pl. + n. nom. sg. (distrib.) + f. gen. sg.","of those (101), of each one (there are) a hundred (sub-nāḍīs)"],["dvā-saptatiḥ dvā-saptatiḥ","f. nom. sg. (repet.)","seventy-two thousand each (of the 100 sub-nāḍīs)"],["pratiśākhā-nāḍī-sahasrāṇi","n. nom. pl. tp.","thousands of branch-nāḍīs"],["bhavanti","3 pl. pres.","are"],["āsu vyānaḥ carati","f. loc. pl. + m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","in these vyāna moves"]],"lit":"“For in the heart is this Self. Here are the 101 nāḍīs; of those, of each one, there are 100 (sub-nāḍīs); of each (sub-nāḍī) seventy-two-thousand branching-nāḍīs. In these (vast network) vyāna moves.”","para":"The seat of the ātman is the heart; from the heart issue 101 principal nāḍīs (channels); each of those branches into 100, each of those into 72,000 — a total network of approximately 727 million sub-channels. Vyāna is the prāṇa that moves through this network (the all-pervading bodily breath).","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The 101 nāḍīs derive from the heart; one of them — the 101st, the suṣumnā — leads upward through the crown. The other 100 lead outward and laterally. The whole tree-like network (101 × 100 × 72,000) is the highway-system of vyāna, the all-pervading bodily prāṇa. Cf. Chāndogya 8.6.6 (śataṃ ca ekaṃ ca hṛdayasya nāḍyaḥ)."],["Sharvananda","The huge total — 727,200,000 — is not a precise anatomical count but a way of expressing the body's vastness-of-channel as compared with its singular centre. The body is a complex periphery; the heart is the simple centre. Vyāna is what unifies them."],["Sivananda","This verse is foundational for Sivananda's nāḍī-śuddhi prāṇāyāma instruction: visualise the nāḍī-network as you breathe, with vyāna being moved by the prāṇāyāma practice itself."],["Krishnananda","‘hṛdi hi eṣa ātmā’ — the heart-statement — is the praśna's only direct reference to the seat of the ātman. The whole nāḍī-network is therefore physiologically grounded in a metaphysical centre."],["Deussen","The 101-nāḍī doctrine is shared with Chāndogya 8.6.6, Kaṭha 6.16, Maitrāyaṇī 6.21. The Praśna's contribution is the precise multiplication (× 100 × 72,000) — the most elaborate Upaniṣadic statement of the nāḍī-network."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.7","title":"Udāna and the three post-mortem destinations","sa":["अथैकयोर्ध्व उदानः पुण्येन पुण्यं लोकं नयति पापेन पापमुभाभ्यामेव मनुष्यलोकम् ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["atha ekayā ūrdhva udānaḥ puṇyena puṇyaṃ lokaṃ nayati pāpena pāpam ubhābhyām eva manuṣya-lokam || 7 ||"],"words":[["atha","indecl.","now"],["ekayā","f. instr. sg.","by one (nāḍī, viz. the 101st = suṣumnā)"],["ūrdhvaḥ udānaḥ","m. nom. sg. + m. nom. sg.","udāna (which goes) upward"],["puṇyena puṇyam lokam nayati","n. instr. sg. + n. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","by puṇya (he) leads to a puṇya-world"],["pāpena pāpam","n. instr. sg. + n. acc. sg.","by pāpa (he leads) to a pāpa-(world)"],["ubhābhyām eva manuṣya-lokam","n. instr. du. + m. acc. sg.","by (a mixture of) both, (he leads) to the human world"]],"lit":"“Now by one (nāḍī, viz. the 101st), upward-going, is udāna; by puṇya he leads (the soul) to a puṇya-world; by pāpa to a pāpa-(world); by (a mixture of) both, to the human world.”","para":"Of the 101 nāḍīs, one (the upward one, suṣumnā) carries udāna — the upward-departing prāṇa at death. Udāna leads the soul to one of three post-mortem destinations, depending on the moral residue: pure puṇya → puṇya-loka (svarga); pure pāpa → naraka; mixed → re-entry into manuṣya-loka.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The 101st nāḍī is the suṣumnā; udāna's role at death is to traverse it upward and carry the soul with its karmic deposit to the appropriate world. The tri-destination scheme (puṇya / pāpa / mixed) is the Praśna's Upaniṣadic-archetypal account of post-mortem geography, prior to the more elaborate puranic narakas."],["Sharvananda","The mixed-karma → manuṣya-loka rule is theologically crucial: rebirth as a human is precisely the destiny of those whose puṇya and pāpa are balanced. Therefore the very fact of human birth is a sign of moral mixedness — neither saintly nor monstrous, but mid-state."],["Sivananda","The verse warns: be deliberate about cultivating puṇya without an admixture of pāpa, lest the upward udāna deliver only to the human world again. The practical implication is the sat-saṅga discipline."],["Krishnananda","‘ūrdhva udānaḥ’ — the upward-going one — is the only one of the five prāṇas with a directional name. The other four (prāṇa, apāna, vyāna, samāna) are named by function (inhalation, downward-elimination, all-pervading, distributing); udāna alone is named by direction. This signals its post-mortem prominence."],["Deussen","The post-mortem three-destination scheme is the Atharvan formulation; the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka and Chāndogya use the more elaborate pañcāgni-vidyā. The Praśna's compression is significant — it gives the post-mortem geography in a single mantra."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.8","title":"Outer-inner correspondence: sun, earth, ākāśa, vāyu","sa":["आदित्यो ह वै बाह्यः प्राण उदयत्येष ह्येनं चाक्षुषं प्राणमनुगृह्णानः । पृथिव्यां या देवता सैषा पुरुषस्यापानमवष्टभ्यान्तरा यदाकाशः स समानो वायुर्व्यानः ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["ādityaḥ ha vai bāhyaḥ prāṇaḥ udayati eṣa hi enaṃ cākṣuṣaṃ prāṇam anugṛhṇānaḥ | pṛthivyāṃ yā devatā sā eṣā puruṣasya apānam avaṣṭabhya antarā yat ākāśaḥ sa samānaḥ vāyuḥ vyānaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["ādityaḥ ha vai bāhyaḥ prāṇaḥ udayati","m. nom. sg. + indecl. + m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","the sun, indeed, the outer prāṇa, rises"],["eṣa hi enaṃ cākṣuṣaṃ prāṇam anugṛhṇānaḥ","m. nom. sg. + m. acc. sg. + pap.","for he (rises while) favouring this prāṇa-of-the-eye"],["pṛthivyām yā devatā","f. loc. sg. + f. nom. sg.","the divinity that is in the earth"],["sā eṣā puruṣasya apānam avaṣṭabhya","f. nom. sg. + m. gen. sg. + m. acc. sg. + ger.","she, propping up the apāna of the person"],["antarā yat ākāśaḥ sa samānaḥ","indecl. + rel. + m. nom. sg. + m. nom. sg.","what is the ākāśa in between, that (is) samāna"],["vāyuḥ vyānaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the wind (is) vyāna"]],"lit":"“The sun, indeed, the outer Prāṇa, rises — for he rises while favouring this prāṇa-of-the-eye. The divinity in the earth — she, propping up the apāna of the person (within). What ākāśa is in between (above earth, below sun) — that is samāna. The wind is vyāna.”","para":"The fifth answer — the outer/inner correspondence — maps four of the five sub-prāṇas to four cosmic powers: cosmic Prāṇa = Sun (favours the eye-prāṇa); cosmic apāna = Earth-deity (supports the body's apāna by gravity / downward pull); cosmic samāna = the mid-region ākāśa (the diffusive medium); cosmic vyāna = Wind (the all-pervading mover). Udāna (the fifth) is treated in the next mantra.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The outer-inner correspondence is the praśna's adhidaivika–adhyātmika alignment, standard Upaniṣadic technique. Each inner prāṇa has a cosmic correlate: sun ↔ prāṇa-of-the-eye; earth ↔ apāna; ākāśa ↔ samāna; vāyu ↔ vyāna. The sūtra of Praśna 3 is therefore: there is no inner without an outer counterpart."],["Sharvananda","‘pṛthivyāṃ yā devatā’ — the earth-deity — is the deity of gravity, the downward-puller. The body's apāna (excretory, downward) is held in place by this cosmic downward pull. The Praśna grasps the function of apāna in straightforward gravitational terms."],["Sivananda","For meditation: while sitting in prāṇāyāma, become aware of the four correspondences — sun above (prāṇa), earth below (apāna), ākāśa between (samāna), wind in motion (vyāna). One's body is the meeting-point of these four."],["Krishnananda","Note: the eye-prāṇa is specially named here (cākṣuṣa-prāṇa) as the sun's special concern. The Praśna privileges the eye among the senses — perhaps because the eye is, more than any other sense, what physically receives the cosmic Prāṇa daily."],["Deussen","Compare Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.5.21 (adhidaivika–adhyātmika alignment of the cosmic and bodily devas). The Praśna systematises this older Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka teaching."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.9","title":"Udāna as tejas; the dying soul rejoins the indriyas in mind","sa":["तेजो ह वा उदानस्तस्मादुपशान्ततेजाः पुनर्भवमिन्द्रियैर्मनसि सम्पद्यमानैः ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["tejaḥ ha vai udānaḥ tasmāt upaśānta-tejāḥ punar-bhavam indriyaiḥ manasi sampadyamānaiḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["tejaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the (fiery) light"],["ha vai udānaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","verily (is) udāna"],["tasmāt","indecl.","therefore"],["upaśānta-tejāḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","(he) whose tejas has subsided"],["punar-bhavam","m. acc. sg.","rebirth"],["indriyaiḥ manasi sampadyamānaiḥ","n. instr. pl. + n. loc. sg. + pres. pass. part.","with the senses (being) gathered into the mind"]],"lit":"“Tejas, indeed, is udāna; therefore the one whose tejas has subsided (goes to) rebirth, with the senses (being) gathered into the mind.”","para":"Udāna is identified with cosmic tejas (the upward-burning fiery principle). Therefore, when a person's tejas wanes (= dying), udāna's upward force fails; the senses, no longer fed, are gathered back into the mind — and this is the immediate antecedent of departure.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘upaśānta-tejāḥ’ describes the state of dying: when bodily tejas (= udāna) subsides, the senses cannot hold their outward positions and are gathered into the manas — a clinical Upaniṣadic description of the moment before death. The next mantra (3.10) will describe what happens after that gathering."],["Sharvananda","The verse is the Praśna's precise account of how dying happens: not a sudden departure but a phased gathering: senses → mind, mind → prāṇa, prāṇa → tejas (= udāna), udāna → ātman."],["Sivananda","A verse for end-of-life contemplation: when bodily tejas wanes, what remains is the gathering of senses into mind. Practise this gathering daily through pratyāhāra, so that the death-process is familiar."],["Krishnananda","‘indriyaiḥ manasi sampadyamānaiḥ’ — the senses being gathered into the mind — is the same process described at Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.1–2 (Yājñavalkya's death-vision) and Kaṭha 6.10 (the five organs gathered into the mind). The Praśna shares this canonical death-physiology."],["Deussen","‘tejaḥ ha vai udānaḥ’ is the Atharvan identification; the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka and Chāndogya call udāna only by its function (utkrāmaṇa). The Praśna's reduction of udāna to tejas is a step toward later Sāṅkhya-Yoga prāṇa-doctrine."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.10","title":"Whatever the thought at the moment of death, that world he wins","sa":["यच्चित्तस्तेनैष प्राणमायाति । प्राणस्तेजसा युक्तः सहात्मना यथासङ्कल्पितं लोकं नयति ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["yat-cittaḥ tena eṣa prāṇam āyāti | prāṇaḥ tejasā yuktaḥ saha ātmanā yathā-saṅkalpitaṃ lokaṃ nayati || 10 ||"],"words":[["yat-cittaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","(the dying person) of whatever thought"],["tena eṣa prāṇam āyāti","n. instr. sg. + m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","by that (thought) (he) comes to the prāṇa"],["prāṇaḥ tejasā yuktaḥ","m. nom. sg. + n. instr. sg. + pp.","Prāṇa, joined to tejas (= udāna)"],["saha ātmanā","indecl. + m. instr. sg.","together with the ātman"],["yathā-saṅkalpitam lokam nayati","m. acc. sg. + m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","leads to a world corresponding to (his last) resolve"]],"lit":"“He (the dying person), of whatever thought (he is in), by that (thought) he comes to the prāṇa. Prāṇa, joined to tejas (= udāna) and together with the ātman, leads to a world corresponding to (his last) resolve.”","para":"The last thought of the dying person (yat-cittaḥ) is the determinant: it shapes how he comes-to-prāṇa. Prāṇa, joined with tejas (= udāna's upward fire) and bearing the ātman, then leads to the world that matches the last resolve (saṅkalpa). The post-mortem destination is therefore not arbitrary but conformable to the terminal thought.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘yat-cittaḥ’ is the canonical Upaniṣadic statement of the antakāla-citta doctrine — the thought-at-the-moment-of-death determines the next birth. Compare Bhagavad-Gītā 8.5–6 (‘yaṃ yaṃ vāpi smaran bhāvaṃ tyajaty ante kalevaram, taṃ tam evaiti’), where the same doctrine is stated almost verbatim."],["Sharvananda","The verse is the scriptural ground for the death-bed practice across the Indic traditions — the dying person is to be aided to a clear and high thought, since that thought will steer his post-mortem trajectory."],["Sivananda","A verse Sivananda quotes more frequently than perhaps any other. ‘yathā-saṅkalpitaṃ lokam’ — practise daily a clear saṅkalpa for liberation, so that at the moment of death it is already in place."],["Krishnananda","‘saha ātmanā’ — together with the ātman — is important: the ātman is not the agent of the journey but the passenger; prāṇa + tejas is the vehicle. The post-mortem dynamics are therefore prāṇic, not ātmic — though the ātman is what is being conveyed."],["Deussen","Note that this is one of the older Upaniṣadic statements of the antakāla-citta doctrine; the Gītā's statement (8.5–6) appears to draw on this very Praśna verse, since the verbal correspondence is exact."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.11","title":"The knower of Prāṇa: undecreasing progeny, immortality","sa":["य एवं विद्वान्प्राणं वेद न हास्य प्रजा हीयतेऽमृतो भवति तदेष श्लोकः ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["yaḥ evaṃ vidvān prāṇaṃ veda na ha asya prajā hīyate amṛto bhavati tat eṣa ślokaḥ || 11 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ","m. nom. sg. rel.","(he) who"],["evaṃ vidvān","indecl. + m. nom. sg. pap.","thus knowing"],["prāṇam veda","m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pf. used as pres.","knows Prāṇa"],["na ha asya prajā hīyate","neg. + emph. + m. gen. sg. + f. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","his progeny is not diminished"],["amṛtaḥ bhavati","m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","(he) becomes immortal"],["tat eṣa ślokaḥ","n. nom. sg. + m. nom. sg.","now, here is the (summary) verse"]],"lit":"“He who, thus knowing, knows Prāṇa — his progeny is not diminished, (he) becomes immortal. Now, here is the (summary) verse:”","para":"The phala-statement: one who knows Prāṇa in the manner just expounded (his origin, entry, division, abiding, departure, outer-inner correspondence) gains a double fruit: his progeny does not waste, and he himself becomes immortal. The next mantra is the summary śloka.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The double phala — progeny-undiminished and immortal — is the standard Upaniṣadic dvivargya-phala (dual fruit): worldly continuation (prajā = progeny, which is the lower goal of all Vedic ritual) plus mokṣa-immortality (which is the higher Upaniṣadic goal). The knower of Prāṇa gets both."],["Sharvananda","Notes the formula ‘na hāsya prajā hīyate’ — recurs in Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.5.21 and Chāndogya 1.5.4 as the standard phala-statement for prāṇa-vidyā. The Praśna preserves this older formula."],["Sivananda","‘amṛto bhavati’ — becomes immortal — read by Sivananda strictly: this is jīvan-mukti, immortality within this very body, not post-mortem survival merely."],["Krishnananda","The double phala marks the Praśna's reluctance to oppose prajā (progeny / worldly continuation) to mokṣa (release). The two are compatible; both are gained by the prāṇa-knower."],["Deussen","Compares the dual-phala formula with the closing of the Chāndogya's prāṇa-vidyā (1.5–1.7) — almost identical wording. The Praśna and the Chāndogya share a common phala-tradition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3.12","title":"Summary verse: origin, coming, station, fivefold lordship, inner mode","sa":["उत्पत्तिमायतिं स्थानं विभुत्वं चैव पञ्चधा ।","अध्यात्मं चैव प्राणस्य विज्ञायामृतमश्नुते विज्ञायामृतमश्नुत इति ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["utpattim āyatim sthānaṃ vibhutvam ca eva pañcadhā |","adhyātmaṃ ca eva prāṇasya vijñāya amṛtam aśnute vijñāya amṛtam aśnute iti || 12 ||"],"words":[["utpattim","f. acc. sg.","the origin"],["āyatim","f. acc. sg.","the coming (= entry)"],["sthānam","n. acc. sg.","the station / abiding"],["vibhutvam ca eva pañcadhā","n. acc. sg. + adv.","and the fivefold lordship"],["adhyātmam ca eva","n. acc. sg.","and the inner mode"],["prāṇasya","m. gen. sg.","of Prāṇa"],["vijñāya","ger. of vi-jñā","having (fully) known"],["amṛtam aśnute","n. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres. mid. of aś","attains the immortal"],["(repetition + iti)","—","(the closing repetition marks the end of the praśna)"]],"lit":"“Having (fully) known of Prāṇa — his origin, his coming, his station, his five-fold lordship, and his inner mode — one attains the immortal. One attains the immortal. (Thus ends Praśna 3.)”","para":"The summary recapitulates Kausalya's five sub-questions answered above: utpatti = origin (3.3); āyati = coming-into-the-body (3.3, 3.4); sthāna = abiding (3.5–3.6); vibhutva-pañcadhā = five-fold lordship (3.5, 3.7, 3.8, 3.9–3.10); adhyātma = the inner mode (3.5, 3.6). To know all five = to attain immortality.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The double iteration (‘amṛtam aśnute amṛtam aśnute iti’) is the canonical Upaniṣadic mark of chapter-end — repeated emphasis is the syntactic full-stop. The five terms exactly track Kausalya's five questions; this is the structural seal of Praśna 3."],["Sharvananda","‘vibhutvaṃ ca eva pañcadhā’ — the fivefold lordship — names the five-prāṇa distribution as itself the form of Prāṇa's vibhū (lordship-by-pervasion). To know that Prāṇa's lordship is precisely his five-fold distribution is the heart of prāṇa-vidyā."],["Sivananda","A verse to be recited as a daily mantra. ‘utpattim āyatim sthānaṃ vibhutvaṃ cāpi pañcadhā / adhyātmaṃ caiva prāṇasya vijñāyāmṛtam aśnute’ — recite this and review, every morning, your knowledge of these five."],["Krishnananda","Note the closing repetition — ‘amṛtam aśnute amṛtam aśnute’. The doubling is theological: the immortality won by knowing prāṇa is not provisional but final; the Upaniṣad says it twice to remove any doubt."],["Deussen","The five-term summary is the locus classicus of Upaniṣadic prāṇa-vidyā. All later prāṇa-vidyā summaries — Maitrāyaṇī 6.30, Brahmasūtra-bhāṣya 2.4 — refer back to these five."]],"notes":["End of Praśna 3."]}]},{"t":"Praśna 4 — Sauryāyaṇī Gārgya: What sleeps, what wakes? (11 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Praśna 4","title":"Sauryāyaṇī Gārgya: What sleeps, what wakes? (11 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Praśna 4 is the most psychologically rich of the six and the immediate Upaniṣadic prototype of the Māṇḍūkya's four-state schema. Sauryāyaṇī asks five linked questions about the embodied person's states of consciousness — what sleeps, what wakes, who sees dreams, in whom rests sukha, in whom do they all rest? Pippalāda's answer traces a three-tier ascent: from waking (vyutthāna, indriyas active), to dream (svapna, mind active over its impressions), to deep sleep (suṣupti, all gathered into the supreme), and finally to the witness above all three, the akṣara-puruṣa."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 4.1","title":"Sauryāyaṇī's five-fold question on sleep, waking, dream","sa":["अथ हैनं सौर्यायणी गार्ग्यः पप्रच्छ । भगवन्नेतस्मिन्पुरुषे कानि स्वपन्ति कान्यस्मिञ्जाग्रति कतर एष देवः स्वप्नान्पश्यति कस्यैतत्सुखं भवति कस्मिन्नु सर्वे संप्रतिष्ठिता भवन्तीति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha ha enaṃ sauryāyaṇī gārgyaḥ papraccha | bhagavan etasmin puruṣe kāni svapanti kāni asmin jāgrati katara eṣa devaḥ svapnān paśyati kasya etat sukhaṃ bhavati kasmin nu sarve sampratiṣṭhitā bhavanti iti || 1 ||"],"words":[["atha","indecl.","then"],["ha enam","indecl. + m. acc. sg.","(addressing) him"],["sauryāyaṇī gārgyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Sauryāyaṇī of the Garga gotra"],["papraccha","3 sg. pf.","asked"],["bhagavan","m. voc. sg.","O Blessed One"],["etasmin puruṣe","m. loc. sg.","in this Person"],["kāni svapanti","n. nom. pl. interrog. + 3 pl. pres.","what (things) sleep?"],["kāni asmin jāgrati","n. nom. pl. interrog. + m. loc. sg. + 3 pl. pres.","what (things) wake in him?"],["kataraḥ eṣa devaḥ svapnān paśyati","m. nom. sg. + m. acc. pl. + 3 sg. pres.","which deva sees the dreams?"],["kasya etat sukhaṃ bhavati","m. gen. sg. + n. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","whose is this sukha?"],["kasmin nu sarve sampratiṣṭhitā bhavanti","m. loc. sg. + emph. + m. nom. pl. pp. + 3 pl. pres.","in whom indeed do all (these) come to rest?"],["iti","indecl.","(end of question)"]],"lit":"“Then Sauryāyaṇī of the Garga (lineage) asked him: ‘Bhagavan, in this Person, what (faculties) sleep? Which wake in him? Which deva sees the dreams? Whose is this sukha? In whom indeed do all (these) come to rest?’”","para":"Five sub-questions: what sleeps? what wakes? who dreams? whose is sukha? what is the common ground of them all? Pippalāda will answer these in order across the 11 mantras.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The five sub-questions trace the architecture of consciousness: jāgrat (waking), svapna (dream), suṣupti (deep sleep), sukha (the ānanda of suṣupti), and the witness (tūrīya, not yet named). The praśna therefore anticipates the four-state Māṇḍūkya schema in full — but without yet using the term tūrīya."],["Sharvananda","‘kataraḥ eṣa devaḥ svapnān paśyati’ — which deva sees the dreams? — is the central interpretive question of all later Indian psychology. The Praśna's answer (in 4.5) is the mind itself, but operating on the saṃskāras alone, without the indriyas."],["Sivananda","The verse is the daily question for the meditator: who is asleep in me now? who is awake? whose are these dreams? Whose is this comfort I feel just before falling asleep? In whom do all these rest?"],["Krishnananda","Note the use of ‘etasmin puruṣe’ — ‘in this Person’ — rather than ‘in this body’. The Praśna's psychology is purusha-psychology, not body-psychology. The Person is the locus of states; the body is only one of his settings."],["Deussen","The five-fold question is the most precise psychological catechism in the Upaniṣadic corpus. The Praśna here is, in effect, the structural precedent of the Māṇḍūkya's four-state architecture and of Gauḍapāda's analysis."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.2","title":"The setting-sun simile: all gather into the higher deva, mind","sa":["तस्मै स होवाच यथा गार्ग्य मरीचयोऽर्कस्यास्तं गच्छतः सर्वा एतस्मिंस्तेजोमण्डल एकीभवन्ति ताः पुनः पुनरुदयतः प्रचरन्त्येवꣳ ह वै तत्सर्वं परे देवे मनस्येकीभवति । तेन तर्ह्येष पुरुषो न शृणोति न पश्यति न जिघ्रति न रसयते न स्पृशते नाभिवदते नादत्ते नानन्दयते न विसृजते नेयायते स्वपितीत्याचक्षते ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["tasmai sa hovāca yathā gārgya marīcayaḥ arkasya astaṃ gacchataḥ sarvāḥ etasmin tejo-maṇḍale ekī-bhavanti tāḥ punaḥ punaḥ udayataḥ pracaranti evaṃ ha vai tat sarvaṃ pare deve manasi ekī-bhavati | tena tarhi eṣa puruṣaḥ na śṛṇoti na paśyati na jighrati na rasayate na spṛśate na abhivadate na ādatte na ānandayate na visṛjate na iyāyate svapiti iti ācakṣate || 2 ||"],"words":[["tasmai sa hovāca","—","to him he said"],["yathā gārgya","indecl. + m. voc. sg.","as, O Gārgya"],["marīcayaḥ arkasya astaṃ gacchataḥ","m. nom. pl. + m. gen. sg. + n. acc. sg. + pap. gen.","the rays of the sun (when) setting"],["sarvāḥ etasmin tejo-maṇḍale ekī-bhavanti","f. nom. pl. + n. loc. sg. + 3 pl. pres.","all become one in this orb of light"],["tāḥ punaḥ punaḥ udayataḥ pracaranti","f. nom. pl. + indecl. + pap. + 3 pl. pres.","they, again and again, while (it) rises, go forth"],["evaṃ ha vai tat sarvaṃ pare deve manasi ekī-bhavati","indecl. + m. loc. sg. + n. loc. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","even so, all this becomes one in the higher deva, mind"],["tena tarhi","n. instr. sg. + indecl.","by that, then"],["eṣa puruṣaḥ na śṛṇoti …","m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres. (×10)","this person does not hear, see, smell, taste, touch, speak, grasp, enjoy, evacuate, move"],["svapiti iti ācakṣate","3 sg. pres. + iti + 3 pl. pres. mid.","‘he sleeps’ — (so) they say"]],"lit":"“To him he said: ‘O Gārgya, just as the rays of the setting sun all become one in its disk of light, and the same rays, when it rises again, go forth — so does all this become one in the higher deva, mind. Therefore (in sleep) this person does not hear, does not see, does not smell, does not taste, does not touch, does not speak, does not take, does not enjoy, does not evacuate, does not move — they say of him: «he is asleep».’”","para":"The first answer (what sleeps): the sun-rays simile. At sunset all rays are gathered into the sun's disk; at sunrise they re-emerge. So in sleep all the sensory rays — the indriyas — are gathered into the higher deva, manas. The person therefore neither hears, sees, smells, tastes, touches, speaks, grasps, enjoys, evacuates, nor moves: this is what is called ‘sleeping’.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The list of ten negations (na śṛṇoti … na iyāyate) covers all five jñānendriyas (hearing, seeing, smelling, tasting, touching) and all five karmendriyas (speaking, grasping, enjoying, evacuating, moving) — the canonical ten. ‘pare deve manasi’ — the higher deva, mind — names manas as the supreme of the senses, not as the supreme of the person; the Praśna will go higher in 4.6 onward."],["Sharvananda","The setting-sun simile is among the most pedagogically vivid Upaniṣadic images. The rays are not extinguished at sunset; they are reabsorbed into their source. The same is true of the senses in sleep — they are not extinguished but gathered."],["Sivananda","The verse is a meditation aid: at the moment of falling asleep, observe how each of the ten faculties withdraws into the mind. Daily attention to this withdrawal is the foundation of conscious sleep (yoga-nidrā)."],["Krishnananda","‘pare deve manasi’ is striking — manas is called pare deve (in the higher deva). The Praśna is distinguishing between manas-as-faculty (one of the indriyas) and manas-as-pare-deva (the higher gathering principle). The same word does double duty."],["Deussen","The setting-sun simile is unique to the Praśna; later Upaniṣads (Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.3 onward) use other similes. The Praśna's image stands on its own — a poetic achievement of the late Atharvan period."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.3","title":"The prāṇa-fires remain awake in the city of the body","sa":["प्राणाग्नय एवैतस्मिन्पुरे जाग्रति । गार्हपत्यो ह वा एषोऽपानो व्यानोऽन्वाहार्यपचनो यद्गार्हपत्यात्प्रणीयते प्रणयनादाहवनीयः प्राणः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["prāṇa-agnayaḥ eva etasmin pure jāgrati | gārhapatyaḥ ha vai eṣaḥ apānaḥ vyānaḥ anvāhārya-pacanaḥ yat gārhapatyāt praṇīyate praṇayanāt āhavanīyaḥ prāṇaḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["prāṇa-agnayaḥ eva","m. nom. pl. tp. + emph.","the prāṇa-fires alone"],["etasmin pure jāgrati","n. loc. sg. + 3 pl. pres.","wake in this city (= body)"],["gārhapatyaḥ ha vai eṣaḥ apānaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the householder's-fire (gārhapatya) (is) this apāna"],["vyānaḥ anvāhārya-pacanaḥ","m. nom. sg.","vyāna (is) the southern-fire (anvāhārya-pacana, the dakṣiṇāgni)"],["yat gārhapatyāt praṇīyate","rel. + m. abl. sg. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","(the fire) that is led forth from the gārhapatya"],["praṇayanāt āhavanīyaḥ prāṇaḥ","n. abl. sg. + m. nom. sg. + m. nom. sg.","by reason of (that) leading-forth, the āhavanīya (= the eastern fire) is prāṇa"]],"lit":"“Only the prāṇa-fires remain awake in this city (of the body). The gārhapatya-fire is verily this apāna; the anvāhārya-pacana (= dakṣiṇāgni, southern fire) is vyāna; (the fire) that is led forth from the gārhapatya — by that very leading-forth, the āhavanīya (eastern fire) is Prāṇa.”","para":"Second answer (what wakes in sleep): the prāṇa-fires. The Upaniṣad maps the body's prāṇa-functions onto the three sacred fires of the Vedic agnihotra: gārhapatya (the household fire, kept always alight) = apāna (the persistently downward, supporting fire); anvāhārya-pacana = dakṣiṇāgni (southern fire, used for offerings to the fathers) = vyāna (the lateral / all-pervading); āhavanīya (the eastern offering-fire, kindled from the gārhapatya for each oblation) = Prāṇa proper (the inhalation that is ‘led forth’ for each fresh act).","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The body-as-city (puram) and the prāṇas-as-fires (agni) is the Praśna's compressed adhiyajña-vidyā: the body itself is the yajña-vāṭa (sacrificial enclosure), with its three fires ever-burning. The agnihotra is therefore not just a ritual but a continuous bodily process. The verse is the locus classicus of the Upaniṣadic body-as-sacrifice doctrine."],["Sharvananda","The technical fire-names — gārhapatya, anvāhārya-pacana, āhavanīya — are exact Vedic ritual terms. The Praśna assumes the reader knows the agnihotra; the mapping is therefore accessible only to those trained in śrauta ritual."],["Sivananda","A meditation: as you fall asleep, attend to the three fires that remain alive — apāna at the lower openings (gārhapatya), vyāna throughout the body (dakṣiṇāgni), prāṇa at the upper openings (āhavanīya). The body is the yajña-śālā; the breaths are the priests."],["Krishnananda","Note the etymological argument: āhavanīya is so called because it is the praṇayana of the gārhapatya — the ‘leading-forth’ of the household fire to the offering-fire. The Praśna preserves the canonical Vedic etymology, but turns it into prāṇa-doctrine: prāṇa is the led-forth of apāna."],["Deussen","Compare Chāndogya 5.19–24 (the prāṇāgnihotra of Uddālaka). The Praśna's formulation is a compressed Atharvan version. Both texts share the doctrine that the body's prāṇas are the perpetual sacrifice and need not be supplemented by external oblation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.4","title":"Samāna as the equalizer; udāna as the leader; the threefold yajña","sa":["यदुच्छ्वासनिश्वासावेतावाहुती समं नयतीति स समानः । मनो ह वाव यजमानः । इष्टफलमेवोदानः स एनं यजमानमहरहर्ब्रह्म गमयति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["yat ucchvāsa-niḥśvāsau etau āhutī samaṃ nayati iti sa samānaḥ | manaḥ ha vāva yajamānaḥ | iṣṭa-phalam eva udānaḥ sa enaṃ yajamānam aharahaḥ brahma gamayati || 4 ||"],"words":[["yat ucchvāsa-niḥśvāsau","n. acc. du. dv.","the two — exhalation and inhalation"],["etau āhutī","f. acc. du.","these two oblations"],["samaṃ nayati","adv. + 3 sg. pres.","(he who) leads equally"],["iti sa samānaḥ","—","that is samāna"],["manaḥ ha vāva yajamānaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the mind, indeed, (is) the sacrificer"],["iṣṭa-phalam eva udānaḥ","n. nom. sg. tp. + m. nom. sg.","the fruit-of-the-sacrifice itself (is) udāna"],["sa enam yajamānam","m. nom. sg. + m. acc. sg.","he, this sacrificer"],["aharahaḥ brahma gamayati","adv. + n. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres. caus.","day by day, (causes him) to reach Brahman"]],"lit":"“He who leads these two oblations — exhalation and inhalation — equally, he is samāna. The mind, indeed, is the sacrificer. The fruit-of-the-sacrifice (iṣṭa-phala) is udāna; he (udāna), day by day, leads this sacrificer (the mind) to Brahman.”","para":"Continuing the body-as-sacrifice: exhalation and inhalation are the two āhutis (oblations); samāna is the priest who equalises them (the integrator-fire); the mind is the yajamāna (the sponsoring sacrificer who keeps the sacrifice running); udāna is the iṣṭa-phala — the very fruit of the sacrifice — for it leads the mind, day after day, to Brahman during deep sleep.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The verse extends the body-as-yajña to its fullest. Udāna's role here is the most exalted — he is the iṣṭa-phala, the bearer-of-fruit, the daily conveyor of the mind to Brahman. ‘aharahaḥ brahma gamayati’ — daily takes (him) to Brahman — refers to suṣupti, the daily entry into Brahman."],["Sharvananda","The phrase ‘aharahaḥ brahma gamayati’ is the Praśna's most explicit statement of suṣupti-as-daily-Brahman-touch. Every night, in deep sleep, the soul touches Brahman; udāna is the conveyer. The cosmic-soteriological lesson: liberation is not foreign — we taste it nightly."],["Sivananda","A core verse for his ‘conscious sleep’ instruction. Watch the moment of falling asleep; observe udāna's lifting of the mind into Brahman. Awareness of this conveyance is the seed of jīvan-mukti."],["Krishnananda","The samāna's function — to equalise the in-breath and out-breath — is the Upaniṣad's earliest articulation of the integrative principle. Samāna is not a third breath alongside prāṇa and apāna; he is the equaliser who makes them complementary."],["Deussen","‘aharahar brahma gamayati’ — daily to Brahman — is, says Deussen, the seed of the Vedāntic suṣupti-doctrine: every dreamless sleep is a brief return to ātman-Brahman. The Praśna pre-articulates the doctrine that Śaṅkara will systematise in his Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā commentary."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.5","title":"The dream-deva: re-experiences all, real and unreal","sa":["अत्रैष देवः स्वप्ने महिमानमनुभवति । यद्दृष्टं दृष्टमनुपश्यति श्रुतꣳ श्रुतमेवार्थमनुशृणोति देशदिगन्तरैश्च प्रत्यनुभूतं पुनः पुनः प्रत्यनुभवति दृष्टं चादृष्टं च श्रुतं चाश्रुतं चानुभूतं चाननुभूतं च सच्चासच्च सर्वं पश्यति सर्वः पश्यति ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["atra eṣa devaḥ svapne mahimānam anubhavati | yat dṛṣṭaṃ dṛṣṭam anupaśyati śrutaṃ śrutam eva artham anuśṛṇoti deśa-dig-antaraiḥ ca pratyanubhūtaṃ punaḥ punaḥ pratyanubhavati dṛṣṭam ca adṛṣṭam ca śrutaṃ ca aśrutaṃ ca anubhūtaṃ ca ananubhūtaṃ ca sat ca asat ca sarvaṃ paśyati sarvaḥ paśyati || 5 ||"],"words":[["atra eṣa devaḥ svapne mahimānam anubhavati","—","here this deva, in dream, experiences (his) greatness"],["yat dṛṣṭaṃ dṛṣṭam anupaśyati","n. acc. sg. pp. + … + 3 sg. pres.","what has been seen, that he sees again"],["śrutaṃ śrutam eva artham anuśṛṇoti","—","what has been heard, that very thing he hears again"],["deśa-dig-antaraiḥ ca pratyanubhūtam","m./n. instr. pl. + pp.","and (that which has been) experienced in (various) countries and directions"],["punaḥ punaḥ pratyanubhavati","indecl. + 3 sg. pres.","(that) he experiences again and again"],["dṛṣṭam ca adṛṣṭam ca śrutaṃ ca aśrutaṃ ca anubhūtaṃ ca ananubhūtaṃ ca","n. acc. sg.","seen and unseen, heard and unheard, experienced and not-experienced"],["sat ca asat ca sarvaṃ paśyati","n. acc. sg. + n. acc. sg.","real and unreal — all (this) he sees"],["sarvaḥ paśyati","m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","(being) all, he sees"]],"lit":"“Here this deva, in dream, experiences (his) greatness. What has been seen, that he sees again; what has been heard, that very thing he hears again; what has been experienced in (various) countries and directions, that he experiences again and again. (Both) seen and unseen, heard and unheard, experienced and not-experienced, real and unreal — all this he sees. Being all, he sees.”","para":"Third answer (who dreams): in dream, the deva (the mind, or more precisely the conscious-self operating without the indriyas) experiences his own greatness. He re-perceives whatever has been previously perceived; he creates novel combinations; he sees seen and unseen, heard and unheard, real and unreal alike. In dream, the deva himself is the all that he sees.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The dream-deva is the conscious-self operating on the impressions (saṃskāras) stored from waking experience. Three classes are named: pure recollection (yat dṛṣṭam, that he sees again), recombinant experience (deśa-dig-antaraiḥ — combinations across times and places), and pure imagination (adṛṣṭam, aśrutam, asat — what was never seen, heard, or real). All three are species of the same dreaming-activity."],["Sharvananda","‘sarvaḥ paśyati’ — being all, (he) sees — is the dream's key feature: in dream the seer-and-seen distinction is internal to a single mind. The dreamer is everything in his dream. This phenomenology will become the technical pratyaya of Vedānta dream-analysis."],["Sivananda","An essential verse for dream-yoga. Cultivate awareness in dream: notice that in dream you see what you have seen, hear what you have heard, but also what you have not — the dream world is fed by, but not bounded by, the waking world."],["Krishnananda","‘sat ca asat ca’ — real and unreal — in the dream the distinction is suspended. The dreamer does not distinguish real from unreal; both are simply ‘what he sees’. The Praśna here lays the foundation for Gauḍapāda's much-developed dream-criticism in the Kārikā."],["Deussen","The dream-passage is the most psychologically observed in any Upaniṣad. The careful phenomenology — seen / unseen / heard / unheard / experienced / unexperienced / real / unreal — anticipates modern phenomenologies of dream by two and a half millennia."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.6","title":"When overcome by tejas, no dreams; this is the sukha","sa":["स यदा तेजसाऽभिभूतो भवति । अत्रैष देवः स्वप्नान्न पश्यत्यथ तदैतस्मिञ्शरीर एतत्सुखं भवति ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["sa yadā tejasā abhibhūtaḥ bhavati | atra eṣa devaḥ svapnān na paśyati atha tadā etasmin śarīre etat sukhaṃ bhavati || 6 ||"],"words":[["sa yadā tejasā abhibhūtaḥ bhavati","m. nom. sg. + indecl. + n. instr. sg. + pp. + 3 sg. pres.","when he becomes overpowered by tejas"],["atra eṣa devaḥ svapnān na paśyati","—","here this deva does not see dreams"],["atha tadā etasmin śarīre etat sukhaṃ bhavati","—","now then, in this body, this sukha arises"]],"lit":"“When he (the deva, the mind) is overpowered by tejas (= the prāṇa-light gathered back), here this deva does not see dreams. Now then, in this body, this sukha arises.”","para":"Fourth answer (whose is the sukha): when the deva is overpowered by tejas — the gathered prāṇic-luminous principle drawn back to its source — dreams cease. This is suṣupti, deep sleep. And the sukha (the peculiar pleasantness of deep sleep) is what arises in the body at that moment.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘tejasā abhibhūtaḥ’ — overpowered by tejas — is the technical Upaniṣadic description of the entry into deep sleep. The deva (mind) is not exactly extinguished but ‘overpowered’ (abhibhūta) — covered over — by the prāṇic radiance returning to its source. The sukha that arises is not produced by an object; it is the natural state of the mind freed from the object-impressions."],["Sharvananda","The ‘sukha of suṣupti’ is the canonical Upaniṣadic anchor of the ānanda-mīmāṃsā. Even pre-Advaita, it was understood that the universal preference for deep sleep over dream — even though deep sleep is the absence of any objective content — proves that ānanda is the nature of the self, not a product of any object."],["Sivananda","The verse is the scriptural foundation for the daily practice of observing deep sleep: at the moment of slipping into dreamless sleep, attend to the sukha that arises. This is a glimpse of the natural ānanda of the ātman."],["Krishnananda","‘etasmin śarīre etat sukham bhavati’ — ‘in this body this sukha arises’ — note that the sukha is located ‘in this body’, not in the supreme ātman. This locates the sukha at the kāraṇa-śarīra level (the causal body, ānandamaya-kośa) — not yet at the ātman proper."],["Deussen","The triadic state-analysis — waking (4.2), dream (4.5), deep sleep (4.6) — is here for the first time fully delineated as a doctrine. The Māṇḍūkya's later addition of tūrīya completes a structure that the Praśna leaves three-tiered."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.7","title":"Birds returning to the tree at evening — all rest in the supreme ātman","sa":["स यथा सोम्य वयांसि वासोवृक्षꣳ संप्रतिष्ठन्ते । एवꣳ ह वै तत्सर्वं पर आत्मनि संप्रतिष्ठते ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["sa yathā somya vayāṃsi vāso-vṛkṣaṃ sampratiṣṭhante | evaṃ ha vai tat sarvaṃ pare ātmani sampratiṣṭhate || 7 ||"],"words":[["sa yathā somya","—","now, just as, O dear (one)"],["vayāṃsi vāso-vṛkṣaṃ sampratiṣṭhante","n. nom. pl. + m. acc. sg. tp. + 3 pl. pres. mid.","birds gather to (their) roosting-tree"],["evaṃ ha vai tat sarvaṃ pare ātmani sampratiṣṭhate","—","even so all this gathers in the supreme Ātman"]],"lit":"“Now, just as birds, O dear one, gather to (their) roosting-tree (at evening), even so all this gathers in the supreme Ātman.”","para":"Fifth answer (in whom do all rest): the bird-and-tree simile. As birds return at sundown to the tree where they sleep, so do all the contents of consciousness — senses, mind, prāṇas, all — return at the close of waking to the supreme Ātman. The Ātman is therefore the common ground (sampratiṣṭhā) of all that has been catalogued in 4.2–4.6.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The bird-and-tree simile is among the most famous in the Upaniṣadic corpus. ‘pare ātmani sampratiṣṭhate’ — ‘gathers in the supreme Ātman’ — is the verse's answer to Sauryāyaṇī's last question: the ground of all states is not manas (the higher deva of waking), not the mind that dreams, not the tejas of suṣupti, but the supreme Ātman beyond all three."],["Sharvananda","The vocative ‘somya’ — ‘O dear one’ — is the Chāndogya's famous address-form (cf. tat tvam asi, repeated nine times in Chāndogya 6 with this vocative). The Praśna's borrowing of the address marks its location in the Chāndogya-Praśna shared tradition."],["Sivananda","A verse for evening practice: as you sit in evening meditation, watch your faculties returning home like birds. Each thought, each sense-impression, comes back to its roost in the Ātman."],["Krishnananda","The verse establishes the supreme Ātman as the natural ground of all states. Note: ‘pare ātmani’ — in the supreme — is distinguished from ‘pare deve manasi’ of 4.2. The Praśna explicitly distinguishes the supreme of the senses (manas) from the supreme of the person (ātman)."],["Deussen","The bird-and-tree simile recurs at Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.3.18, where it is applied to suṣupti specifically. The Praśna's broader application — ‘all this’, not just suṣupti — is the older, less analytical use."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.8","title":"The list of elements, sense-organs, faculties, and their objects","sa":["पृथिवी च पृथिवीमात्रा चापश्चापोमात्रा च तेजश्च तेजोमात्रा च वायुश्च वायुमात्रा चाकाशश्चाकाशमात्रा च चक्षुश्च द्रष्टव्यं च श्रोत्रं च श्रोतव्यं च घ्राणं च घ्रातव्यं च रसश्च रसयितव्यं च त्वक्च स्पर्शयितव्यं च वाक्च वक्तव्यं च हस्तौ चादातव्यं चोपस्थश्चानन्दयितव्यं च पायुश्च विसर्जयितव्यं च पादौ च गन्तव्यं च मनश्च मन्तव्यं च बुद्धिश्च बोद्धव्यं चाहंकारश्चाहंकर्तव्यं च चित्तं च चेतयितव्यं च तेजश्च विद्योतयितव्यं च प्राणश्च विधारयितव्यं च ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["pṛthivī ca pṛthivī-mātrā ca āpaḥ ca āpo-mātrā ca tejaḥ ca tejo-mātrā ca vāyuḥ ca vāyu-mātrā ca ākāśaḥ ca ākāśa-mātrā ca | cakṣuḥ ca draṣṭavyaṃ ca śrotraṃ ca śrotavyaṃ ca ghrāṇaṃ ca ghrātavyaṃ ca rasaḥ ca rasayitavyaṃ ca tvak ca sparśayitavyaṃ ca | vāk ca vaktavyaṃ ca hastau ca ādātavyaṃ ca upasthaḥ ca ānandayitavyaṃ ca pāyuḥ ca visarjayitavyaṃ ca pādau ca gantavyaṃ ca | manaḥ ca mantavyaṃ ca buddhiḥ ca boddhavyaṃ ca ahaṃkāraḥ ca ahaṃkartavyaṃ ca cittam ca cetayitavyaṃ ca tejaḥ ca vidyotayitavyaṃ ca prāṇaḥ ca vidhārayitavyaṃ ca || 8 ||"],"words":[["pṛthivī … ākāśa-mātrā ca","f./m./n. nom. sg.","the five elements and their five mātrās (= tanmātrās): earth & earth-essence, water & water-essence, fire & fire-essence, wind & wind-essence, ākāśa & ākāśa-essence"],["cakṣuḥ … sparśayitavyaṃ ca","n. nom. sg.","the five jñānendriyas and their five objects: eye & the-to-be-seen, ear & the-to-be-heard, smell & the-to-be-smelt, taste & the-to-be-tasted, skin & the-to-be-touched"],["vāk … gantavyaṃ ca","—","the five karmendriyas and their objects: speech & the-to-be-spoken, hands & the-to-be-grasped, genitals & the-to-be-enjoyed, anus & the-to-be-excreted, feet & the-to-be-walked"],["manaḥ … vidhārayitavyaṃ ca","—","the four inner instruments and other principles, each with its function-object: mind & the-to-be-thought, intellect & the-to-be-discerned, ahaṃkāra & the-to-be-self-arrogated, citta & the-to-be-made-conscious, tejas & the-to-be-illumined, prāṇa & the-to-be-sustained"]],"lit":"“(In him rest:) earth and its essence; water and its essence; fire and its essence; wind and its essence; ākāśa and its essence; — eye and the-to-be-seen, ear and the-to-be-heard, smell and the-to-be-smelt, taste and the-to-be-tasted, skin and the-to-be-touched; — speech and the-to-be-spoken, hands and the-to-be-grasped, genitals and the-to-be-enjoyed, anus and the-to-be-excreted, feet and the-to-be-walked; — mind and the-to-be-thought, intellect and the-to-be-discerned, ahaṃkāra and the-to-be-self-arrogated, citta and the-to-be-made-conscious, tejas and the-to-be-illumined, prāṇa and the-to-be-sustained.”","para":"A vast catalogue of what rests in the supreme Ātman: the five elements (with their tanmātrā-essences), the five jñānendriyas (with their objects), the five karmendriyas (with their objects), and six further inner principles (manas, buddhi, ahaṃkāra, citta, tejas, prāṇa — each with its proper function-object). Twenty-six pairs in all.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The list pre-empts the later Sāṅkhya 25-tattva enumeration. The Praśna gives 26 pairs (5+5+5+6) — the 25 Sāṅkhya tattvas plus prāṇa (which Sāṅkhya treats as a function of manas rather than as a separate tattva). The list is therefore older than the canonical Sāṅkhya and represents an earlier proto-Sāṅkhya stratum."],["Sharvananda","The pairing of indriya-with-its-object (cakṣuḥ ca draṣṭavyaṃ ca, etc.) is significant: the Praśna teaches that the indriya rests in the ātman not by itself but together with its object — for the indriya without its object is, by Vedānta-analysis, no indriya at all."],["Sivananda","A verse for tattva-meditation: recite the 26 pairs as a daily inventory, observing that each pair rests, ultimately, in the one supreme Ātman. This is the Upaniṣadic adhyāropa-apavāda exercise in mantra form."],["Krishnananda","The most striking inclusion is ‘ahaṃkāraḥ ca ahaṃkartavyaṃ ca’ — the ahaṃkāra and the-to-be-self-arrogated. The Praśna teaches that even the ahaṃkāra has its proper object (whatever it claims as ‘mine’), and that even that pair rests in the supreme. The supreme is therefore beyond even the ahaṃkāra's reach."],["Deussen","The closing pair ‘prāṇaḥ ca vidhārayitavyaṃ ca’ — prāṇa and the-to-be-sustained — closes the cycle: the body itself is what prāṇa sustains, and even this fundamental pair rests in the Ātman. The list is therefore complete."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.9","title":"He is the seer, hearer, knower, agent — the conscious-Self","sa":["एष हि द्रष्टा स्प्रष्टा श्रोता घ्राता रसयिता मन्ता बोद्धा कर्ता विज्ञानात्मा पुरुषः । स परेऽक्षर आत्मनि सम्प्रतिष्ठते ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["eṣa hi draṣṭā spraṣṭā śrotā ghrātā rasayitā mantā boddhā kartā vijñāna-ātmā puruṣaḥ | sa pare akṣare ātmani sampratiṣṭhate || 9 ||"],"words":[["eṣa hi","m. nom. sg. + emph.","for this one (is)"],["draṣṭā spraṣṭā śrotā ghrātā rasayitā","m. nom. sg. (agent-nouns)","the seer, toucher, hearer, smeller, taster"],["mantā boddhā kartā","m. nom. sg.","the thinker, knower, agent"],["vijñāna-ātmā puruṣaḥ","m. nom. sg. tp.","the conscious-Self, the Person"],["sa pare akṣare ātmani","m. nom. sg. + m. loc. sg.","he, in the supreme imperishable Ātman"],["sampratiṣṭhate","3 sg. pres. mid.","rests / abides"]],"lit":"“For this one is the seer, the toucher, the hearer, the smeller, the taster, the thinker, the knower, the agent — the conscious-Self, the Person. He rests in the supreme imperishable Ātman.”","para":"The eight agent-nouns (seer, toucher, hearer, smeller, taster, thinker, knower, agent) describe the vijñāna-ātman — the conscious-Self that is the experiencer behind all 26 pairs of 4.8. And this conscious-Self itself rests in the para akṣara ātman — the supreme imperishable Ātman beyond him.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The list of eight agent-nouns (draṣṭā … kartā) names the jīva-ātman — the empirical conscious self, the experiencer-and-agent. The Praśna locates him at the second-highest level: above the 26-pair manifold but below the para-akṣara. This is the classical three-tier ontology: tattvas → jīva → para-Brahman."],["Sharvananda","‘vijñāna-ātmā’ here is the canonical term for the jīva in the Upaniṣadic vocabulary; cf. Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.21–22 (‘yad eṣa vijñāna-mayaḥ’). The Praśna shares the term with the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka and uses it for the same referent."],["Sivananda","A core verse for self-inquiry: ‘who is the seer? who is the toucher?’ — the answer is the vijñāna-ātman; ‘in whom does the vijñāna-ātman rest?’ — the para-akṣara ātman. Two levels of inquiry, both pointed."],["Krishnananda","The hierarchy: vijñāna-ātman (the experiencer) → para-akṣara ātman (the unaccountable). The first is finite consciousness; the second is the unborn ground. The Praśna's two-tier consciousness-doctrine is the Upaniṣadic precedent for Śaṅkara's jīva-Īśvara-Brahman analysis."],["Deussen","The eight agent-nouns mark the conscious-Self as agent-of-all-acts. The Praśna here gives the most systematic account in the Upaniṣadic corpus of the relation between the empirical experiencer and the metaphysical ground."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.10","title":"He attains the supreme imperishable — shadowless, bodyless, colourless","sa":["परमेवाक्षरं प्रतिपद्यते स यो ह वै तदच्छायमशरीरमलोहितꣳ शुभ्रमक्षरं वेदयते यस्तु सोम्य । स सर्वज्ञः सर्वो भवति । तदेष श्लोकः ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["param eva akṣaraṃ pratipadyate sa yaḥ ha vai tat acchāyam aśarīram alohitaṃ śubhram akṣaraṃ vedayate yaḥ tu somya | sa sarvajñaḥ sarvaḥ bhavati | tat eṣa ślokaḥ || 10 ||"],"words":[["param eva akṣaraṃ pratipadyate","n. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres. mid.","(he) attains the supreme imperishable"],["saḥ yaḥ ha vai","m. nom. sg.","he who, indeed,"],["tat acchāyam aśarīram alohitam śubhram akṣaram","n. acc. sg. (string of attributes)","that shadowless, bodyless, blood-less (= non-material), pure, imperishable"],["vedayate","3 sg. pres. mid. caus.","knows / brings (it) to knowledge"],["yaḥ tu somya","m. nom. sg. + emph. + voc.","who indeed, O dear (one),"],["sa sarvajñaḥ sarvaḥ bhavati","m. nom. sg.","he becomes all-knowing, all"],["tat eṣa ślokaḥ","—","now, here is the (summary) verse"]],"lit":"“He attains the supreme imperishable — he who indeed, O dear one, knows that shadowless, bodyless, blood-less (= non-material), pure, imperishable. He becomes all-knowing, (he becomes) all. Now, here is the (summary) verse.”","para":"The phala-statement: the seeker who knows the akṣara qualified by five negatives (without shadow, without body, without blood/red colour = without sense-material, pure, imperishable) attains it — and becomes sarva-jña and sarva (all-knowing, and indeed all). The mantra closes by announcing the next verse (4.11) as the summary śloka.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The five qualifiers — acchāya (without shadow), aśarīra (without body), alohita (without redness, the lohita that marks materiality), śubhra (pure), akṣara (imperishable) — describe the supreme as the via-negativa: it is what is not gross, not material, not perishable. The double phala ‘sarvajñaḥ sarvaḥ bhavati’ — all-knowing and all — is the canonical Upaniṣadic statement of the knower's transformation: not ‘he knows all’ but ‘he becomes all’."],["Sharvananda","‘alohitam’ — colourless / blood-less — is a striking negative. Lohita (red, the colour of blood, the colour of mortality) is precisely what the supreme lacks. The Praśna pushes further than the standard ‘colourless’ by naming the colour-that-is-absent."],["Sivananda","A core verse of jñāna-yoga: meditate on the five negatives (acchāya, aśarīra, alohita, śubhra, akṣara) as via-negativa qualifiers of the Self. The very act of negation is the contemplation."],["Krishnananda","‘sa sarvajñaḥ sarvo bhavati’ — he becomes all-knowing, (he becomes) all. The doctrine is identification, not knowledge-by-acquaintance. The supreme is not an object that the knower comes to know; the supreme is what the knower comes to be."],["Deussen","The five negatives recall Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.8.8 (the famous ‘neti-neti’ passage) and Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.20 (‘aśarīram śarīreṣv anavasthāny aveṣṭaṃ’). The Praśna belongs to the via-negativa tradition that culminates in the Māṇḍūkya's seventh mantra."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4.11","title":"Summary verse: knowing the akṣara in whom all rest, one becomes all-knowing","sa":["विज्ञानात्मा सह देवैश्च सर्वैः प्राणा भूतानि सम्प्रतिष्ठन्ति यत्र ।","तदक्षरं वेदयते यस्तु सोम्य स सर्वज्ञः सर्वमेवाविवेशेति ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["vijñāna-ātmā saha devaiḥ ca sarvaiḥ prāṇāḥ bhūtāni sampratiṣṭhanti yatra |","tat akṣaraṃ vedayate yaḥ tu somya sa sarvajñaḥ sarvam eva ā-viveśa iti || 11 ||"],"words":[["vijñāna-ātmā","m. nom. sg.","the conscious-Self"],["saha devaiḥ ca sarvaiḥ","indecl. + m. instr. pl.","together with all the devas"],["prāṇāḥ bhūtāni","m./n. nom. pl.","the prāṇas, the elements"],["sampratiṣṭhanti yatra","3 pl. pres. + rel.","all come to rest in whom"],["tat akṣaraṃ vedayate yaḥ tu somya","n. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres. + rel.","(he) who, O dear one, knows that imperishable"],["sa sarvajñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","he, all-knowing,"],["sarvam eva ā-viveśa iti","n. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pf. of ā-viś + iti","enters into the All"]],"lit":"“The conscious-Self, together with all the devas, the prāṇas, and the elements — in whom these all come to rest — that imperishable, he who knows, O dear one, becomes all-knowing and enters into the All. (Thus ends Praśna 4.)”","para":"The summary śloka: knowing the akṣara — that supreme imperishable in whom rest the vijñāna-ātman together with all the devas, the prāṇas, and the bhūtas — one becomes sarvajña (all-knowing) and enters the sarva (the All). Praśna 4 closes with this declarative verse.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The summary inverts the agency: instead of ‘he becomes all’ (4.10), the closing verse says ‘he enters the All’ (sarvam eva āviveśa). The two formulations are equivalent in import — there is no distance left between the knower and the All — but the second is more spatial / Vedic, the first more identitarian / Upaniṣadic. The Praśna preserves both."],["Sharvananda","‘saha devaiḥ ca sarvaiḥ prāṇā bhūtāni sampratiṣṭhanti yatra’ — recapitulates the 26 pairs of 4.8 in compressed form: vijñāna-ātmā + devas + prāṇas + bhūtas. The supreme akṣara is the residence of all four classes."],["Sivananda","An evening recitation. End each day with this verse, naming the four classes (conscious-self, devas, prāṇas, elements) and resting them in the supreme."],["Krishnananda","The closing word ‘āviveśa’ is in the perfect tense — ‘has entered’. The transformation is complete and irreversible: not ‘will become’ but ‘has already entered’. This perfective is the Upaniṣadic warrant for jīvan-mukti."],["Deussen","Compare the Māṇḍūkya's seventh mantra (the description of tūrīya as that ‘in which the world is dissolved, the peaceful, the auspicious, the non-dual’). The Praśna's closing verse is the structural ancestor of that Māṇḍūkya passage."]],"notes":["End of Praśna 4."]}]},{"t":"Praśna 5 — Satyakāma Śaibya: Meditation on Oṃ (7 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Praśna 5","title":"Satyakāma Śaibya: Meditation on Oṃ (7 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Praśna 5 is the Upaniṣad's most explicit teaching on praṇava-upāsanā (Oṃ-meditation). Satyakāma asks a single concentrated question: he who meditates on Oṃ until the end of life — which world does he win? Pippalāda answers by analysing Oṃ into its three mātrās (a + u + m), assigning to each a Vedic correlate (Ṛc, Yajus, Sāman) and a corresponding fruit (human world, antarikṣa, brahma-loka). The praśna is the immediate scriptural precedent of the Māṇḍūkya's full analysis of Oṃ."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 5.1","title":"Satyakāma's question on Oṃ-meditation","sa":["अथ हैनं शैब्यः सत्यकामः पप्रच्छ । स यो ह वै तद्भगवन्मनुष्येषु प्रायणान्तमोङ्कारमभिध्यायीत । कतमं वाव स तेन लोकं जयतीति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha ha enaṃ śaibyaḥ satyakāmaḥ papraccha | sa yaḥ ha vai tat bhagavan manuṣyeṣu prāyaṇāntam oṃkāram abhidhyāyīta | katamam vāva sa tena lokaṃ jayati iti || 1 ||"],"words":[["atha","indecl.","then"],["ha enam","indecl. + m. acc. sg.","(addressing) him"],["śaibyaḥ satyakāmaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Satyakāma of the Śaibya lineage"],["papraccha","3 sg. pf.","asked"],["saḥ yaḥ ha vai tat","m. nom. sg. + rel. + emph.","he, indeed, who"],["bhagavan","m. voc. sg.","O Blessed One"],["manuṣyeṣu","m. loc. pl.","among humans"],["prāyaṇāntam","m./n. acc. sg.","until the very end (of life)"],["oṃkāram","m. acc. sg.","(meditates on) the syllable Oṃ"],["abhidhyāyīta","3 sg. opt. mid. of abhi-dhyai","should meditate"],["katamam vāva","m. acc. sg. interrog. + emph.","which (world) indeed"],["sa tena lokam jayati","m. nom. sg. + n. instr. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","does he win thereby?"],["iti","indecl.","(end of question)"]],"lit":"“Then Satyakāma of the Śaibya (lineage) asked him: ‘Bhagavan, he who, indeed, among humans, should meditate upon the syllable Oṃ until the very end (of life) — which world does he win thereby?’”","para":"Satyakāma asks one focused question: what is the post-mortem fruit of Oṃ-meditation sustained until death (prāyaṇānta = ‘up to the moment of departure’)?","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘prāyaṇāntam’ is the technical word — until the very moment of departure (prāyaṇa = utkrāmaṇa, going-out). The Praśna's question presupposes that meditation must continue all the way to the death-moment to bear its full fruit; an Oṃ-meditation that lapses before death yields a partial fruit only. The Bhagavad-Gītā's ‘antakāle ca mām eva smaran’ (8.5) is, says Śaṅkara, an echo of this Praśna formulation."],["Sharvananda","‘Śaibya’ is technically descended from Śibi, the legendary king whose generosity is celebrated in the Mahābhārata. The patronymic locates Satyakāma in a royal-priestly hereditary line."],["Sivananda","The question is the householder-yogin's daily question: if I meditate on Oṃ every day until the end of my life, what is the outcome? Pippalāda's answer (across 5.2–5.7) is correspondingly graduated, depending on the depth of the meditation."],["Krishnananda","‘manuṣyeṣu’ — among humans — is specific: the question is about the Oṃ-meditator who is born human and dies human. Not about the divine seer; not about the post-mortem ascended; about the human at the moment of death."],["Deussen","‘abhidhyāyīta’ in the optative-middle is the technical Upaniṣadic verb for the meditator's reflexive identification with the object of meditation. The Praśna treats Oṃ-meditation as not mere mental repetition but as identification."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 5.2","title":"Oṃ is the higher and the lower Brahman","sa":["तस्मै स होवाच एतद्वै सत्यकाम परं चापरं च ब्रह्म यदोङ्कारः । तस्माद्विद्वानेतेनैवायतनेनैकतरमन्वेति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["tasmai sa hovāca etat vai satyakāma paraṃ ca aparaṃ ca brahma yat oṃkāraḥ | tasmāt vidvān etena eva āyatanena ekataram anveti || 2 ||"],"words":[["tasmai sa hovāca","—","to him he said"],["etat vai satyakāma","n. nom. sg. + voc.","this very (syllable), O Satyakāma"],["paraṃ ca aparaṃ ca brahma","n. nom. sg.","is (both) the higher and the lower Brahman"],["yat oṃkāraḥ","rel. + m. nom. sg.","(namely) what is Oṃ-kāra"],["tasmāt vidvān","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","therefore the knower"],["etena eva āyatanena","n. instr. sg.","by this very support"],["ekataram anveti","m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","reaches either (of the two)"]],"lit":"“To him he said: ‘This very (syllable), O Satyakāma — what is Oṃkāra — is (both) the higher and the lower Brahman. Therefore the knower, by this very support, reaches either (of the two).’”","para":"The first answer-statement: Oṃ is both the saguṇa (lower) Brahman and the nirguṇa (higher) Brahman. The same syllable, therefore, can carry the seeker to either — depending on the depth of his meditation. The next mantras (5.3–5.5) will assign each level of mātrā-meditation to its corresponding fruit.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The verse states the canonical Vedāntic doctrine that Oṃ is the symbol of both saguṇa and nirguṇa Brahman, and that meditation on Oṃ can therefore yield either krama-mukti (via saguṇa = Brahmaloka, gradual liberation) or sadyo-mukti (via nirguṇa = direct realisation, immediate liberation). The Praśna will work out the mātrā-graduations to show how the depth of meditation determines the destination."],["Sharvananda","‘āyatana’ — support, foundation, abode — is the canonical technical term for the symbol-of-meditation in the Vedānta tradition. The Praśna's choice of word (āyatana) for Oṃ marks Oṃ as the foundation, not merely the means."],["Sivananda","A core mantra of Sivananda's Oṃ-meditation course: ‘paraṃ ca aparaṃ ca brahma yad oṃkāraḥ’ — Oṃ is both. Meditate on Oṃ and you have already engaged both saguṇa and nirguṇa; depth determines which becomes the resting-place."],["Krishnananda","Note the subtle implication: Oṃ is not a symbol of two different Brahmans, but the single symbol that points to both faces of one Brahman. The duality is not in Brahman but in the meditator's depth of penetration."],["Deussen","The Praśna's identification of Oṃ with both paraṃ and aparaṃ brahma is the structural precedent of the Māṇḍūkya's seventh mantra, where Oṃ is identified with both the manifest tattvas and the unmanifest tūrīya."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 5.3","title":"One-mātrā meditation — the human world","sa":["स यद्येकमात्रमभिध्यायीत स तेनैव संवेदितस्तूर्णमेव जगत्यामभिसम्पद्यते । तमृचो मनुष्यलोकमुपनयन्ते स तत्र तपसा ब्रह्मचर्येण श्रद्धया सम्पन्नो महिमानमनुभवति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["sa yadi eka-mātram abhidhyāyīta sa tena eva saṃveditas tūrṇam eva jagatyām abhisampadyate | tam ṛcaḥ manuṣya-lokam upanayante sa tatra tapasā brahmacaryeṇa śraddhayā sampannaḥ mahimānam anubhavati || 3 ||"],"words":[["sa yadi eka-mātram abhidhyāyīta","m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. opt. + n. acc. sg.","if he should meditate on (only) the one-mātrā"],["sa tena eva saṃveditaḥ","m. nom. sg. + n. instr. sg. + pp.","he, enlightened by that alone"],["tūrṇam eva jagatyām abhisampadyate","indecl. + f. loc. sg. + 3 sg. pres. mid.","quickly comes back to the world"],["tam ṛcaḥ manuṣya-lokam upanayante","m. acc. sg. + f. nom. pl. + m. acc. sg. + 3 pl. pres. mid.","the Ṛc-verses lead him to the human world"],["sa tatra tapasā brahmacaryeṇa śraddhayā sampannaḥ","m. nom. sg. + indecl. + n./n./f. instr. sg. + pp.","there, endowed with tapas, brahmacarya, śraddhā,"],["mahimānam anubhavati","m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","(he) experiences greatness"]],"lit":"“If he should meditate on (only) the one-mātrā (a), enlightened by that alone, he quickly comes back to the world. The Ṛc-verses lead him to the human world; there, endowed with tapas, brahmacarya, śraddhā, he experiences greatness.”","para":"Fruit of one-mātrā Oṃ-meditation (the ‘a’ sound, corresponding to the Ṛg-Veda): he is enlightened only superficially, returns quickly to manuṣya-loka (the human world); the Ṛcs (Ṛg-Vedic mantras, the meditator's accumulated puṇya) lead him there. Once there, by tapas + brahmacarya + śraddhā, he experiences a greater human life — but a human life nonetheless.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The one-mātrā meditation engages only the ‘a’ phoneme of Oṃ (a-u-m). ‘a’ corresponds, in the Māṇḍūkya scheme, to the waking state (jāgrat) and to vaiśvānara. The fruit is therefore appropriately the manuṣya-loka — the world in which the meditator dwelt during his Oṃ-practice. He returns to where he was, but enriched."],["Sharvananda","‘tūrṇam abhisampadyate’ — quickly returns — is the technical phrase. The one-mātrā meditation does not break the bond of saṃsāra; it only refines its quality."],["Sivananda","Many meditators meditate only on the ‘a’ portion of Oṃ and never penetrate to ‘u’ and ‘m’. The Praśna says: their result is genuine but limited — a better human birth. Push the meditation to the full three mātrās."],["Krishnananda","The graduated rule: the depth of mātrā-penetration determines the post-mortem destination. One-mātrā → human; two-mātrā → antarikṣa; three-mātrā → brahma-loka. The grading is mathematical-spiritual."],["Deussen","The triadic mātrā-fruit pattern is the Praśna's distinctive contribution. Earlier Upaniṣads (Chāndogya 1.1, Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 5.1) meditate on Oṃ as one whole; the Praśna is the first to differentiate the fruits by mātrā-depth."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 5.4","title":"Two-mātrā meditation — antarikṣa / soma-loka","sa":["अथ यदि द्विमात्रेण मनसि सम्पद्यते सोऽन्तरिक्षं यजुर्भिरुन्नीयते सोमलोकम् । स सोमलोके विभूतिमनुभूय पुनरावर्तते ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["atha yadi dvi-mātreṇa manasi sampadyate so 'ntarikṣaṃ yajurbhiḥ unnīyate soma-lokam | sa soma-loke vibhūtim anubhūya punaḥ āvartate || 4 ||"],"words":[["atha yadi dvi-mātreṇa","indecl. + n. instr. sg.","now, if by (means of) two-mātrā (meditation)"],["manasi sampadyate","n. loc. sg. + 3 sg. pres. mid.","(he) is established in (the higher) mind"],["saḥ antarikṣaṃ yajurbhiḥ unnīyate","m. nom. sg. + n. acc. sg. + n. instr. pl. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","he is led by the Yajurs to the antarikṣa"],["soma-lokam","m. acc. sg.","the soma-world"],["sa soma-loke vibhūtim anubhūya","m. nom. sg. + m. loc. sg. + f. acc. sg. + ger.","having experienced majesty in the soma-loka"],["punaḥ āvartate","indecl. + 3 sg. pres. mid.","(he) returns again"]],"lit":"“Now, if by (means of) the two-mātrā (meditation, i.e. a + u) he is established in the (higher) mind, he is led by the Yajurs to the antarikṣa, the soma-world. Having experienced majesty in the soma-loka, he returns again.”","para":"Fruit of two-mātrā Oṃ-meditation (a + u, corresponding to the Yajur-Veda): manas-attainment (sampatti); led by the Yajurs to the antarikṣa (the mid-region) = soma-loka (the lunar world). After experiencing vibhūti (majesty, power) there, he returns — that is, he is reborn.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The two-mātrā meditation engages ‘a + u’ of Oṃ. ‘u’ corresponds, in the Māṇḍūkya scheme, to the dream state (svapna) and to taijasa. The fruit is therefore the soma-loka (the lunar world, the world of the moon, the world to which the puṇya-karmins ascend by the pitṛ-yāṇa). It is higher than the manuṣya-loka but still finite; he returns."],["Sharvananda","‘antarikṣa = soma-loka’ — the mid-region between earth and the sun — is the world of those who have practised tapas and ritual but have not realised the supreme. The Yajurs (the karma-pradhāna Veda) are the conveyors."],["Sivananda","The two-mātrā meditator achieves much — vibhūti in the soma-loka — but he returns. The lesson: deepen the meditation to three mātrās before death."],["Krishnananda","The doctrinal point: the soma-loka is finite. ‘punaḥ āvartate’ — he returns again — is the unmistakable signal that this is not mokṣa but only krama-mokṣa stalled at the second tier."],["Deussen","Note that the Yajus-mediated ascent to the antarikṣa parallels the Chāndogya 5.10 (pañcāgni-vidyā) sequence, where the soma-loka is also intermediate. The Praśna preserves the older two-stage cosmography."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 5.5","title":"Three-mātrā meditation — brahma-loka, vision of the supreme Puruṣa","sa":["यः पुनरेतं त्रिमात्रेणोमित्येतेनैवाक्षरेण परं पुरुषमभिध्यायीत । स तेजसि सूर्ये सम्पन्नः । यथा पादोदरस्त्वचा विनिर्मुच्यत एवꣳ ह वै स पाप्मना विनिर्मुक्तः । स सामभिरुन्नीयते ब्रह्मलोकं स एतस्माज्जीवघनात्परात्परं पुरिशयं पुरुषमीक्षते । तदेतौ श्लोकौ भवतः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["yaḥ punaḥ etaṃ tri-mātreṇa om iti etena eva akṣareṇa paraṃ puruṣam abhidhyāyīta | sa tejasi sūrye sampannaḥ | yathā pādodaras tvacā vinirmucyate evaṃ ha vai sa pāpmanā vinirmuktaḥ | sa sāmabhiḥ unnīyate brahma-lokaṃ sa etasmāt jīva-ghanāt parāt paraṃ puri-śayaṃ puruṣam īkṣate | tat etau ślokau bhavataḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ punaḥ etaṃ tri-mātreṇa","rel. m. + indecl. + m. acc. sg. + n. instr. sg.","but he who (meditates) by the three-mātrā (a + u + m)"],["om iti etena eva akṣareṇa","indecl. + n. instr. sg.","by this very syllable, Oṃ"],["paraṃ puruṣam abhidhyāyīta","m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. opt.","should meditate on the supreme Person"],["sa tejasi sūrye sampannaḥ","m. nom. sg. + n./m. loc. sg. + pp.","he, united with (the) light in the sun"],["yathā pādodaraḥ tvacā vinirmucyate","indecl. + m. nom. sg. + f. instr. sg. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","as a serpent (‘he-whose-belly-is-his-foot’) is freed from its skin"],["evaṃ ha vai sa pāpmanā vinirmuktaḥ","—","even so he, freed from sin"],["sa sāmabhiḥ unnīyate brahma-lokam","m. nom. sg. + n. instr. pl. + 3 sg. pres. pass. + m. acc. sg.","he is led by the Sāmans to the brahma-world"],["sa etasmāt jīva-ghanāt parāt","m. nom. sg. + m. abl. sg. + abl. sg.","he, from this dense-aggregate-of-life-itself,"],["paraṃ puri-śayaṃ puruṣam īkṣate","m. acc. sg. + m. acc. sg. + m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres. mid.","(he) sees the supreme Person, who lies in the (heart-)city"],["tat etau ślokau bhavataḥ","—","now, here are these two verses"]],"lit":"“But he who (meditates) by the three-mātrā Oṃ — by this very syllable, on the supreme Person — he becomes united with the light in the sun. As a serpent is freed from its skin, so he is freed from sin. He is led by the Sāmans to the brahma-world. From this dense-aggregate-of-life-itself, he sees the supreme Person — who lies in the city (of the heart). Now, here are two verses.”","para":"Fruit of three-mātrā Oṃ-meditation (a + u + m together, corresponding to the Sāma-Veda): he is united with the light in the sun (tejas), shed of sin like a serpent of its old skin, led by the Sāmans to the brahma-loka; and from the brahma-loka (here called jīva-ghana — the ‘dense-mass-of-life’, the saguṇa Brahman, Hiraṇyagarbha) he sees the supreme Person who lies in the heart-city (puri-śaya). Two summary verses follow.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The three-mātrā meditation engages the full a + u + m of Oṃ. ‘m’ corresponds, in the Māṇḍūkya scheme, to deep sleep (suṣupti) and to prājña. ‘jīva-ghana’ — the dense-mass-of-life — is the saguṇa Brahman, Hiraṇyagarbha, which the three-mātrā meditator attains at the brahma-loka stage. But from there he sees the para-puruṣa beyond — the nirguṇa Brahman. The Praśna therefore distinguishes the brahma-loka attainment (krama-mukti) from the puruṣa-darśana (which is the gate to sadyo-mukti at the dissolution of the cycle)."],["Sharvananda","The serpent-and-skin simile (pādodaras tvacā vinirmucyate) is among the oldest in the Upaniṣadic corpus; cf. Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.7. The Praśna here adapts it specifically to pāpa-vimokṣa (sin-shedding), framing the three-mātrā meditator's transformation as the shedding of all karmic residue."],["Sivananda","A core verse for daily three-mātrā Oṃ-meditation: pronounce a-u-m fully, equally, with awareness of each part, and visualise the serpent-shed-skin imagery as one's own daily pāpa-vimokṣa."],["Krishnananda","Notice the precise phrasing: the three-mātrā meditator at brahma-loka does not become the supreme Person; he sees (īkṣate) Him. The seer–seen relation remains. The complete dissolution of this relation belongs to the sadyo-mukti of Praśna 6, which has no graduation by mātrās at all."],["Deussen","‘jīva-ghana’ — the ‘dense-mass-of-life’ — is a unique Upaniṣadic term, found here and arguably once more at Muṇḍaka 3.1.4. It names Hiraṇyagarbha as the cosmic-life-aggregate, the saguṇa Brahman just below the para Brahman. The Praśna here makes a precise hierarchical distinction."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 5.6","title":"The three mātrās — separately bind, joined liberate","sa":["तिस्रो मात्रा मृत्युमत्यः प्रयुक्ता अन्योन्यसक्ता अनविप्रयुक्ताः ।","क्रियासु बाह्याभ्यन्तरमध्यमासु सम्यक्प्रयुक्तासु न कम्पते ज्ञः ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["tisraḥ mātrāḥ mṛtyumatyaḥ prayuktāḥ anyonya-saktāḥ anaviprayuktāḥ |","kriyāsu bāhya-ābhyantara-madhyamāsu samyak prayuktāsu na kampate jñaḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[["tisraḥ mātrāḥ","f. nom. pl.","the three mātrās"],["mṛtyumatyaḥ prayuktāḥ","f. nom. pl. adj. + pp.","(when) employed (separately) are death-charged"],["anyonya-saktāḥ anaviprayuktāḥ","f. nom. pl. (paired adj.)","(but when) mutually attached, not-separated"],["kriyāsu bāhya-ābhyantara-madhyamāsu","f. loc. pl. + adj. loc. pl.","in the outer, inner, and middle actions"],["samyak prayuktāsu","indecl. + f. loc. pl. pp.","(when) rightly employed"],["na kampate jñaḥ","neg. + 3 sg. pres. mid. + m. nom. sg.","the knower does not waver"]],"lit":"“The three mātrās, (when) employed (separately), are charged with death; (but when) mutually attached, un-separated, in actions outer, inner, and middle, when rightly employed — the knower does not waver.”","para":"First summary verse: the three mātrās of Oṃ, taken separately (a alone → human; a-u → antarikṣa; a-u-m as analytically distinct → brahma-loka with return possible), still belong to the realm of mortality. But when the three are united (anyonya-saktāḥ — mutually clinging, anaviprayuktāḥ — not-separated), in all three classes of action (outer, inner, middle), rightly employed — the knower does not waver. The vibrational unity, not the analytical distinction, is what liberates.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The decisive doctrinal point. The three mātrās analysed-and-separated yield three different finite worlds (5.3, 5.4, 5.5). The three mātrās meditated-as-one yield the supreme. Analytic vivisection of Oṃ is propaedeutic; synthesis is liberation. ‘bāhya-ābhyantara-madhyamāsu kriyāsu’ — in outer (ritual), inner (mental), and middle (prāṇic) actions — names the three classes of conscious activity, all of which must be permeated by the unified Oṃ."],["Sharvananda","‘mṛtyumatyaḥ’ — death-bearing — is a striking term. The separate mātrās are not neutral; they actively bear death. Only their union is amṛta-bearing."],["Sivananda","A daily-practice verse. Whenever you speak Oṃ, ensure the three mātrās are joined (anyonya-saktāḥ); separate them only for analysis, never for practice."],["Krishnananda","The verse is the Praśna's most sophisticated meditation-instruction. It implies: graduated Oṃ-meditation (a, then u, then m, separately) is a learning device, but the final meditation collapses the graduation. The mature meditator engages Oṃ as one continuous vibration."],["Deussen","Comparable to Māṇḍūkya 12 (‘amātraś caturthaḥ’) — the fourth, mātrā-less mode of Oṃ. The Praśna here approaches the same insight from a different angle: not by adding a fourth, but by collapsing the three into one."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 5.7","title":"By Ṛcs this, by Yajurs that, by Sāmans the supreme — by Oṃ all","sa":["ऋग्भिरेतं यजुर्भिरन्तरिक्षꣳ सामभिर्यत्तत्कवयो वेदयन्ते ।","तमोङ्कारेणैवायतनेनान्वेति विद्वान्यत्तच्छान्तमजरममृतमभयं परं चेति ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["ṛgbhiḥ etaṃ yajurbhiḥ antarikṣaṃ sāmabhiḥ yat tat kavayaḥ vedayante |","tam oṃkāreṇa eva āyatanena anveti vidvān yat tat śāntam ajaram amṛtam abhayaṃ paraṃ ca iti || 7 ||"],"words":[["ṛgbhiḥ etam","f. instr. pl. + m. acc. sg.","by the Ṛcs, (one attains) this (human world)"],["yajurbhiḥ antarikṣam","n. instr. pl. + n. acc. sg.","by the Yajurs, the antarikṣa"],["sāmabhiḥ yat tat kavayaḥ vedayante","n. instr. pl. + rel. + m. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pres.","by the Sāmans, that which the seers know (= brahma-loka)"],["tam oṃkāreṇa eva āyatanena anveti vidvān","m. acc. sg. + m. instr. sg. + 3 sg. pres. + m. nom. sg.","the knower reaches it by Oṃ as the (sole) support"],["yat tat śāntam ajaram amṛtam abhayaṃ paraṃ ca iti","n. nom. sg. (series)","that (which is) peaceful, ageless, immortal, fearless, supreme"]],"lit":"“By the Ṛcs, (one attains) this (human world); by the Yajurs, the antarikṣa; by the Sāmans, what the seers know (= brahma-loka). By Oṃ as the sole support, the knower attains it — that which is peaceful, ageless, immortal, fearless, supreme. (Thus ends Praśna 5.)”","para":"The closing summary: each of the three Vedas (Ṛc, Yajus, Sāman) leads to its own loka (human, antarikṣa, brahma-loka). But by Oṃ alone, as the supreme āyatana (support), the knower reaches what is beyond all three: the śānta-ajara-amṛta-abhaya-para — peaceful, ageless, immortal, fearless, supreme.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The closing verse confirms the doctrinal point of 5.6. The three Vedas as instruments yield three finite results; Oṃ as instrument transcends all three. The five qualifiers (śānta-ajara-amṛta-abhaya-para) describe the supreme via a positive-via-negativa: peaceful (not active), ageless (not aging), immortal (not dying), fearless (not anxious), supreme (not exceeded)."],["Sharvananda","The qualifier-chain ‘śāntam ajaram amṛtam abhayaṃ param’ recurs in many late Upaniṣads (cf. Muṇḍaka 2.2.10) as the standard description of Brahman. The Praśna stands in a continuous Atharvan tradition that uses just these five adjectives."],["Sivananda","This verse is to be recited at the close of any Oṃ-meditation session. The five qualifiers form an audible reminder of what Oṃ points to."],["Krishnananda","The structure of 5.7 is a careful inversion of 5.3–5.5: in 5.3–5.5 the three Vedas lead to three different worlds; in 5.7 the same three Vedas are recapitulated only to be transcended by Oṃ alone. The Praśna's pedagogical method: build up a hierarchy, then collapse it."],["Deussen","The closing five-qualifier chain (śānta-ajara-amṛta-abhaya-para) is the locus classicus of the Upaniṣadic via-positiva description of Brahman. Subsequent texts (Muṇḍaka, Māṇḍūkya, Kaṭha) all draw from this Praśna's formulation."]],"notes":["End of Praśna 5."]}]},{"t":"Praśna 6 — Sukeśā Bhāradvāja: The Puruṣa of sixteen kalās (8 mantras)","u":[{"ref":"Praśna 6","title":"Sukeśā Bhāradvāja: The Puruṣa of sixteen kalās (8 mantras)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The sixth and concluding praśna is asked by the most senior of the six students, Sukeśā Bhāradvāja. He recounts an earlier encounter: a Kosalan prince, Hiraṇyanābha Kausalya, had once posed to him a question Sukeśā could not answer — ‘Bhāradvāja, do you know the Person of sixteen kalās?’ Sukeśā now brings the same question to Pippalāda. The answer locates the sixteen-kalā Puruṣa not in any cosmic elsewhere but ‘here, within this body’; traces the genesis of the sixteen kalās from Prāṇa; and closes with the river-and-ocean simile that became one of the most-cited images of the Upaniṣadic corpus."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 6.1","title":"Sukeśā recounts: ‘where is the Person of sixteen kalās?’","sa":["अथ हैनं सुकेशा भारद्वाजः पप्रच्छ । भगवन् हिरण्यनाभः कौसल्यो राजपुत्रो मामुपेत्यैतं प्रश्नमपृच्छत । षोडशकलं भारद्वाज पुरुषं वेत्थ । तमहं कुमारमब्रुवं नाहमिमं वेद । यद्यहमिममवेदिषं कथं ते नावक्ष्यमिति । समूलो वा एष परिशुष्यति योऽनृतमभिवदति तस्मान्नार्हाम्यनृतं वक्तुम् । स तूष्णीꣳ रथमारुह्य प्रवव्राज । तं त्वा पृच्छामि क्वासौ पुरुष इति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["atha ha enaṃ sukeśā bhāradvājaḥ papraccha | bhagavan hiraṇya-nābhaḥ kausalyaḥ rāja-putraḥ mām upetya etaṃ praśnam apṛcchata | ṣoḍaśa-kalaṃ bhāradvāja puruṣaṃ vettha | tam ahaṃ kumāram abruvam na aham imaṃ veda | yadi aham imam avediṣaṃ kathaṃ te na avakṣyam iti | sa-mūlaḥ vai eṣaḥ pariśuṣyati yaḥ anṛtam abhivadati tasmāt na arhāmi anṛtaṃ vaktum | sa tūṣṇīṃ ratham āruhya pravavrāja | taṃ tvā pṛcchāmi kva asau puruṣaḥ iti || 1 ||"],"words":[["hiraṇya-nābhaḥ kausalyaḥ rāja-putraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Hiraṇya-nābha (‘golden-naveled’), a prince of Kosala"],["mām upetya etaṃ praśnam apṛcchata","1 sg. acc. + ger. + m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. impf.","having approached me, asked this question"],["ṣoḍaśa-kalaṃ bhāradvāja puruṣaṃ vettha","m. acc. sg. tp. + voc. + m. acc. sg. + 2 sg. pf.","‘Bhāradvāja, do you know the Person of sixteen kalās?’"],["tam ahaṃ kumāram abruvam","m. acc. sg. + 1 sg. nom. + m. acc. sg. + 1 sg. impf.","to him, that prince, I said"],["na aham imaṃ veda","neg. + 1 sg. pf.","I do not know this (one)"],["yadi aham imam avediṣam","indecl. + 1 sg. aor.","if I had known him,"],["kathaṃ te na avakṣyam iti","indecl. + 2 sg. dat. + 1 sg. cond. + iti","why would I not have told you?"],["sa-mūlaḥ vai eṣaḥ pariśuṣyati","m. nom. sg. bv. + 3 sg. pres.","with-root verily this one dries up"],["yaḥ anṛtam abhivadati","rel. + n. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","who speaks falsehood"],["tasmāt na arhāmi anṛtaṃ vaktum","indecl. + neg. + 1 sg. pres. + n. acc. sg. + inf.","therefore I do not dare to speak falsehood"],["sa tūṣṇīṃ ratham āruhya pravavrāja","m. nom. sg. + indecl. + m. acc. sg. + ger. + 3 sg. pf.","he, silently, having mounted (his) chariot, departed"],["taṃ tvā pṛcchāmi","m. acc. sg. (anaph.) + 2 sg. acc.","(this very question) I ask of you"],["kva asau puruṣaḥ iti","indecl. interrog. + m. nom. sg.","‘where is that Person?’"]],"lit":"“Then Sukeśā Bhāradvāja asked him: ‘Bhagavan, Hiraṇyanābha, a prince of Kosala, having approached me, asked this question: «Bhāradvāja, do you know the Person of sixteen kalās?» To him, that prince, I said: «I do not know him; had I known him, why should I not have told you? Truly the one who speaks falsehood withers root-and-all; therefore I do not dare to speak falsehood.» He, silently, having mounted his chariot, departed. The very (question I ask) of you — where is that Person?’”","para":"Sukeśā tells the story of his earlier failure to answer the same question from a Kosalan prince. Rather than fabricate, he refused to answer; the prince, dissatisfied, departed. Sukeśā now brings the same question to Pippalāda: where is the Person of sixteen kalās?","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The frame story makes three doctrinal points. First, that the question of the sixteen-kalā Puruṣa is one that even an accomplished brahmana cannot answer offhand. Second, that truth-telling (satya-vāda) is more important than appearing to know — the one who speaks falsehood withers root-and-all (sa-mūlaḥ pariśuṣyati). Third, that the question came to Sukeśā originally from a kṣatriya (a royal questioner of a brāhmaṇa), a recurrent Upaniṣadic motif (cf. Pravāhaṇa Jaibali questioning Śvetaketu in Chāndogya 5)."],["Sharvananda","‘sa-mūlaḥ pariśuṣyati yo'nṛtam abhivadati’ is one of the most-cited Upaniṣadic statements on truth-telling. The metaphor is botanical: the one who lies dries up like a tree pulled up by its roots. Sharvananda treats this as a foundational adhikāra-statement: the spiritual aspirant must be incapable of falsehood."],["Sivananda","‘kvāsau puruṣaḥ iti’ — ‘where is that Person?’ — is the fundamental locative question of all spiritual practice. The Praśna's answer (in 6.2) is the most consoling in the corpus: ihaivāntaḥ-śarīre — right here, inside this body."],["Krishnananda","Note the patronymic detail. Sukeśā is named Bhāradvāja, and the prince is named Hiraṇyanābha Kausalya (a Kosalan, of the line of Aśvala). The genealogical specificity grounds the Praśna's authority in real Vedic lineages, not abstract types."],["Deussen","The Sukeśā-Hiraṇyanābha exchange is the only embedded narrative within the Praśna's main dialogue. Its function is rhetorical: by deferring the answer to the sixteen-kalā question, the Praśna heightens the doctrinal climax. The reader has been waiting for this answer since the opening verse."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6.2","title":"The Person of sixteen kalās: right here, in this body","sa":["तस्मै स होवाच । इहैवान्तःशरीरे सोम्य स पुरुषो यस्मिन्नेताः षोडशकलाः प्रभवन्तीति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["tasmai sa hovāca | iha eva antaḥ-śarīre somya sa puruṣaḥ yasmin etāḥ ṣoḍaśa-kalāḥ prabhavanti iti || 2 ||"],"words":[["tasmai sa hovāca","—","to him he said"],["iha eva antaḥ-śarīre","indecl. + n. loc. sg. tp.","right here, within (this) body,"],["somya","m. voc. sg.","O dear (one)"],["sa puruṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) that Person"],["yasmin etāḥ ṣoḍaśa-kalāḥ prabhavanti","m. loc. sg. + f. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pres.","in whom these sixteen kalās arise"],["iti","indecl.","(thus, end of statement)"]],"lit":"“To him he said: ‘Right here, within this body, O dear one, is that Person, in whom these sixteen kalās arise.’”","para":"Pippalāda's startlingly direct answer: the Person of sixteen kalās is not in any heaven or hidden place but within this very body. The sixteen kalās arise in him; the next mantras will trace their genesis.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The locative ‘ihaivāntaḥ-śarīre’ — ‘right here, within this body’ — is the Praśna's most decisive anti-cosmic-elsewhere statement. The Puruṣa is not to be sought in a sky or a heaven; he is in the hṛdaya. Śaṅkara cross-references Chāndogya 8.1 (the daharākāśa) and Kaṭha 4.12 (aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣaḥ)."],["Sharvananda","‘sa puruṣaḥ yasmin etāḥ ṣoḍaśa-kalāḥ prabhavanti’ — ‘that Person in whom these sixteen kalās arise’ — establishes the Puruṣa as the source (prabhava) of the sixteen, not as one among them. He is the principle from whom they originate."],["Sivananda","A core verse for daily contemplation. ‘ihaivāntaḥ-śarīre’ — right here, inside this body. Drop the search outside; turn the attention inward; the Person is already here."],["Krishnananda","The doctrinal point implicit in 6.2 is realised-immanence: the supreme is not transcendent-in-distance but transcendent-while-immanent. The Puruṣa is in the body but not of the body. This is the structural foundation of Vedānta's later sākṣi-vāda."],["Deussen","‘iha eva antaḥ-śarīre’ — the Atharvan locative — parallels the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka's frequent ‘antar-hṛdaye’ (within the heart). The Praśna's broader locative (the whole body, not just the heart) is significant: the body itself is the locus."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6.3","title":"The Person's self-questioning: ‘in whose departure shall I be departed?’","sa":["स ईक्षाञ्चक्रे । कस्मिन्नहमुत्क्रान्त उत्क्रान्तो भविष्यामि कस्मिन्वा प्रतिष्ठिते प्रतिष्ठास्यामीति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["sa īkṣāñcakre | kasmin aham utkrānte utkrāntaḥ bhaviṣyāmi kasmin vā pratiṣṭhite pratiṣṭhāsyāmi iti || 3 ||"],"words":[["sa īkṣāñcakre","m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pf. of īkṣ","he reflected"],["kasmin aham utkrānte","m. loc. sg. interrog. + 1 sg. nom. + pp. loc. abs.","by whose departure"],["utkrāntaḥ bhaviṣyāmi","m. nom. sg. + 1 sg. fut.","shall I be the departed?"],["kasmin vā pratiṣṭhite","m. loc. sg. + pp. loc. abs.","or by whose abiding"],["pratiṣṭhāsyāmi","1 sg. fut. mid.","shall I (myself) abide?"],["iti","indecl.","(thus, end of reflection)"]],"lit":"“He (the Puruṣa) reflected: ‘In whose departure shall I be the departed? In whose abiding shall I (myself) abide?’”","para":"The Puruṣa, before any creation, asks himself the foundational question of identity-by-relation: my coming-and-going is tied to whose coming-and-going? On whose abidance does my own depend? This precedes — and motivates — the creative emission of the sixteen kalās.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The Puruṣa's self-question is a creation-prologue: before producing the sixteen, he asks what their function will be in relation to himself. The answer (implicit in 6.4) is that the sixteen will be the carriers of his presence-and-absence: his utkramaṇa (departure) is by their dissolution; his pratiṣṭhā (abiding) is by their integration."],["Sharvananda","The verse establishes the Puruṣa as reflective, not blind. Cosmogony is preceded by īkṣaṇa (reflection); this is the same pattern as Chāndogya 6.2 (‘tad aikṣata bahu syām’) and Aitareya 1.1. The Praśna's compression is striking."],["Sivananda","A meditation: ask yourself the Puruṣa's question — by whose presence am I present? by whose absence shall I be absent? The Praśna teaches that this very question is the Puruṣa's first creative act."],["Krishnananda","The double future (‘shall I be departed? shall I abide?’) signals that the Puruṣa is not yet active — the sixteen kalās have not yet been produced. The question is therefore pre-creative; the kalās arise as its answer."],["Deussen","The īkṣaṇa-cosmogony is the Atharvan and Sāṅkhya-Yoga signature; cf. Aitareya 1, Chāndogya 6, Praśna 6. The Praśna's distinct contribution is to frame the īkṣaṇa as a question about identity-by-relation, not as a creative wish."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6.4","title":"The sixteen kalās emerge from Prāṇa","sa":["स प्राणमसृजत प्राणाच्छ्रद्धां खं वायुर्ज्योतिरापः पृथिवीन्द्रियं मनः । अन्नमन्नाद्वीर्यं तपो मन्त्राः कर्म लोका लोकेषु च नाम च ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["sa prāṇam asṛjata prāṇāt śraddhāṃ khaṃ vāyuḥ jyotiḥ āpaḥ pṛthivī indriyaṃ manaḥ | annam annāt vīryaṃ tapaḥ mantrāḥ karma lokāḥ lokeṣu ca nāma ca || 4 ||"],"words":[["sa prāṇam asṛjata","m. nom. sg. + m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. impf. of sṛj","he produced Prāṇa"],["prāṇāt śraddhām","m. abl. sg. + f. acc. sg.","from Prāṇa, faith"],["khaṃ vāyuḥ jyotiḥ āpaḥ pṛthivī","n./m./n./f./f. nom./acc. sg.","ākāśa, wind, fire, water, earth (five elements)"],["indriyaṃ manaḥ","n. nom./acc. sg.","(the) sense-organs, mind"],["annam annāt vīryaṃ tapaḥ mantrāḥ karma lokāḥ","n./n./n./n./m./n./m. nom./acc. sg./pl.","food; from food: vigour, tapas, mantras, karman, worlds"],["lokeṣu ca nāma ca","m. loc. pl. + n. nom. sg.","and in the worlds, name"]],"lit":"“He produced Prāṇa; from Prāṇa, faith; (then) ākāśa, wind, fire, water, earth; the sense-organs, mind; food. From food: vigour, tapas, mantras, karman, worlds; and in the worlds, name.”","para":"The list of sixteen kalās in order of emanation: (1) Prāṇa, (2) Śraddhā (faith), (3) Khaṃ (ākāśa), (4) Vāyu (wind), (5) Jyotis (fire), (6) Āpas (water), (7) Pṛthivī (earth), (8) Indriya (the senses, treated collectively), (9) Manas (mind), (10) Anna (food), (11) Vīrya (vigour), (12) Tapas, (13) Mantrāḥ (mantras), (14) Karma (action / ritual action), (15) Lokāḥ (worlds), (16) Nāma (name). The first is closest to the Puruṣa; the last is the furthest periphery.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The order is significant: Prāṇa first (closest to the Self), then faith, then the five elements, then the senses and the mind, then food and its consequences (vigour, tapas, mantras, karma, lokas), and finally name. The list spans from the most subtle (Prāṇa) to the most peripheral (Nāma = the names of the worlds). Śaṅkara cross-references the Praśna's prāṇa-first sequence with Taittirīya 2.1 (annāt … prāṇaḥ … manaḥ …) — the Praśna inverts the Taittirīya's annapūrva sequence, placing Prāṇa at the origin and Anna at the middle."],["Sharvananda","The sixteen are not arbitrary but constitute a deliberate inventory. They cover (i) life (prāṇa), (ii) orientation (śraddhā), (iii) elements (5), (iv) cognition (indriya, manas), (v) life-products (anna, vīrya, tapas, mantra, karma), (vi) cosmos (loka), and (vii) language (nāma). The Praśna's sixteen-fold is therefore the Upaniṣadic anthropo-cosmology in compressed form."],["Sivananda","A daily inventory: trace each of the sixteen within yourself: my prāṇa, my śraddhā, my five elements, my senses, my mind, my food, my vigour, my tapas, my mantras, my karman, my world, my name. Each is a kalā; all are mine; none is me."],["Krishnananda","The inclusion of ‘śraddhā’ as the second kalā (immediately after Prāṇa, before any elements) is the Praśna's most original contribution to Upaniṣadic cosmology. Faith is, structurally, more fundamental than matter — only in the kalā-list of this Upaniṣad."],["Deussen","The sixteen-kalā doctrine of the Praśna is the principal Upaniṣadic source for the later Tantric and Pāñcarātric sixteen-kalā theology. Note: the Praśna's sixteen are not yet the Tantric ‘sixteen lunar digits’ but the cosmological-anthropological sixteen of an emanationist scheme. The Tantric reinterpretation comes later."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6.5","title":"The rivers-into-ocean simile — kalās dissolved in the Puruṣa","sa":["स यथेमा नद्यः स्यन्दमानाः समुद्रायणाः समुद्रं प्राप्यास्तं गच्छन्ति । भिद्येते तासां नामरूपे समुद्र इत्येवं प्रोच्यते । एवमेवास्य परिद्रष्टुरिमाः षोडशकलाः पुरुषायणाः पुरुषं प्राप्यास्तं गच्छन्ति । भिद्येते तासां नामरूपे पुरुष इत्येवं प्रोच्यते स एषोऽकलोऽमृतो भवति । तदेष श्लोकः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["sa yathā imāḥ nadyaḥ syandamānāḥ samudra-ayanāḥ samudraṃ prāpya astaṃ gacchanti | bhidyete tāsāṃ nāma-rūpe samudra iti evaṃ procyate | evam eva asya pari-draṣṭuḥ imāḥ ṣoḍaśa-kalāḥ puruṣa-ayanāḥ puruṣaṃ prāpya astaṃ gacchanti | bhidyete tāsāṃ nāma-rūpe puruṣa iti evaṃ procyate sa eṣaḥ akalaḥ amṛto bhavati | tat eṣa ślokaḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["sa yathā imāḥ nadyaḥ syandamānāḥ","—","now, just as these flowing rivers"],["samudra-ayanāḥ","f. nom. pl. bv.","ocean-bound"],["samudraṃ prāpya astaṃ gacchanti","m. acc. sg. + ger. + 3 pl. pres.","having reached the ocean, come to (their) end"],["bhidyete tāsāṃ nāma-rūpe","n. nom. du. + 3 du. pres. mid. of bhid","(their) name-and-form are broken"],["samudra iti evaṃ procyate","m. nom. sg. + indecl. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","they are spoken of only as ‘ocean’"],["evam eva asya pari-draṣṭuḥ","—","even so, of this beholder (= seeker who beholds all sides)"],["imāḥ ṣoḍaśa-kalāḥ puruṣa-ayanāḥ","f. nom. pl. bv.","these sixteen kalās, Person-bound"],["puruṣaṃ prāpya astaṃ gacchanti","—","having reached the Person, come to (their) end"],["bhidyete tāsāṃ nāma-rūpe","—","(their) name-and-form are broken"],["puruṣa iti evaṃ procyate","—","they are spoken of only as ‘Puruṣa’"],["sa eṣaḥ akalaḥ amṛto bhavati","m. nom. sg. + adj. + m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","this one becomes part-less, immortal"],["tat eṣa ślokaḥ","—","now, here is the (summary) verse"]],"lit":"“Just as these flowing rivers, ocean-bound, having reached the ocean, come to their end — their names and forms are broken; they are spoken of only as ‘ocean’ — even so the sixteen kalās of the beholder (= the seeker), Person-bound, having reached the Person, come to their end; their names and forms are broken; they are spoken of only as ‘Puruṣa’. He becomes part-less, immortal. Now, here is the summary verse.”","para":"The famous rivers-into-ocean simile. Just as rivers, flowing toward the ocean, lose their individual names and forms once they merge — and what was Gaṅgā, Yamunā, Sarasvatī becomes simply ‘ocean’ — so the sixteen kalās of the seeker, who has been moving toward the Puruṣa, lose their distinct names and forms upon reaching him, and what was prāṇa, śraddhā, elements, etc., becomes simply ‘Puruṣa’. The seeker thereby becomes akala (part-less, undivided) and amṛta (immortal).","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The rivers-into-ocean simile is among the three most famous Upaniṣadic similes (alongside the salt-in-water of Chāndogya 6.13 and the spider-and-web of Muṇḍaka 1.1.7). Its central doctrinal claim is the dissolution of nāma-rūpa: when the kalās reach the Puruṣa, their individual nāma-rūpa is broken (bhidyete). What remains is only the Puruṣa. This is the precise Upaniṣadic statement of advaita-mukti — not the seeker's annihilation, but the dissolution of the differentiating nāma-rūpa."],["Sharvananda","‘sa eṣo'kalo'mṛto bhavati’ — this one becomes akala, immortal. Note the inflection: not ‘he becomes akala; he becomes amṛta’ but a single state-change that is both at once. To become akala is to become amṛta; division and mortality are the same thing."],["Sivananda","This verse appears in every major Sivananda lecture on mokṣa. The simile is to be visualised: see the kalās as rivers; see the Puruṣa as the ocean; see the merger; rest in that."],["Krishnananda","‘pari-draṣṭuḥ’ — the beholder-on-all-sides — is a precise term: not just the seeker but the one who beholds his own kalās from every side. The kalās are dissolved not in any other Person but in the very Puruṣa of the seer himself. Krishnananda emphasises: the rivers are the seer's own; the ocean is the seer's own depth."],["Deussen","The simile is paralleled at Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.6 (‘yathā vṛkṣasya puṣpaṃ’) and Muṇḍaka 3.2.8 (‘yathā nadyaḥ syandamānāḥ’), both with the same rivers-into-ocean image. The Praśna's distinctive contribution is the explicit nāma-rūpa-bhidyate formulation: it gives the analytic terminology of dissolution that the other texts only image."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6.6","title":"The summary verse: as spokes in a hub, so kalās in the Puruṣa","sa":["अरा इव रथनाभौ कला यस्मिन्प्रतिष्ठिताः ।","तं वेद्यं पुरुषं वेद यथा मा वो मृत्युः परिव्यथा इति ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["arā iva ratha-nābhau kalā yasmin pratiṣṭhitāḥ |","taṃ vedyaṃ puruṣaṃ veda yathā mā vaḥ mṛtyuḥ parivyathā iti || 6 ||"],"words":[["arāḥ iva ratha-nābhau","m. nom. pl. + indecl. + m. loc. sg.","as spokes in the hub of a wheel"],["kalāḥ yasmin pratiṣṭhitāḥ","f. nom. pl. + m. loc. sg. + pp.","the kalās, in whom (they are) fixed"],["taṃ vedyam puruṣaṃ veda","m. acc. sg. + m. acc. sg. + 2 sg. impv./pf. used as impv.","know that Person — who is to be known"],["yathā mā vaḥ mṛtyuḥ parivyathāḥ iti","indecl. + neg. + 2 pl. acc. + m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. opt. + iti","so that death may not afflict you on every side"]],"lit":"“As spokes (are fixed) in the hub of a wheel, the kalās (are fixed) in him — that Person who is to be known. Know him, so that death may not afflict you on every side.”","para":"The classic hub-and-spokes simile, applied here for the second time in the Upaniṣad (cf. 2.6 with prāṇa in the hub). The kalās are spokes; the Puruṣa is the hub. To know the Puruṣa is to know the centre on which all the kalās depend — and thereby to escape death-on-every-side (parivyathā).","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The repetition of the spokes-in-hub simile from Praśna 2 (where Prāṇa was the hub) here positions the Puruṣa as the deeper hub: behind the prāṇa-hub of the body lies the puruṣa-hub of the Self. The verse therefore moves the meditation one level deeper. ‘parivyathāḥ’ — affliction-on-all-sides — is the strongest Upaniṣadic word for the experience of mortality as enveloping; the Puruṣa-knower escapes precisely this surrounding affliction."],["Sharvananda","‘veda’ is the imperative-of-knowing — not an indicative. The verse is an instruction, not a description. Know — actually know, not merely understand."],["Sivananda","A daily mantra. Repeat the verse with hands cupped at the heart-centre: ‘arā iva ratha-nābhau kalā yasmin pratiṣṭhitāḥ / taṃ vedyaṃ puruṣaṃ veda yathā mā vo mṛtyuḥ parivyathāḥ’."],["Krishnananda","The doubling of the spokes-in-hub simile (2.6 → 6.6) is the Praśna's most significant internal cross-reference. The Upaniṣad is constructed in a deliberate double-helix: prāṇa-hub in the lower praśnas, puruṣa-hub in the upper. Praśna 2 and Praśna 6 are the two ends of this helix."],["Deussen","Note that the verse ends with ‘parivyathāḥ’ in the subjunctive-optative — death may afflict, may not. The construction admits the possibility while warning against it. The pedagogical tone is exact."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6.7","title":"Pippalāda's closing declaration","sa":["तान् होवाच एतावदेवाहमेतत्परं ब्रह्म वेद नातः परमस्तीति ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["tān hovāca etāvat eva aham etat paraṃ brahma veda na ataḥ param asti iti || 7 ||"],"words":[["tān hovāca","m. acc. pl. + 3 sg. pf.","to them (all six) he said"],["etāvat eva aham","indecl. + 1 sg. nom.","(only) this much I"],["etat paraṃ brahma veda","n. acc. sg. + n. acc. sg. + 1 sg. pf. used as pres.","know this supreme Brahman"],["na ataḥ param asti iti","neg. + indecl. + n. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres. + iti","there is nothing higher than this"]],"lit":"“To them (all six) he said: ‘Just this much do I know of this supreme Brahman. There is nothing higher than this.’”","para":"Pippalāda's final statement to the six gathered students. He has taught all he knows; what he has taught is the supreme Brahman; and beyond what he has taught, there is nothing higher. The probationary year and the six praśnas are complete.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘etāvat eva aham veda’ — ‘only this much do I know’ — is not modesty but completeness. The Praśna's six teachings (cosmogony, body-sustenance, prāṇa-physiology, sleep-states, Oṃ-meditation, the sixteen-kalā Puruṣa) together exhaust the para Brahman — there is no other doctrine to be taught. ‘nātaḥ param asti’ — there is nothing higher than this — is therefore a doctrinal completeness-claim, not a confession of limitation."],["Sharvananda","‘tān hovāca’ — to them (all six) he said. The closing is collective. Each questioner has been answered; the closing word is to the assembly. This is the formal seal of the dialogue."],["Sivananda","Pippalāda's modest-yet-firm closing is the model for the teacher: teach only what you know; assert its completeness when warranted; refuse to fabricate. The opening (1.2's ‘yadi vijñāsyāmaḥ’) and the closing (6.7's ‘etāvad eva veda’) frame the whole Upaniṣad with this same teacher-integrity."],["Krishnananda","‘nātaḥ param asti’ is a strong doctrinal claim: the Praśna's teachings exhaust the para Brahman. Krishnananda notes that this is consistent with Pippalāda's earlier ‘brahmiṣṭho 'si’ (3.2): Pippalāda speaks as a brahmiṣṭha, and what he speaks is the whole of what is."],["Deussen","The closing formula ‘nātaḥ param asti’ has direct parallels at Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.3.32 (‘etāvān asyānubhāvaḥ’) and at Muṇḍaka 3.1.10 (‘etāvad eva’). Pippalāda's closing is therefore in the formulaic register of the Upaniṣadic teacher-conclusion."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6.8","title":"Closing salutation: ‘you are our father, who carries us across’","sa":["ते तमर्चयन्तस्त्वं हि नः पिता योऽस्माकमविद्यायाः परं पारं तारयसीति । नमः परमऋषिभ्यो नमः परमऋषिभ्यः ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["te tam arcayantaḥ tvaṃ hi naḥ pitā yaḥ asmākam avidyāyāḥ paraṃ pāraṃ tārayasi iti | namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["te tam arcayantaḥ","m. nom. pl. + m. acc. sg. + pap.","they, worshipping him"],["tvaṃ hi naḥ pitā","2 sg. nom. + emph. + 1 pl. dat. + m. nom. sg.","‘you indeed (are) our father’"],["yaḥ asmākam avidyāyāḥ paraṃ pāraṃ tārayasi","rel. + 1 pl. gen. + f. abl. sg. + m. acc. sg. + m. acc. sg. + 2 sg. pres.","‘who carry us beyond the further shore of (our) ignorance’"],["iti","indecl.","(thus they said)"],["namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ","m. dat. pl. (×2)","salutation to the supreme ṛṣis"]],"lit":"“They, worshipping him, said: ‘You indeed are our father, who carries us beyond the further shore of our ignorance.’ Salutation to the supreme ṛṣis! Salutation to the supreme ṛṣis!”","para":"The closing salutation. The six, having received the full teaching, address Pippalāda as their father — the one who carries them across the further shore of ignorance (avidyāyāḥ paraṃ pāraṃ). The Upaniṣad ends with the formulaic double salutation to the parama-ṛṣis: salutations, salutations.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The image of the teacher as the one who carries the disciple across the ocean of avidyā (avidyāyāḥ paraṃ pāraṃ) is older than the Praśna; cf. Muṇḍaka 1.2.7. The Praśna invokes the same image as its closing seal. ‘pitā’ — father — is not biological but spiritual paternity, the canonical Upaniṣadic teacher-disciple relation."],["Sharvananda","The double salutation ‘namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyo namaḥ parama-ṛṣibhyaḥ’ is plural — to the supreme ṛṣis in general, not to Pippalāda alone. The Upaniṣad closes by extending the teacher-honour to the whole lineage of supreme ṛṣis that Pippalāda represents."],["Sivananda","‘avidyāyāḥ paraṃ pāraṃ tārayasi’ — ‘carry us across the further shore of avidyā’ — is the daily prayer to the guru. Sivananda used this phrase as the closing of his daily satsaṅga."],["Krishnananda","Note the construction ‘paraṃ pāraṃ’ — not just ‘the other shore’ but ‘the further-than-the-other shore’. Pippalāda has carried them not merely beyond ignorance but to a station beyond what ignorance could even conceive as ‘beyond’."],["Deussen","The closing double salutation is the canonical Upaniṣadic colophon-formula. The Praśna ends in the same register as the Muṇḍaka and the Māṇḍūkya — its two sibling Atharvan Upaniṣads."]],"notes":["End of Praśna 6, and end of the Praśna Upaniṣad."]},{"ref":"Visual Representations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Praśna's architecture is unusually amenable to visualisation. Six diagrams below abstract its principal doctrines: (1) the six-questioner frame; (2) the cosmogonic descent of Praśna 1; (3) the body-as-city of Praśna 2 with its nine devas + Prāṇa; (4) the five-fold prāṇa-map of Praśna 3; (5) the three-state architecture of Praśna 4; (6) the mātrā-fruit grid of Praśna 5; and (7) the sixteen-kalā emanation of Praśna 6 with the rivers-into-ocean closing."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1. The six praśnas as an ascent of interiority","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Order in which the six are introduced (most senior → most junior, in the opening frame) is the reverse of the order in which they are answered (most junior first). The progression of the answers is from cosmos to interior to source.","<pre>  Questioner               Question                       Domain                    Direction\n  ─────────────────────    ────────────────────────       ────────────────────      ──────────\n  1. Kabandhī Kātyāyana    Whence creatures?               cosmogony / rayi-prāṇa     outer\n  2. Bhārgava Vaidarbhi    Which deva sustains?            body / supremacy of prāṇa  inward\n  3. Kausalya Āśvalāyana   Whence prāṇa? how divided?     prāṇa-anatomy              deeper\n  4. Sauryāyaṇī Gārgya     What sleeps? what wakes?        states of consciousness    deeper\n  5. Satyakāma Śaibya      Oṃ-meditation fruit?            soteriology (krama-mukti)  source-ward\n  6. Sukeśā Bhāradvāja     Where is the 16-kalā Puruṣa?    direct identity / sadyo-m. innermost</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. The cosmogonic descent of Praśna 1","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                         PRAJĀPATI (desiring progeny, performs tapas)\n                                          │\n                          ┌───────────────┴───────────────┐\n                          │                                 │\n                       PRĀṆA                              RAYI\n                    (formless / sun)                   (formed / moon)\n                          │                                 │\n                          └────────────── YEAR (Prajāpati) ──┘\n                                          │\n                           ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐\n                           │                              │\n                    UTTARĀYAṆA                      DAKṢIṆĀYANA\n                  (deva-yāna, prāṇa-side)       (pitṛ-yāṇa, rayi-side)\n                  no return; brahmaloka          cāndramasa-loka; return\n                           │                              │\n                           └────────────── MONTH ──────────┘\n                                          │\n                           ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐\n                           │                              │\n                    ŚUKLA-PAKṢA                    KṚṢṆA-PAKṢA\n                       (prāṇa)                        (rayi)\n                                          │\n                                        DAY-AND-NIGHT\n                                          │\n                           ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐\n                        AHAS (prāṇa)             RĀTRI (rayi)\n                                          │\n                                         FOOD (annam, Prajāpati)\n                                          │\n                                       SEMEN (retas)\n                                          │\n                                       CREATURES (prajāḥ)</pre>","Read top-down: each level is Prajāpati split into a prāṇa-aspect and a rayi-aspect; from the lowest split (food / semen) come creatures. To trace the chain in reverse is to ascend from one's body back to Prajāpati."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. The body-as-city in Praśna 2: nine devas and the king","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n                │                THE CITY OF THE BODY                  │\n                │                                                       │\n                │           five elemental devas:                       │\n                │           ākāśa · vāyu · agni · āpas · pṛthivī        │\n                │                                                       │\n                │           four sense-faculties:                       │\n                │           vāk · manas · cakṣus · śrotra               │\n                │                                                       │\n                │              ╔══════════════════════╗                 │\n                │              ║       PRĀṆA          ║                 │\n                │              ║    (variṣṭha, king)  ║                 │\n                │              ╚══════════════════════╝                 │\n                │              (when he stirs to leave,                 │\n                │               all nine stir; when he                  │\n                │               settles, all settle — as                │\n                │               bees with their king-bee)               │\n                └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The five-fold prāṇa-map of Praśna 3","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Sub-prāṇa</td><td>Bodily station</td><td>Cosmic correlate</td><td>Function</td></tr><tr><td>Prāṇa (proper)</td><td>Upper openings: eyes, ears, mouth, nose</td><td>Sun (ādityaḥ)</td><td>Inhalation, sensory access</td></tr><tr><td>Apāna</td><td>Lower openings: anus, genitals</td><td>Earth-deity</td><td>Excretory + downward holding</td></tr><tr><td>Samāna</td><td>Middle (around navel)</td><td>Mid-region ākāśa</td><td>Equalises in-and-out breath; distributes food</td></tr><tr><td>Vyāna</td><td>All-over: through 101 × 100 × 72,000 nāḍīs</td><td>Wind (vāyu)</td><td>Pervasive circulation</td></tr><tr><td>Udāna</td><td>Upward path; the 101st nāḍī (suṣumnā)</td><td>Tejas (fiery upward)</td><td>Departure at death; leads soul</td></tr></table>","Diagrammatic body-map (head at top, feet at bottom):","<pre>                  ┌────────────────────┐\n                  │   eye · ear        │\n                  │   mouth · nose     │  ◀──  PRĀṆA (upper)\n                  ├────────────────────┤\n                  │                    │  ◀──  UDĀNA (upward,\n                  │       throat       │           via suṣumnā)\n                  │                    │\n                  │ ┌────────────────┐ │\n                  │ │     heart      │ │  ◀──  ĀTMAN (101 nāḍīs origin)\n                  │ │  101 nāḍīs ↘   │ │\n                  │ │   (each →100)  │ │  ◀──  VYĀNA (in network)\n                  │ └────────────────┘ │\n                  │                    │\n                  │      navel         │  ◀──  SAMĀNA (middle)\n                  │   (jāṭhara-agni)   │\n                  ├────────────────────┤\n                  │  anus · genitals   │  ◀──  APĀNA (lower)\n                  └────────────────────┘</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"5. The three-state architecture of Praśna 4","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>State</td><td>Character</td><td>Simile</td><td>Praśna 4 reference</td></tr><tr><td>Jāgrat (waking)</td><td>Indriyas active outward</td><td>Sun's rays spread out</td><td>Mantra 4.2 (negation)</td></tr><tr><td>Svapna (dream)</td><td>Indriyas withdrawn into manas; manas operates on saṃskāras</td><td>Sun setting — rays gathered into disk</td><td>Mantras 4.2 (gathering) and 4.5 (re-experience)</td></tr><tr><td>Suṣupti (deep sleep)</td><td>Manas overpowered by tejas (= prāṇa-light returning home); ‘daily Brahman-touch’</td><td>Birds returned to roosting tree</td><td>Mantras 4.6 (no dreams; sukha) and 4.7 (bird simile)</td></tr><tr><td>(Tūrīya — implicit)</td><td>The witness of all three; the supreme akṣara of 4.10–4.11</td><td>—</td><td>Mantras 4.9–4.11; later Māṇḍūkya tūrīya</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"6. The mātrā-fruit grid of Praśna 5","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Meditation depth</td><td>Vedic correlate</td><td>Post-mortem destination</td><td>Return?</td></tr><tr><td>1 mātrā (a)</td><td>Ṛg-Veda</td><td>Manuṣya-loka (with greater tapas/brahmacarya)</td><td>Return (quick)</td></tr><tr><td>2 mātrās (a + u)</td><td>Yajur-Veda</td><td>Antarikṣa = soma-loka (vibhūti)</td><td>Return (delayed)</td></tr><tr><td>3 mātrās (a + u + m, analysed)</td><td>Sāma-Veda</td><td>Brahma-loka (jīva-ghana); vision of supreme Puruṣa</td><td>Krama-mukti (no further return after cycle)</td></tr><tr><td>3 mātrās joined (anyonya-saktāḥ, un-separated)</td><td>Beyond the three Vedas: Oṃ alone</td><td>śānta · ajara · amṛta · abhaya · param</td><td>Sadyo-mukti (no return)</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"7. The sixteen-kalā emanation and the rivers-into-ocean","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>      THE PURUṢA  (ihaivāntaḥ-śarīre — right here, in this body)\n          │\n          │ (asks: in whose departure am I departed?)\n          │ (creates Prāṇa — the first kalā)\n          ▼\n       1. PRĀṆA\n          │\n          ▼\n       2. ŚRADDHĀ (faith)\n          │\n          ├─→ 3. KHAṂ (ākāśa)\n          ├─→ 4. VĀYU (wind)\n          ├─→ 5. JYOTIḤ (fire)\n          ├─→ 6. ĀPAḤ (water)\n          └─→ 7. PṚTHIVĪ (earth)\n          │\n          ├─→ 8. INDRIYA (the senses, collectively)\n          └─→ 9. MANAS (mind)\n          │\n          ▼\n      10. ANNA (food)\n          │\n          ├─→ 11. VĪRYA (vigour)\n          ├─→ 12. TAPAS\n          ├─→ 13. MANTRĀḤ (mantras)\n          ├─→ 14. KARMA (action)\n          └─→ 15. LOKĀḤ (worlds)\n          │\n          ▼\n       16. NĀMA (name)\n\n      And: rivers flowing — names dissolved — ‘ocean’ alone remains.\n      So: 16 kalās of the seer — names dissolved — ‘Puruṣa’ alone remains.\n      He becomes akala (part-less), amṛta (immortal).</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical Steps and Observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["What follows are practices derived directly from the six praśnas, presented as the Praśna's own sādhanā-curriculum. Each section names the verses from which the practice is derived. The four core Praśna-disciplines — tapas, brahmacarya, śraddhā, vidyā (1.10) — are not separate practices but the permeating quality that all the practices below presuppose."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"A. From Praśna 1","title":"Cosmological orientation","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Daily āditya-darśana (1.6–1.8). At sunrise, face east and attend to the rising sun as bāhya prāṇa. The sun is gathering, into its rays, the lives of all creatures facing east. Place your own breath in the same act. Three minutes is sufficient; the practice is recognition, not duration.","Two-path discrimination (1.9–1.10). Once each evening, review the day's actions and divide them into iṣṭā-pūrta (works of ritual + public welfare → rayi, dakṣiṇāyana, return) and tapas-brahmacarya-śraddhā-vidyā (uttarāyaṇa → no return). The point is not to renounce iṣṭā-pūrta but to know to which kind a given action belongs — and so to keep at least some action on the no-return path each day.","Tri-negation examen (1.16). Before sleep, ask: was there today (a) jihmam — crookedness in conduct? (b) anṛtam — falsehood in speech? (c) māyā — deceit in mind? If yes, acknowledge and resolve. The ‘brahma-world virajas’ is reserved for those in whom all three negatives stand."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"B. From Praśna 2","title":"Body-as-city; supremacy of Prāṇa","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The bee-king meditation (2.4). At the start of pratyāhāra: feel the senses as a swarm of bees. Imagine Prāṇa as the king-bee preparing to stir. Stay with the imminent stirring; the senses settle of their own accord.","Hymn-recitation as identification (2.5–2.13). Recite mantras 2.5–2.13 (or just 2.13) as a daily prāṇa-hymn. Identify each cosmic name (Agni, Sūrya, Parjanya, Indra, Vāyu) successively with the breath. The mantra is not external praise; it is the breath's own recognition of itself as cosmic Prāṇa.","‘mā utkramīḥ’ at session-start (2.12). At the start of each prāṇāyāma session, recite mantra 2.12: ‘yā te tanūr vāci pratiṣṭhitā … śivāṃ tāṃ kuru mā utkramīḥ’ — ‘the form of yours in speech, ear, eye, mind: make that auspicious; do not depart.’ The session itself is the holding-in-place that the petition asks."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"C. From Praśna 3","title":"Prāṇa-anatomy and end-of-life preparation","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Five-prāṇa scan (3.5). Sit; trace attention through the five stations in order: apāna (lower openings), samāna (navel), prāṇa-proper (eye / ear / mouth / nostrils), vyāna (whole body via the nāḍī-network), udāna (upward axis from heart through crown). Spend equal time at each. The five-fold scan is the Praśna's complete prāṇa-anatomy in five breaths.","Body-as-yajña recitation (3.5 read with 4.3). Treat each meal as the homa described in 3.5: food offered into the body-fire is distributed by samāna; the seven openings of the head are the seven flames. Eat deliberately, with one full breath of acknowledgement between bites — explicit if needed, silent if not.","Antakāla-citta practice (3.10). Daily, just before sleep, take up the verse ‘yat-cittas tena eṣa prāṇam āyāti, prāṇas tejasā yuktaḥ saha ātmanā yathā-saṅkalpitaṃ lokaṃ nayati’. Form a clear saṅkalpa for liberation; sleep with the saṅkalpa as the last thought. Over years, this becomes the saṅkalpa available at the moment of death."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"D. From Praśna 4","title":"Conscious sleep","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Sunset-of-the-senses (4.2). At the moment of falling asleep — usually missed — observe the gathering of the ten faculties (na śṛṇoti … na iyāyate) into the higher deva, manas. The rays-into-disk simile of the setting sun is the daily image.","Dream-as-projection (4.5). On waking from a dream, name the categories applicable to its content: dṛṣṭam (seen-again), adṛṣṭam (never-seen), śrutam-aśrutam, sat-asat. The naming dissolves the dream's claim to a separate world; it returns the dream to the dreamer.","Suṣupti-sukha witness (4.6). At the moment of slipping into dreamless sleep, hold a thin thread of attention on the arising sukha. This is the daily glimpse of the natural ānanda of the ātman; cultivated, it becomes the seed of jīvan-mukti.","Bird-and-tree contemplation (4.7). Evening practice: as faculties return home for the night, observe them as birds returning to their roosting-tree. The tree is the supreme Ātman. Each thought, each sensation, comes back to its branch."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"E. From Praśna 5","title":"Oṃ-meditation","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Three-mātrā Oṃ (5.5). Pronounce Oṃ slowly: ‘a’ from the navel, ‘u’ from the throat, ‘m’ from the crown. Three equal mātrās; let the resonance carry from one to the next without break. Twenty-one repetitions, morning and evening.","Joined-mātrā Oṃ (5.6). Once the three-mātrā analytic recitation is steady, collapse it: pronounce Oṃ as a single continuous vibration in which the three are anyonya-saktāḥ (mutually clinging), anaviprayuktāḥ (un-separated). This is the supreme Oṃ-meditation of the Praśna.","Triple-action Oṃ (5.6). Apply the joined Oṃ to all three classes of action: bāhya (outer / ritual / speech), ābhyantara (inner / mental), madhyama (middle / prāṇic). Recite Oṃ before each. The mature practice is to feel the joined Oṃ as the resonant ground of every act."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"F. From Praśna 6","title":"The Puruṣa within","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["‘Ihaivāntaḥ-śarīre’ contemplation (6.2). When the search-mind reaches outward for the divine, recall: ‘right here, within this body.’ Do not move; turn the searching inward. The Puruṣa is not in the place you are looking; he is in the looker.","Sixteen-kalā inventory (6.4). Once each week, conduct a sixteen-kalā inventory of yourself: my prāṇa, my śraddhā, my five elements, my senses, my mind, my food, my vigour, my tapas, my mantras, my karman, my world, my name. Each is a kalā. The inventory itself is the contemplation of their dependence on the Puruṣa.","Rivers-into-ocean visualisation (6.5). In meditation, visualise the sixteen kalās as sixteen rivers, each flowing toward the ocean of the Puruṣa. Watch them reach the ocean. Watch their names dissolve. Hold the ocean-only state for as long as natural; do not force it.","Hub-and-spokes contemplation (6.6, with 2.6). Visualise the body as a wheel: the kalās as spokes, the heart-Puruṣa as the hub. The spokes have no separate existence; they are spokes only by virtue of the hub. So you. Recite 6.6 at the close: ‘taṃ vedyaṃ puruṣaṃ veda yathā mā vo mṛtyuḥ parivyathāḥ’."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"G. Across the Upaniṣad: the four permeating disciplines","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Praśna names four disciplines at 1.10, again at 1.15, and effectively at the close of every praśna. These are the permeating qualities that every specific practice above must rest on:","<table><tr><td>Discipline</td><td>Operative meaning in the Praśna</td></tr><tr><td>tapas</td><td>Sustained voluntary austerity / heating — chosen restriction (of food, speech, comfort) as the engine of clarity. Without tapas, no other practice has traction.</td></tr><tr><td>brahmacarya</td><td>Continence of vital energy — bodily, verbal, mental. Not mere abstinence but the redirection of vīrya toward the inquiry. Both 1.10 and 1.13 specify.</td></tr><tr><td>śraddhā</td><td>Confidence in the teaching and the teacher — operative trust, not mere belief. The second of Praśna 6's sixteen kalās: śraddhā is structurally just beneath prāṇa itself.</td></tr><tr><td>vidyā</td><td>Direct knowledge — not information about, but identification with. The fruit of the other three; the condition of the deva-yāna path.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-References in the Upaniṣadic Corpus","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Praśna sits in continuous conversation with the rest of the Upaniṣadic corpus. The cross-references below trace the Praśna's principal doctrines through their parallels and successors elsewhere."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"A. Praśna ↔ Atharvan triad (Muṇḍaka, Māṇḍūkya)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Praśna passage</td><td>Parallel</td><td>Comment</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 1.9–1.10 (two paths)</td><td>Muṇḍaka 1.2.7–11 (ritual path returns) ; Muṇḍaka 3.2.6 (deva-yāna)</td><td>Both Atharvan texts develop the dual-path doctrine; the Praśna is the more compressed.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 4.2–4.11 (three-state)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya 3–7 (jāgrat / svapna / suṣupti / tūrīya)</td><td>The Praśna gives three states; the Māṇḍūkya adds tūrīya. The Praśna's vijñāna-ātmā (4.9) ≈ Māṇḍūkya's prājña (5).</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 5.2–5.7 (Oṃ as paraṃ and aparam)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya 1, 8–12 (Oṃ = all; Oṃ's three+ mātrās)</td><td>The Praśna's three-mātrā fruits anticipate the Māṇḍūkya's three-mātrā state-correlations; the Māṇḍūkya adds the amātra fourth.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 6.4 (16 kalās from Prāṇa)</td><td>Muṇḍaka 2.1.2–9 (cosmogonic emanation from Puruṣa)</td><td>Both Atharvan texts present cosmogony as emanation from a personal Puruṣa, but the Praśna's enumeration is the more systematic.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 6.5 (rivers-into-ocean)</td><td>Muṇḍaka 3.2.8 (rivers-into-ocean almost verbatim)</td><td>Verbal echoes are exact. Either the Praśna draws from the Muṇḍaka or both share an Atharvan source.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"B. Praśna ↔ Bṛhadāraṇyaka / Chāndogya","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Praśna passage</td><td>Parallel</td><td>Comment</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 2 (prāṇa-vivāda; bee-king)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 6.1; Chāndogya 5.1 (prāṇa-saṃvāda)</td><td>The older Bṛh./Chānd. prāṇa-disputes are the Praśna's source-tradition; the Praśna's bee-king simile is its distinct addition.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 1.5 (sun = prāṇa; moon = rayi)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.3 (mūrtam-amūrtam)</td><td>The Praśna's rayi-prāṇa pair is the Atharvan version of the Bṛh.'s mūrta-amūrta pair.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 3.5–3.6 (five prāṇas; 101 nāḍīs)</td><td>Chāndogya 5.19–24 (prāṇāgnihotra); Chāndogya 8.6.6 (101 nāḍīs); Bṛh. 1.5.3 (3 prāṇas only)</td><td>The Praśna systematises an older five-prāṇa tradition. The 101-nāḍī number is shared with Chānd. 8.6.6 and Kaṭha 6.16.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 3.10 (yat-cittas tena prāṇam āyāti)</td><td>Bṛh. 4.4.5–7 (kāma-saṅkalpa determines rebirth); Gītā 8.5–6</td><td>The Praśna's antakāla-citta is one of the earliest formulations; the Gītā's near-verbatim adoption is the most famous later use.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 4.7 (birds-into-tree)</td><td>Bṛh. 4.3.18 (bird-and-tree applied to suṣupti)</td><td>Both texts use the simile; the Praśna applies it more broadly (‘all this rests in the supreme ātman’) than the Bṛh.'s suṣupti-specific use.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 4.10 (acchāya, aśarīra, alohita, śubhra, akṣara)</td><td>Bṛh. 3.8.8 (neti-neti); Bṛh. 4.4.20</td><td>Praśna belongs to the via-negativa tradition; its five-negative chain is its precise formulation.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"C. Praśna ↔ Kṛṣṇa-Yajur Upaniṣads (Kaṭha, Taittirīya, Śvetāśvatara)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Praśna passage</td><td>Parallel</td><td>Comment</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 1.14 (annaṃ vai prajāpatiḥ)</td><td>Taittirīya 2.1 (annāt bhūtāni jāyante)</td><td>The Praśna identifies food with Prajāpati; the Taittirīya gives the same doctrine in a fuller five-kośa framework.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 3.6 (heart, 101 nāḍīs)</td><td>Kaṭha 6.16 (śatam caikā ca hṛdayasya nāḍyaḥ)</td><td>Praśna's 101 is the same as Kaṭha's 101; the Kaṭha specifies that one (the 101st = suṣumnā) leads upward.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 6.2 (ihaivāntaḥ-śarīre … puruṣaḥ)</td><td>Kaṭha 4.12 (aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣaḥ); Śvet. 3.13 (aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣo madhya ātmani tiṣṭhati)</td><td>Praśna and Kaṭha share the doctrine of the Puruṣa within the body. Praśna's contribution is the sixteen-kalā emanation.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 1.10 (tapas-brahmacarya-śraddhā-vidyā)</td><td>Muṇḍaka 1.2.11; Taittirīya 1.9 (śraddhāyā satyaṃ tapaḥ)</td><td>The four-fold discipline is the standard late-Upaniṣadic shortlist for the deva-yāna path.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"D. Praśna ↔ Later Vedānta and Yoga","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Praśna passage</td><td>Later development</td><td>Comment</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 3.5 (five-prāṇa physiology)</td><td>Maitrāyaṇī 6.20–30 (five-prāṇa elaborated); Yoga-sūtra 2.49–53 (prāṇāyāma); Haṭha-yoga-pradīpikā 3</td><td>The Praśna's five-prāṇa is the source-doctrine for all later Yogic prāṇa-physiology.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 4 (three states + witness)</td><td>Gauḍapāda Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā (esp. ch. 1–2)</td><td>Gauḍapāda's analysis of the three states is structurally dependent on the Praśna's earlier formulation.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 5 (Oṃ as paraṃ and aparam)</td><td>Brahmasūtra 1.3.13 (oṃkārādhikaraṇam); Śaṅkara-bhāṣya thereon</td><td>Cited as the principal Upaniṣadic basis for the doctrine that Oṃ-meditation can yield either saguṇa or nirguṇa fruit.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 6.4 (16 kalās)</td><td>Pāñcarātra and Śaiva-Tantra ‘sixteen-kalā’ doctrines</td><td>The cosmological 16 of the Praśna is the scriptural prius of the later soteriological / Tantric 16; the lunar-digit reading is post-Upaniṣadic.</td></tr><tr><td>Praśna 6.5 (rivers-into-ocean = nāma-rūpa-bhidyate)</td><td>Brahmasūtra 4.4 (sarva-mukti); Śaṅkara on Chāndogya 6.8</td><td>The Praśna's explicit nāma-rūpa-bhidyate is the Upaniṣadic basis for the dissolution-of-name-and-form doctrine in classical Vedānta.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Primary Sanskrit text and editions","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Praśnopaniṣad (encoded text). sanskritdocuments.org, file praznopanishhad.itx. The Śaṅkara-bhāṣya recension; the basis of the Sanskrit text quoted in this volume.","Limaye, V. P. &amp; Vadekar, R. D., eds. Eighteen Principal Upaniṣads. Vol. 1. Vaidika Saṃśodhana Maṇḍala, Poona, 1958. Critical Devanāgarī text with apparatus.","Olivelle, Patrick. The Early Upaniṣads: Annotated Text and Translation. Oxford University Press, 1998. Critical Sanskrit text and translation; the standard modern Indological edition."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five running commentaries (in the order used)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Swami Krishnananda. The Praśna Upaniṣad","A Series of Lectures. Divine Life Society, Rishikesh, 1981 (also published online at swami-krishnananda.org). The philosophical-Advaita reading."]],"notes":["Swami Gambhirananda, tr. Eight Upaniṣads with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, Volume Two. Advaita Ashrama, Calcutta, 1958. Praśna Upaniṣad with Śaṅkara's bhāṣya and Anandagiri's ṭīkā. The classical Advaitin reading.","Swami Sharvananda, tr. Praśnopaniṣad. Sri Ramakrishna Math, Madras, 1923 (and many reprints). Standard Ramakrishna-Mission translation with commentary. Particularly strong on the prāṇa-physiology of Praśnas 2 and 3.","Swami Sivananda. The Principal Upanishads. Divine Life Society, Rishikesh, 1937. Plus the Praśna lectures recorded in The Yoga-Vedanta Forest Academy notes. The practical-sādhanā Advaita reading.","Paul Deussen. Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda. F. A. Brockhaus, Leipzig, 1897. English translation by V. M. Bedekar and G. B. Palsule, Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda, 2 vols., Motilal Banarsidass, Delhi, 1980. The Indological-historical reading."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Other classical and modern commentaries (cited inline)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Anandagiri. Praśnopaniṣad-Śāṅkara-bhāṣya-ṭīkā. Sub-commentary on Śaṅkara, carried in Gambhirananda 1958 above.","Rangaramanuja. Praśnopaniṣad-bhāṣya. Viśiṣṭādvaita commentary; consulted only where it diverges sharply from Śaṅkara.","Swami Nikhilananda, tr. The Upanishads, Vol. I. Harper &amp; Brothers, New York, 1949. (Includes Praśna.) A second Ramakrishna-Mission translation, more recent than Sharvananda.","Robert Ernest Hume. The Thirteen Principal Upanishads. Oxford University Press, 1921 (rev. 1931). The standard older English translation; cited for points of conservative philology.","S. Radhakrishnan. The Principal Upaniṣads. George Allen &amp; Unwin, London, 1953. Includes Praśna with comparative-philosophical notes; cited where his cross-references are illuminating."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-textual and background references","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Bṛhadāraṇyaka, Chāndogya, Taittirīya, Kaṭha, Muṇḍaka, Māṇḍūkya — Devanāgarī texts at sanskritdocuments.org; translations in Olivelle 1998, Radhakrishnan 1953, and Gambhirananda's Eight Upaniṣads volumes.","Gauḍapāda. Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā. Trans. Swami Nikhilananda, Advaita Ashrama 1936. Indispensable for the relation between Praśna 4 and the later four-state analysis.","Atharva-Veda 10.7 (the skambha-sūkta); 11.4 (the prāṇa-sūkta); 13.2 (the rohita-sūkta); 15 (the vrātya-book). Trans. W. D. Whitney, Harvard Oriental Series 7–8, 1905. The Atharvan ground from which the Praśna grows.","Brahmasūtras, with Śaṅkara-bhāṣya. Trans. Swami Gambhirananda, Brahma-Sūtra-Bhāṣya of Śaṅkarācārya, Advaita Ashrama, 1965. The Praśna is one of the principal Upaniṣadic sources for the prāṇa- and Oṃ-adhikaraṇas.","Bhagavad-Gītā, chapters 8 (antakāla-yoga) and 15 (puruṣottama-yoga). Praśna 3.10 and Praśna 6 are the Upaniṣadic foundations of these two Gītā chapters."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Indological studies","title":"","sa":["ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Patrick Olivelle. The Early Upaniṣads (Oxford, 1998); see esp. the introduction and the apparatus on Praśna.","Joel Brereton. The Significance of the Praśna Upaniṣad. Journal of the American Oriental Society, various papers from the 1990s onward, on the prāṇa- and three-state doctrines.","Jan Gonda. Vedic Cosmogony and the Atharvan Texts. Mouton, The Hague, 1965. Especially the chapter on rayi-prāṇa, which contextualises Praśna 1.","Asko Parpola. The Pre-Vedic Indian Background of the Śrauta Ritual. Various papers; useful for the agnihotra-physiology of Praśna 4.3.","End of the Praśna Upaniṣad — Complete Working Edition with Five Commentaries, Visual Representations, and Practical Steps."]}]}]};
